(window.COURSE_MODULES = window.COURSE_MODULES || []).push({"id":"M1","code":"Module 1","title":"Visual and Interactive Design","overview":"<p>Module 1 turns you from a consumer of digital media into a designer and producer. You will learn the pre-production process that every professional product starts with (problem definition, brainstorming, sketches, storyboards, Gantt charts and prototype testing), then master the elements of visual design, colour theory, harmony rules and the rules of composition, together with the five principles of interactive design. You will critique indigenous Caribbean digital products, choose the right platforms and file formats, produce audio, video and image work, animate in 2D and 3D, collaborate with digital tools, plan a promotional campaign, track design trends and write a project proposal with a budget and a pitch. This module follows the ten sections of the DME2 course outline.</p>\n<p>In CAPE Unit 2, <b>Paper 01</b> has 45 multiple-choice items, 15 from each module, worth 20% (1 hour 30 minutes). <b>Paper 02</b> has three compulsory questions, one per module, each worth 15 marks, together worth 20% (2 hours 15 minutes, no internet). Expect practical tasks such as planning, sketching, editing and exporting files, alongside structured and essay parts. The <b>School-Based Assessment (Paper 031)</b> is worth 60%: in groups of 2 to 3 you design a product or service, preferably for a local social or entrepreneurial need, and implement it with digital tools. It is marked out of 45 (weighted to 90): <b>Proposal 14</b>, <b>Progress and Process 12</b> and <b>Product 19</b>, which includes a <b>30-second audio-visual short ad</b> worth 5 marks. The report must not exceed 1,500 words. Module 1 gives you the planning, design, animation, campaign and proposal skills that your whole SBA depends on.</p>\n<p>Work through the units in order. Read each learn screen, try the activities (use the easier version if you need a first step), complete the quick check-in honestly, and write the short task at the end of each unit. Keep a sketchbook or digital folder of your mood boards, colour schemes, storyboards and animation tests: this becomes evidence for your SBA progress and process marks. Finish with the Module 1 Test (pass mark 70%) and the Paper 02 practice question.</p>","objectives":["By the end you will be able to apply design principles to the creation of a digital media product through structured pre-production planning, from problem definition and brainstorming to storyboards, Gantt charts, prototype validation and implementation.","By the end you will be able to create design solutions grounded in the elements of visual design, colour theory and harmony rules, rules of composition and the principles of interactive design.","By the end you will be able to critique the impact and motivations for indigenous Caribbean digital products through case study analysis, screening and critical observation.","By the end you will be able to select and apply appropriate digital media platforms and file formats (MP4, MP3, PDF, PNG, JPEG, GIF) for a given purpose and audience, including importing, exporting, sharing and embedding.","By the end you will be able to integrate basic image manipulation, sound editing, video capture, lighting, editing and export principles into digital media designs.","By the end you will be able to apply the basics of 2D and 3D animation, including keyframing, timing, spacing, squash and stretch, interpolation and a basic walk cycle.","By the end you will be able to use interactive digital media tools and electronic communities effectively for collaboration and self-directed activities, observing netiquette.","By the end you will be able to apply current digital media industry practices to plan a promotional campaign and measure its effectiveness with analytics and KPIs.","By the end you will be able to discuss the factors that influence trends and developments in visual and interactive design and use research tools to track them.","By the end you will be able to outline the basic considerations for developing a project proposal, including writing, sources of funding, budgeting and pitching."],"glossary":[{"term":"Problem definition","def":"The first pre-production step: a clear statement of the challenge, purpose and goal the digital media product must address."},{"term":"Target audience","def":"The specific group of people a product is designed for, described by needs, demographics and behaviours."},{"term":"Mind map","def":"A brainstorming diagram that branches related ideas out from one central topic."},{"term":"SCAMPER","def":"A brainstorming technique that improves an existing idea by asking how to Substitute, Combine, Adapt, Modify, Put to another use, Eliminate or Reverse it."},{"term":"Mood board","def":"A collage of images, colours, textures and fonts that sets the look and feel of a project."},{"term":"Style guide","def":"A document of fixed rules (logo use, fonts, hex colour codes, spacing) that keeps every piece of a project consistent."},{"term":"Storyboard","def":"A sequence of sketched frames with notes that plans the shots of a video, animation or interactive product."},{"term":"Gantt chart","def":"A bar chart that shows project tasks against time, with durations, overlaps and milestones."},{"term":"Milestone","def":"A key checkpoint in a production schedule, such as 'storyboard approved' or 'final cut delivered'."},{"term":"Prototype","def":"An early, testable model of a product used to check an idea before full production."},{"term":"Idea validation","def":"Testing a concept or prototype with real target users to confirm it meets their needs."},{"term":"Elements of visual design","def":"The basic building blocks of any design: line, shape, form, space, texture, value and colour."},{"term":"Value","def":"The lightness or darkness of a colour or tone."},{"term":"Primary colours","def":"Colours that cannot be made by mixing others; red, yellow and blue on the traditional colour wheel (red, green and blue for screens)."},{"term":"Tertiary colours","def":"Colours made by mixing a primary colour with a neighbouring secondary colour, such as red-orange."},{"term":"Complementary scheme","def":"A harmony rule that pairs two colours directly opposite each other on the colour wheel for maximum contrast."},{"term":"Analogous scheme","def":"A harmony rule using three to five colours that sit side by side on the colour wheel for a calm, unified look."},{"term":"Triadic scheme","def":"A harmony rule using three colours evenly spaced (120 degrees apart) around the colour wheel."},{"term":"Split-complementary scheme","def":"A harmony rule that pairs a base colour with the two colours either side of its complement."},{"term":"Tetradic scheme","def":"A harmony rule using four colours made of two complementary pairs."},{"term":"Tint","def":"A hue lightened by adding white."},{"term":"Shade","def":"A hue darkened by adding black."},{"term":"Tone","def":"A hue muted by adding grey."},{"term":"Rule of thirds","def":"A composition guide that divides the frame into a three-by-three grid and places key subjects on the lines or intersections."},{"term":"Golden ratio","def":"A proportion of about 1 to 1.618, used in the golden rectangle and golden section to create naturally pleasing layouts."},{"term":"Point of interest","def":"The part of a design or frame that the viewer's eye is drawn to first; the most prominent subject."},{"term":"Negative space","def":"The empty or white space around and between elements, which gives a design room to breathe."},{"term":"Proximity","def":"Placing related elements close together so viewers read them as a group."},{"term":"Perceivability","def":"An interactive design principle: users can easily see and recognise what can be clicked, tapped or used."},{"term":"Learnability","def":"An interactive design principle: users can quickly learn how to use an interface."},{"term":"Predictability","def":"An interactive design principle: users can correctly expect what will happen before they act."},{"term":"Feedback (interactive design)","def":"An interactive design principle: the system responds to every user action with a visible or audible signal."},{"term":"Indigenous digital product","def":"Digital content created by local or regional creators that reflects their own culture, identity and stories."},{"term":"Cultural authenticity","def":"Representing a culture accurately and respectfully, ideally with input from people who belong to it."},{"term":"Knowledge platform","def":"A platform built to collect and share information, such as Wikipedia or Quora."},{"term":"Crowd-sourcing platform","def":"A platform that gathers ideas, work or funds from a large online crowd, such as GoFundMe or Kickstarter."},{"term":"Infrastructure platform","def":"A platform that provides the underlying computing services other products are built on, such as cloud hosting."},{"term":"Lossy compression","def":"Compression that permanently removes some data to shrink files, as in JPEG and MP3."},{"term":"Lossless compression","def":"Compression that shrinks files without losing any data, as in PNG."},{"term":"Embedding","def":"Placing content from another source, such as a YouTube video or Google Map, so it displays inside your own page."},{"term":"Cross-platform compatibility","def":"The ability of a file or product to work correctly on different devices, browsers and operating systems."},{"term":"Three-point lighting","def":"A standard lighting set-up using a key light, a fill light and a back light."},{"term":"Colour grading","def":"Adjusting the colours and mood of video footage during editing."},{"term":"Keyframe","def":"A frame that marks the start or end of a movement or change in an animation."},{"term":"Interpolation (tweening)","def":"The automatic generation of in-between frames by software between two keyframes."},{"term":"Squash and stretch","def":"An animation principle that deforms an object to show weight, flexibility and impact."},{"term":"Spacing","def":"How far an object moves between frames; close spacing reads as slow and wide spacing as fast."},{"term":"Walk cycle","def":"A looping series of poses (contact, down, passing, up) that makes a character appear to walk."},{"term":"Vertex, edge and face","def":"The parts of a 3D mesh: a point, a line joining two points, and a flat surface enclosed by edges."},{"term":"Graph editor","def":"A tool in software such as Blender that shows animation curves so you can adjust easing and timing."},{"term":"Electronic community","def":"An online group, forum or wiki where people with shared interests communicate and collaborate."},{"term":"Netiquette","def":"The rules of polite, professional behaviour in online communication."},{"term":"Guerrilla marketing","def":"Unconventional, low-cost, high-impact promotion designed to surprise people in public spaces."},{"term":"Viral marketing","def":"Content designed to be shared rapidly from person to person for organic reach."},{"term":"Key performance indicator (KPI)","def":"A measurable value, such as click-through rate, used to judge whether a campaign meets its goals."},{"term":"Commoditisation","def":"The process by which technology becomes standardised, widely available and cheap."},{"term":"Glassmorphism","def":"A UI trend using frosted, semi-transparent panels over blurred backgrounds."},{"term":"Executive summary","def":"The short opening section of a proposal that summarises the whole project for busy decision makers."},{"term":"Crowdfunding","def":"Raising money for a project through small contributions from many people online."},{"term":"Pitch deck","def":"A short visual presentation used to persuade stakeholders or investors to support a project."}],"units":[{"id":"M1U1","title":"Pre-production principles: creating a digital media product","objectives":["Apply the stages of the creative design process (acceptance, analysis, definition, ideation, selection, implementation, evaluation) to a real local problem, linking back to Unit 1.","Define the creative process (Whyte, 2013), describe its seven stages with their plain-language glosses, and compare the linear, cyclic, stepped and branched network models.","Define a design problem, its purpose and goal, and analyse the target audience and market for a digital media product.","Use brainstorming processes such as mind mapping, SCAMPER and rapid ideation, and plan designs with rough sketches, mood boards and style guides.","Create storyboards and set timelines using production schedules, milestones, work plans and Gantt charts.","Explain the role of pre-production (concept development, logistical planning, risk reduction, creative alignment), its core elements (resources, clearances, copyright and IP, codes of practice, health and safety), the factors affecting creation, and the virtuous cycle.","Explain how prototypes are built and validated with target users, and how a validated prototype is implemented as a finished product."],"videos":[{"title":"The design process explained","q":"design thinking process explained for students empathise define ideate prototype test"},{"title":"SCAMPER brainstorming technique","q":"SCAMPER technique explained with examples creative thinking"},{"title":"How to make a storyboard","q":"how to make a storyboard for video beginners shot types"},{"title":"Gantt charts for beginners","q":"gantt chart explained for beginners project timeline milestones"}],"screens":[{"type":"learn","title":"Recap: the creative design process","html":"<p>In Unit 1 you met the <b>creative design process</b>. The creative process is defined as:</p>\n<p class=\"passage\">\"The sequence of activities that leads from the developing of ideas to producing a <b>desired outcome</b>.\" (Whyte, 2013)</p>\n<p>Unit 2 now asks you to <b>apply</b> it to real products. There are seven stages, and you are expected to document every one of them. The plain-language glosses are in brackets.</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th>Stage</th><th>What happens</th><th>Example: a hurricane-preparedness app for Sint Maarten</th></tr>\n<tr><td><b>1. Acceptance</b> (or understand the problem)</td><td>You commit to undertaking the task and identify the time, energy, resources and responsibility needed for the idea to come to life.</td><td>The Island Pulse Media agency agrees to build the app for a community group after Hurricane Irma and lists the staff, budget and weeks needed.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>2. Analysis</b> (the problem or situation)</td><td>You research the project idea and its market, and speak to the people the product will be useful to.</td><td>Keisha surveys residents in Cul de Sac and Dutch Quarter about how they got storm news in 2017.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>3. Definition</b> (restate the problem clearly by defining the goal)</td><td>You focus on the problem to be solved and set out the scope: timelines, milestones and production schedule.</td><td>\"Residents need one trusted, low-data place for shelter and storm updates.\" Launch milestone: 1 June.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>4. Ideation and brainstorming</b> (to think of the possibilities, come up with options)</td><td>You generate ideas and keep a log of all of them, even ones that seem irrelevant now.</td><td>Mind map: SMS alerts, WhatsApp bot, offline map, radio stream, checklist app.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>5. Selection</b> (decide on the best option by comparing feasibility, risks and so on)</td><td>You choose the idea that most closely meets the project's objectives.</td><td>The offline checklist and shelter map wins: it is feasible and works with no signal.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>6. Implementation</b> (by taking the plunge)</td><td>You work out the tasks and their sequence (often in a Gantt chart), then use resources to execute them.</td><td>Jerome builds it in Thunkable; Maya builds a matching web page.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>7. Evaluation</b> (assess whether the solution works and how it could be improved)</td><td>You gather feedback from the audience, stakeholders and clients, often with a rubric, and decide what to improve or eliminate.</td><td>Residents test it during a June drill; fixes are made before peak season.</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<p>The same journey is also described as <b>pre-production principles</b>: problem definition, target audience and market, brainstorming, planning designs, rough sketches, storyboards, timelines, prototype and idea validation, then implementation. Section 1.1 of the course outline follows this list, and this unit works through each part in order. The next screens give full notes on each stage and on the four creative process models.</p>\n<p>Pre-production is everything you do <b>before</b> full production begins. It is <b>the planning phase of any digital media project</b>, which lays the foundation for smooth execution and high-quality output. It is cheaper to change a sketch than a finished video, so good pre-production avoids costly mistakes in production and post-production.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> Your Unit 2 SBA proposal (14 marks) is really a pre-production document. Every technique in this unit, from the problem statement to the Gantt chart, can appear as evidence in your proposal and in your Progress and Process section.</p>","plain":"<p>The creative process is the set of steps that takes you from an idea to a finished product. It has seven stages: acceptance, analysis, definition, ideation and brainstorming, selection, implementation and evaluation. Think of hosting a family cook-out in Grenada: you agree to host, ask what people like, decide the menu, list ideas, pick the best ones, cook, then ask everyone how it went.</p>","deeper":"<p>Pre-production is not separate from the creative process: it covers the first five stages plus the planning part of implementation (the Gantt chart, the schedule and the prototype). A Grade One answer shows that the stages overlap and loop. Evaluation often sends a team back to analysis, for example when residents testing a hurricane app in Sint Maarten say the text is too small to read. A common mistake is to treat ideation and selection as one step. Examiners expect you to show that ideas are generated <b>without judgement</b> first, then compared on feasibility, cost and risk. When a question asks you to <b>apply</b> the process, name each stage and give one action from the scenario for it.</p>"},{"type":"order","game":true,"title":"Put the creative design process in order","intro":"<p>The Gouyave Heritage Club in Grenada wants a short digital tour of its fishing town. Put the seven stages of the creative process in the correct order.</p>","blocks":[{"t":"Acceptance: the team commits to the task and identifies the time, resources and responsibility needed."},{"t":"Analysis: the team researches the idea and its market and asks residents and visitors for their opinion."},{"t":"Definition: the team restates the problem as a clear goal and sets the scope, timeline and milestones."},{"t":"Ideation and brainstorming: the team logs every idea, such as an audio walk, a map and a video series."},{"t":"Selection: ideas are compared on feasibility and risk, and the one that best meets the objectives is chosen."},{"t":"Implementation: the team takes the plunge, sequences the tasks in a Gantt chart and produces the tour."},{"t":"Evaluation: visitors and the club give feedback, and the team decides what to improve or eliminate."}],"win":"Correct. Acceptance, analysis, definition, ideation and brainstorming, selection, implementation, evaluation: the sequence of activities that leads from developing ideas to producing a desired outcome (Whyte, 2013).","tutor":[{"h":"Understand before you create","p":"<p>Research and defining the problem must come before you generate ideas.</p>"},{"h":"Many, then one","p":"<p>Ideation produces many ideas; selection narrows them to one.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Design process order (Foundation)","intro":"<p>Put these four key stages in order.</p>","blocks":[{"t":"Analysis: research the users and the problem."},{"t":"Ideation and brainstorming: come up with options."},{"t":"Implementation: take the plunge and build the chosen idea."},{"t":"Evaluation: test it with users."}]},"up":{"title":"Put the creative design process in order (Challenge)","bloom":"Analyse","intro":"<p>No stage names this time. Read what Island Pulse Media does for the organisers of Antigua Sailing Week, who want a race-results page for visitors. Put the team's actions in the order of the seven-stage creative process.</p>","blocks":[{"t":"The studio signs the job and lists the staff, weeks and budget it will need."},{"t":"Team members interview skippers and visitors at English Harbour and study what other regatta apps offer."},{"t":"The team writes: \"Visitors need race times and results in one low-data place\", and sets a launch milestone of 1 April."},{"t":"Every suggestion goes on the ideas log: a live results page, a WhatsApp bot, a printed map, even a drone livestream."},{"t":"The ideas are scored for cost, risk and fit with the goal; the live results web page scores highest."},{"t":"The tasks are sequenced in a Gantt chart, and the page is built and tested on phones."},{"t":"After race week, survey results are compared with the goal and a list of fixes is drawn up for next year."}],"win":"Excellent. You recognised each stage from its action alone: acceptance, analysis, definition, ideation and brainstorming, selection, implementation and evaluation. That is how examiners test the process inside a scenario."}},{"type":"learn","title":"The creative process","html":"<p class=\"passage\"><b>The creative process</b> is \"the sequence of activities that leads from the developing of ideas to producing a desired outcome.\" (Whyte, 2013)</p>\n<p>This module has four general objectives: <b>understand</b> the creative process from idea to implementation; <b>appreciate</b> its role and importance in solution design; <b>plan and design</b> creative solutions; and <b>implement</b> solutions using digital content creation tools.</p>\n<p><b>Key points for each stage</b></p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>1. Acceptance</b>: the first stage, when you have committed to undertaking the task. The time, energy, resources and responsibility needed for the idea to come to life are identified.</li>\n<li><b>2. Analysis</b>: comprehensive research into the project idea and the market it will seek. Collect and examine all relevant information, and speak to the people your product or service will be useful to.</li>\n<li><b>3. Definition</b>: clearly focus on the problem to be solved and set out the scope of the project, including timelines, milestones and the production schedule.</li>\n<li><b>4. Ideation and brainstorming</b>: ideation is short for <b>idea generation</b>. Keep a log of all ideas, without discarding any that seem irrelevant now; they may be useful later. Build on ideas with free writing, mind maps and word association.</li>\n<li><b>5. Selection</b>: choose the idea, or way of executing it, that most closely meets the objectives of the project.</li>\n<li><b>6. Implementation</b>: work out all the tasks and their sequence (a Gantt chart shows this graphically), then use resources to execute them.</li>\n<li><b>7. Evaluation</b>: gather feedback from the target audience, stakeholders, clients and colleagues; review the procedures used; decide what to improve and what to eliminate. A rubric or standardised form may be used to judge the outcome.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><b>Creative process models</b></p>\n<svg viewBox=\"0 0 330 120\" width=\"100%\" style=\"max-width:440px\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Four creative process models: linear arrow chain, cyclic loop, stepped chain with loops back, and branched network that splits and converges\">\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.5\">\n<path d=\"M10 30 h55 M58 25 l7 5 l-7 5\"/>\n<circle cx=\"125\" cy=\"30\" r=\"20\"/><path d=\"M140 17 l5 0 l-1 6\"/>\n<path d=\"M190 20 h20 v20 h20 v20 M200 30 q-8 -14 4 -14 M220 50 q-8 -14 4 -14\"/>\n<path d=\"M260 30 l20 -15 M260 30 l20 0 M260 30 l20 15 M280 15 l25 15 M280 30 h25 M280 45 l25 -15 M305 30 h15\"/>\n</g>\n<g fill=\"currentColor\" font-size=\"10\" text-anchor=\"middle\">\n<text x=\"38\" y=\"80\">Linear</text><text x=\"125\" y=\"80\">Cyclic</text><text x=\"210\" y=\"80\">Stepped with</text><text x=\"210\" y=\"92\">feedback loops</text><text x=\"290\" y=\"80\">Branched</text><text x=\"290\" y=\"92\">network</text>\n</g>\n</svg>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Linear model</b>: one step after the other; each step must be clearly completed before you progress to the next.</li>\n<li><b>Cyclic model</b>: the stages form a circle, and you repeat steps until the creative process is complete.</li>\n<li><b>Stepped with immediate feedback loops</b>: each step can feed back to the previous step, so problems are fixed as you go until the process is fully completed.</li>\n<li><b>Branched network</b>: after analysis and definition the process splits into several possible ideas, which converge at selection; implementation then leads to evaluation and re-analysis. Paths can be explored and revisited.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>For example, Malik at the Island Pulse Media uses a stepped model for a 30-second Carnival ad, checking each storyboard frame with Zara before moving on, while Maya uses a branched network to try three website layouts for a boutique on Old Street in Roseau at once.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> Compare these models as a group and <b>justify</b> which best fits your project. Your SBA proposal earns marks for naming a model and showing evidence of every phase, so say which model you used and why.</p>","plain":"<p>The creative process has seven stages, from agreeing to do the job to checking whether it worked. Teams move through them in patterns called models. A linear model goes one step at a time. A cyclic model goes round in a circle. A stepped model lets you go back a step to fix things. A branched network tries several ideas at once, like a cook in Castries testing three sauces.</p>","deeper":"<p>Exam questions often ask you to <b>justify</b> a model, so link the model to the project's risk and deadline. A <b>linear</b> model suits a small, well-understood job with fixed sign-offs, such as resizing a client's logo for new signs. A <b>branched network</b> suits a new product where the best idea is unknown, but it costs more time because several options are developed in parallel. A <b>stepped model with feedback loops</b> is common in animation, where each storyboard frame or animatic is checked before moving on. A common misconception is that a team must use only one model. Real teams often mix them, for example branching at ideation and then working step by step during implementation.</p>"},{"type":"match","game":true,"title":"Match each stage to its description","intro":"<p>Keisha at the Island Pulse Media is labelling the project wall for a breadfruit festival campaign in Kingstown. Match each stage of the creative process to its description.</p>","pairs":[{"l":"Acceptance","r":"Commit to the task; identify the time, energy, resources and responsibility needed"},{"l":"Analysis","r":"Research the idea and its market; speak to the people the product will be useful to"},{"l":"Definition","r":"Restate the problem as a clear goal; set the scope, timelines and milestones"},{"l":"Ideation and brainstorming","r":"Generate ideas and keep a log of all of them, even those that seem irrelevant now"},{"l":"Selection","r":"Compare options on feasibility and risk; choose the one that best meets the objectives"},{"l":"Implementation","r":"Take the plunge: sequence the tasks in a Gantt chart and use resources to execute them"},{"l":"Evaluation","r":"Gather feedback from the audience and clients, often with a rubric, to see what to improve"}],"win":"Excellent. You can describe all seven stages of the creative process, from acceptance to evaluation.","tutor":[{"h":"Research or scope?","p":"<p>One stage collects information and opinions; the next uses it to state the goal and plan the scope.</p>"},{"h":"Many, then one","p":"<p>One stage keeps every idea; the next compares them and picks the best.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Match the stages (Foundation)","pairs":[{"l":"Acceptance","r":"Commit to the task"},{"l":"Ideation and brainstorming","r":"Come up with options"},{"l":"Implementation","r":"Take the plunge and build it"},{"l":"Evaluation","r":"Check whether it works and how to improve it"}]},"up":{"title":"Match each stage to its description (Challenge)","bloom":"Analyse","intro":"<p>Harder: the cards now show what the team actually did, not a definition. Keisha's team is running a campaign for Dominica's World Creole Music Festival. Match each stage to the action that belongs to it.</p>","pairs":[{"l":"Acceptance","r":"The agency signs on and estimates it needs three staff, US$2,000 and five weeks"},{"l":"Analysis","r":"The team surveys festival-goers in Roseau and reviews last year's social media comments"},{"l":"Definition","r":"The goal is written down: increase early-bird ticket sales by 15% before 1 October"},{"l":"Ideation and brainstorming","r":"A mind map logs twenty ideas, including a Kwéyòl phrase-of-the-day reel"},{"l":"Selection","r":"Three concepts are scored for cost, risk and reach, and the reel series scores highest"},{"l":"Implementation","r":"The reels are filmed, edited and scheduled, following the Gantt chart"},{"l":"Evaluation","r":"Ticket data and a feedback form show which reels worked and what to drop next year"}],"win":"Excellent. You can spot each stage of the creative process from what a team does, which is exactly how a scenario question tests it."}},{"type":"mcq","title":"Which creative process model?","q":"Maya at the Island Pulse Media is designing a booking website for the Placencia Kayak Tours. After analysis and definition she develops three different homepage ideas at the same time, brings them together to select one, and after implementation the evaluation sends her back to re-analyse. Which creative process model is she using?","options":[{"t":"Linear model","fb":"A linear model follows one line, completing each step before the next. Maya explored three paths at once."},{"t":"Cyclic model","fb":"A cyclic model repeats the stages in a circle, but it does not split into several parallel ideas."},{"t":"Branched network","ok":true,"fb":"Correct. The process splits into several ideas after analysis and definition, converges at selection, and evaluation leads to re-analysis."},{"t":"Stepped with immediate feedback loops","fb":"This model feeds back to the previous step as you go. The key feature here is the split into several ideas that converge."}],"tutor":[{"h":"Look for the split","p":"<p>Which model divides into several possible ideas and then brings them back together?</p>"},{"h":"Converge","p":"<p>Options converge at selection in one particular model.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Which model? (Foundation)","q":"In which model must each step be clearly completed before you move to the next?","options":[{"t":"Cyclic model","fb":"The cyclic model repeats steps in a circle."},{"t":"Linear model","ok":true,"fb":"Yes. The linear model goes one step after the other, in a line."},{"t":"Branched network","fb":"A branched network splits into several paths."}]},"up":{"title":"Which creative process model? (Challenge)","bloom":"Evaluate","q":"Island Pulse Media is making a two-minute explainer animation for a solar-energy start-up in Georgetown, Guyana. The client must sign off each stage (script, storyboard, animatic, final cut) before the next begins, but the team also wants to fix small problems in the previous step as they go, without restarting the project. Which model BEST fits, and why?","options":[{"t":"Cyclic model, because animation always repeats the same steps.","fb":"A cyclic model repeats the whole circle of stages until the process is complete. Here the team moves forward, making small fixes at each step."},{"t":"Stepped with immediate feedback loops, because the stages are approved in sequence but each step can feed back to the previous one to fix problems as the team goes.","ok":true,"fb":"Correct. It keeps the ordered sign-offs and allows the small, immediate corrections the team wants."},{"t":"Branched network, because the animation has several scenes.","fb":"Several scenes are not several alternative ideas. A branched network splits into different possible solutions that converge at selection."},{"t":"Linear model, because the client signs off each stage in order.","fb":"Ordered sign-off fits a linear model, but a linear model does not feed back to fix the previous step. The team wants both."}]}},{"type":"learn","title":"The role of pre-production","html":"<p class=\"passage\"><b>Pre-production</b> is the planning phase of any digital media project. It lays the foundation for smooth execution and high-quality output, regardless of whether the content is a video, image or audio piece.</p>\n<p><b>(a) Key roles of pre-production</b></p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Concept development</b>: refining ideas, storyboarding, scripting or outlining the creative vision.</li>\n<li><b>Logistical planning</b>: scheduling, budgeting, scouting locations and assembling the right team.</li>\n<li><b>Risk reduction</b>: identifying potential issues ahead of time (technical, legal or safety) and planning around them.</li>\n<li><b>Creative alignment</b>: ensuring all team members share a unified understanding of the project's goals and style.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Pre-production helps <b>avoid costly mistakes</b> during production and post-production.</p>\n<p><b>(b) Core elements of pre-production</b></p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th>Element</th><th>What it covers</th><th>Barbados example</th></tr>\n<tr><td><b>1. Resources</b></td><td><b>Money (budget)</b>: determines the scale, equipment, crew and quality. <b>Time</b>: scheduling for shoots, editing and deadlines. <b>Personnel</b>: directors, producers, editors, photographers, sound engineers. <b>Facilities</b>: studios, post-production houses, sound booths. <b>Location</b>: where filming or recording takes place, including logistics such as travel and weather.</td><td>Malik books a sound booth for a voice-over and plans a beach shoot near Grantley Adams International Airport around the flight times and the forecast.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>2. Clearances</b></td><td><b>Permits</b>: required for shooting in public or regulated spaces. <b>Releases</b>: consent forms from talent, interviewees or property owners.</td><td>Permission before flying a drone near the airport; release forms from vendors filmed on Swan Street in Bridgetown.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>3. Copyright and intellectual property</b></td><td>Use of original content or proper licensing of third-party assets (music, images, scripts); avoiding infringement by making sure all material is legal and cleared.</td><td>Licensing a soca track instead of ripping it from YouTube.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>4. Codes of practice and regulations</b></td><td>Industry standards (broadcast codes, advertising guidelines); legal and ethical compliance, such as truthful advertising and age-appropriate content.</td><td>A rum advert must not target under-18s; a hotel promo must not exaggerate.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>5. Health and safety</b></td><td>Risk assessments for filming conditions, such as working with equipment or stunts.</td><td>Checking cables, heat and sea conditions before a sunrise shoot at Bathsheba.</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<p>The Island Pulse Media keeps a one-page pre-production checklist with these five headings, and Zara will not approve a shoot until every row has a tick.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> Learn the four roles and five core elements by name. If a question asks you to \"explain the role of pre-production\", give the role, say what it involves, and finish with the benefit: avoiding costly mistakes in production and post-production.</p>","plain":"<p>Pre-production is the planning you do before you film, record or design anything. It has four jobs: develop the idea, plan the practical details, spot problems early and make sure the whole team agrees on the goal. It also covers five core elements: resources, clearances, copyright, codes of practice, and health and safety. Planning a school fete in Antigua works the same way: book the field, get permission and check safety first.</p>","deeper":"<p>The strongest answers explain <b>why</b> pre-production saves money: the cost of a change rises at every stage. Moving a scene on a storyboard takes minutes; reshooting it after crew, talent and location have been paid can wipe out the budget. Notice how the core elements connect. A drone shot is a <b>resource</b> decision, but flying near Grantley Adams International Airport also needs a <b>clearance</b>, and a crowded beach adds a <b>health and safety</b> risk. A common mistake is to treat copyright as a post-production issue. Music must be licensed before editing starts, or the whole cut may need to be redone. In an \"explain\" question, give the role, what it involves and the benefit.</p>"},{"type":"sort","game":true,"title":"Which core element of pre-production?","intro":"<p>Keisha at the Island Pulse Media is filling in the pre-production checklist for a Sugar Mas promo video in Basseterre. Sort each task under its core element.</p>","main":"Which core element does this task belong to?","bins":["Resources","Clearances","Copyright and IP","Codes, regulations, health and safety"],"items":[{"t":"Check that every photo in the end titles is original or cleared.","bin":2,"fb":"All material used must be legal and cleared: copyright and IP."},{"t":"Book an editor and a sound engineer for two days.","bin":0,"fb":"Personnel are a resource."},{"t":"Do a risk assessment for filming on a moving float at night.","bin":3,"fb":"Risk assessments for filming conditions are health and safety."},{"t":"License the calypso track used as background music.","bin":2,"fb":"Properly licensing third-party music protects against copyright infringement."},{"t":"Make sure the sponsor's claims in the advert are truthful.","bin":3,"fb":"Truthful advertising is legal and ethical compliance under codes of practice."},{"t":"Collect signed consent forms from the troupe members filmed.","bin":1,"fb":"Releases (consent forms) from talent and interviewees are clearances."},{"t":"Get a permit to film in a public area during the parade.","bin":1,"fb":"Permits for public or regulated spaces are clearances."},{"t":"Set a budget of US$1,500 for crew, gear hire and editing.","bin":0,"fb":"Money (budget) is a resource: it decides scale, equipment, crew and quality."}],"win":"Well sorted. You can place pre-production tasks under resources, clearances, copyright and IP, codes of practice and health and safety.","tutor":[{"h":"Permission from whom?","p":"<p>Permission from authorities or people filmed is one element; permission to use someone else's creative work is another.</p>"},{"h":"Money, time, people, places","p":"<p>These all belong to the same core element.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Core elements (Foundation)","items":[{"t":"Set the project budget.","bin":0,"fb":"Money is a resource."},{"t":"Get a filming permit.","bin":1,"fb":"Permits are clearances."},{"t":"License the background music.","bin":2,"fb":"Licensing protects copyright."},{"t":"Do a risk assessment for the shoot.","bin":3,"fb":"Risk assessment is health and safety."}]},"up":{"title":"Which core element of pre-production? (Challenge)","bloom":"Analyse","intro":"<p>Five bins now: codes of practice and health and safety are separate. Keisha is planning a music video shoot at Pigeon Island, St. Lucia, with two 16-year-old dancers. Sort each task under its core element.</p>","main":"Which core element does this task belong to?","bins":["Resources","Clearances","Copyright and IP","Codes of practice and regulations","Health and safety"],"items":[{"t":"Confirm that the sample used in the beat has been cleared by its original producer.","bin":2,"fb":"Using someone else's recording legally is a copyright and IP matter."},{"t":"Make sure no alcohol brand appears in a way that appeals to under-18 viewers.","bin":3,"fb":"Age-appropriate content is a code of practice and advertising rule."},{"t":"Hire a gimbal and two LED panels for the weekend.","bin":0,"fb":"Equipment hire is a resource, paid for from the budget."},{"t":"Book the boat to the location, with a back-up date in case the sea is rough.","bin":0,"fb":"Location logistics, travel and weather planning are part of resources."},{"t":"Get written permission from the site's managers to film there.","bin":1,"fb":"A permit to film in a regulated space is a clearance."},{"t":"Keep the licence for the stock drone clip used in the intro.","bin":2,"fb":"Third-party footage must be properly licensed, and the licence kept as proof."},{"t":"Assess the risk of filming dancers on wet rocks and plan first aid.","bin":4,"fb":"A risk assessment for filming conditions is health and safety."},{"t":"Tape down cables and keep the lights away from the water's edge.","bin":4,"fb":"Protecting crew from trips and electrical hazards is health and safety."},{"t":"Have the parents of the two 16-year-old dancers sign release forms.","bin":1,"fb":"Releases (consent forms) for people filmed are clearances. Minors need a parent or guardian to sign."},{"t":"Remove the claim that the beach is \"never crowded\" unless it is true.","bin":3,"fb":"Truthful advertising is a code of practice."}],"win":"Excellent. You separated legal permissions (clearances) from ownership of creative material (copyright and IP), and rules about content (codes of practice) from risks to people (health and safety)."}},{"type":"learn","title":"Problem definition: purpose and goal","html":"<p>Every strong digital media product starts with a clearly <b>defined problem</b>. If the problem is vague, the product will be vague. Problem definition answers four questions:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>What is the challenge?</b> The real need or gap, described from the user's point of view.</li>\n<li><b>Who has the problem?</b> The people affected (this leads into audience analysis).</li>\n<li><b>What is the purpose?</b> What the product must do: inform, educate, persuade, entertain, sell or connect.</li>\n<li><b>What is the goal?</b> A measurable result that tells you whether the product worked.</li>\n</ul>\n<p class=\"passage\"><b>Problem statement:</b> one or two sentences that name the user, the need and the reason it matters, without naming the solution. A useful pattern is: \"[User] needs a way to [need] because [insight].\"</p>\n<p>Compare these two statements written by Zara, creative director at the Island Pulse Media, for a client, Bay Street Craft Market in Nassau:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Weak:</b> \"The market needs a cool app.\" This names a solution, gives no user and cannot be measured.</li>\n<li><b>Strong:</b> \"Cruise visitors who have only four hours in Nassau need a quick way to find local craft stalls because most leave the port without visiting the market. Goal: increase visitors to the market by 20% in the next cruise season.\"</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Notice that the strong version does not say \"app\". Keeping the solution open allows ideation to find the best format, which might be a QR-code map at the Nassau cruise port, an Instagram Reel series or a printed flyer with a link.</p>\n<p><b>Setting a good goal.</b> Many designers use <b>SMART</b> goals: Specific, Measurable, Achievable, Relevant and Time-bound. \"Make people like the market\" is not SMART; \"Get 500 map scans at the cruise pier between November and April\" is.</p>\n<p><b>The design brief.</b> The problem statement and goal are usually written into a <b>design brief</b>, the agreed document between client and designer. A brief normally includes the background, problem statement, purpose, target audience, deliverables (for example, a 30-second video and three social posts), budget, deadline and constraints such as brand colours or data limits.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> In Paper 02, if a question says \"state the problem the product addresses\", write a user-centred problem statement, not a description of the product. Examiners reward the user, the need and the reason.</p>","plain":"<p>Before you design anything, say clearly what the problem is. A good problem statement names who has the problem, what they need and why. It does not name the solution yet. Then set a goal you can measure. For example: \"Visitors to Oistins need to find the Friday fish fry stalls quickly because many get lost. Goal: 300 map scans in one month.\"</p>","deeper":"<p>A Grade One candidate keeps the <b>purpose</b> (what the product must do, such as inform or persuade) separate from the <b>goal</b> (the measurable result). Many weak answers mix them up, or give a goal that cannot be checked, such as \"raise awareness\". Make awareness measurable: \"70% of surveyed beachgoers can name two turtle rules after watching\". Also watch for the hidden solution: \"people need an app to...\" has already chosen the format, which closes off ideation. In a design brief the problem statement links straight to the evaluation stage, because the goal you set now becomes the test you apply at the end.</p>"},{"type":"mcq","title":"Choose the strongest problem statement","q":"Grande Riviere Turtle Watch group in Trinidad is worried that beachgoers disturb nesting turtles. Which is the STRONGEST problem statement for a digital media product?","options":[{"t":"Turtles are important to the Caribbean.","fb":"This is a true background fact, but it does not state who has the problem, what they need or what success looks like."},{"t":"The group wants a beautiful website with lots of turtle photos.","fb":"This describes a product and its style, not the user problem. A beautiful website might not change anyone's behaviour."},{"t":"We need to make a TikTok video about turtles.","fb":"This names a solution before the problem is understood, and it has no user or measurable goal."},{"t":"Beachgoers at Grande Riviere and Matura Beach need a quick way to learn how to behave near turtle nests because many disturb nests without realising it; goal: reduce reported disturbances by half during the next nesting season.","ok":true,"fb":"Correct. It names the users, the need and the insight, does not lock in a solution, and has a measurable, time-bound goal."}],"tutor":[{"h":"Four parts","p":"<p>Look for a user, a need, a reason and a measurable goal.</p>"},{"h":"Solution trap","p":"<p>A problem statement should not jump straight to the format (video, website, app).</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Strongest problem statement (Foundation)","q":"Which is a good problem statement?","options":[{"t":"We need a turtle video.","fb":"This names a solution, not the problem."},{"t":"Beachgoers need to learn how to avoid turtle nests because many disturb them by accident.","ok":true,"fb":"Yes. It names the user, the need and the reason."},{"t":"Turtles are nice.","fb":"This is an opinion, not a problem statement."}]},"up":{"title":"Choose the strongest problem statement (Challenge)","bloom":"Evaluate","q":"A youth group in Basseterre, St. Kitts, wants a digital product to reduce litter on the J'ouvert route. All four statements below sound reasonable. Which one would earn FULL marks as a problem statement with a goal?","options":[{"t":"The youth group needs to reduce litter by 25% next year because the streets are dirty.","fb":"The goal is measurable, but the user is the group, not the revellers who drop the litter, and the reason gives no insight into why it happens."},{"t":"J'ouvert revellers in Basseterre need a quick way to find the nearest bin along the route because most litter is dropped where no bin is in sight; goal: cut next year's clean-up time by 25%.","ok":true,"fb":"Correct. It names the users, the need and an insight from research, leaves the format open and sets a measurable, time-bound goal."},{"t":"Revellers need an Instagram filter that shows bin locations because filters are fun; goal: get lots of downloads.","fb":"It names a solution (an Instagram filter) before ideation, and \"lots of downloads\" is neither measurable nor linked to litter."},{"t":"J'ouvert revellers in Basseterre need to know where bins are because litter is a problem.","fb":"Close, but the reason is vague and there is no measurable, time-bound goal."}]}},{"type":"learn","title":"Target audience and market analysis","html":"<p>The <b>target audience</b> is the specific group of people a product is designed for. The <b>target market</b> is the wider group of potential users or buyers, including their size and the competition. You are expected to analyse both before designing.</p>\n<p><b>Ways to describe an audience</b></p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th>Type</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Examples</th></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Demographics</b></td><td>Measurable facts about people</td><td>Age, gender, location, income, education, language, occupation</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Psychographics</b></td><td>Values, interests, attitudes and lifestyle</td><td>Loves soca, eco-conscious, values family, follows fitness trends</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Behaviours</b></td><td>What people actually do</td><td>Uses WhatsApp daily, shops at night, buys data by the day, watches Reels with the sound off</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Needs and pain points</b></td><td>What frustrates or motivates them</td><td>\"I can't find bus times\", \"Data is expensive\", \"I want local news fast\"</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Technical context</b></td><td>Devices, connectivity and skills</td><td>Mid-range Android phone, patchy signal in the hills, low digital literacy</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<p><b>Research methods.</b> <b>Primary research</b> is information you collect yourself: surveys (Google Forms), interviews, focus groups and observation. <b>Secondary research</b> uses existing data: reports from the ITU, GSMA or World Bank, statistics offices, competitor websites and app store reviews. In your SBA, primary research with real local users is strong evidence.</p>\n<p><b>Market analysis</b> asks: How big is the market? Who are the competitors and what do they do badly? What makes your product different (its <b>unique selling point</b>)? Keisha at the Island Pulse Media often runs a quick competitor check by searching the App Store, Google Play and Instagram for similar products in Jamaica, Curaçao and Barbados.</p>\n<p><b>User personas.</b> Designers turn research into a <b>persona</b>: an imagined but realistic profile of a typical user. For example, \"Andre, 17, Sixth Form student in May Pen; Android phone; buys data weekly; uses Instagram and WhatsApp; wants quick, visual information.\" Personas keep the whole team designing for real people instead of for themselves.</p>\n<p>Audience analysis drives every later decision: language and tone, colours, file sizes, video length, platform (TikTok or Facebook?) and accessibility features such as captions.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> When a question asks you to \"describe the target audience\", give at least one demographic, one psychographic or behavioural detail and one technical detail, then <b>explain</b> how each affects the design.</p>","plain":"<p>The target audience is the group your product is made for. Describe them with facts (age, where they live), values (what they care about), behaviours (what they do) and the devices they use. You find this out by asking them directly, with surveys and interviews, or by reading reports. A bakery in Kingston might find its audience is office workers aged 25 to 40 who order lunch on WhatsApp.</p>","deeper":"<p>Strong analysis turns each audience fact into a design decision. \"Uses budget Android phones with prepaid data\" should lead to small file sizes and a web page rather than a large app. \"Watches Reels with the sound off\" should lead to captions. Without that link, an audience description earns few marks. Know the limits of each research method: an online survey is quick but only reaches people who are already online, while observation shows what people actually do rather than what they say they do. A persona summarises research, so a persona built on guesswork is a common weakness. Base it on real survey or interview data from the territory you are designing for.</p>"},{"type":"sort","game":true,"title":"Demographic, psychographic or behaviour?","intro":"<p>Keisha is building a persona for Fish Fry Hop food-tour app in Providenciales. Sort each research finding.</p>","main":"What kind of audience information is this?","bins":["Demographic","Psychographic","Behaviour"],"items":[{"t":"Users see themselves as adventurous foodies.","bin":1,"fb":"Self-image and lifestyle are psychographic."},{"t":"Users read reviews on TripAdvisor before choosing.","bin":2,"fb":"Reading reviews before deciding is a behaviour."},{"t":"Users book tours on their phones in the evening.","bin":2,"fb":"This describes what people actually do, so it is a behaviour."},{"t":"Most users have a household income above the regional average.","bin":0,"fb":"Income is a measurable demographic factor."},{"t":"Most users are aged 25 to 44.","bin":0,"fb":"Age is a measurable fact about people, so it is demographic."},{"t":"Users share food photos on Instagram during the meal.","bin":2,"fb":"Sharing photos is an action they take, so it is a behaviour."},{"t":"Many visitors come from the United States and Canada.","bin":0,"fb":"Location or country of origin is demographic."},{"t":"Users value authentic local food over chain restaurants.","bin":1,"fb":"Values and attitudes are psychographic."}],"win":"Well sorted. Demographics are measurable facts, psychographics are values and lifestyle, and behaviours are what people actually do. A good persona uses all three.","tutor":[{"h":"Facts, feelings, actions","p":"<p>Demographic = facts you could count. Psychographic = what people value or believe. Behaviour = what they do.</p>"},{"h":"Test it","p":"<p>Can you see it happening? If yes, it is probably a behaviour.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Audience information (Foundation)","items":[{"t":"Users are aged 25 to 44.","bin":0,"fb":"Age is demographic."},{"t":"Users value authentic local food.","bin":1,"fb":"Values are psychographic."},{"t":"Users book on their phones at night.","bin":2,"fb":"An action is a behaviour."},{"t":"Users see themselves as adventurous.","bin":1,"fb":"Self-image is psychographic."}]},"up":{"title":"Demographic, psychographic or behaviour? (Challenge)","bloom":"Analyse","intro":"<p>Keisha is now building a full persona for a CSEC revision app for secondary students in Tobago. Sort each research finding into one of FIVE kinds of audience information. Some cards are close: read them carefully.</p>","main":"What kind of audience information is this?","bins":["Demographic","Psychographic","Behaviour","Technical context","Need or pain point"],"items":[{"t":"They believe passing CSEC Mathematics is the key to a good future.","bin":1,"fb":"A belief or attitude is psychographic."},{"t":"\"I can never find past-paper answers that explain the working.\"","bin":4,"fb":"An unmet need is a pain point, and it points straight to a feature."},{"t":"They revise in short bursts on the bus home.","bin":2,"fb":"This is what they actually do: a behaviour."},{"t":"Most speak English at home and some also speak Spanish.","bin":0,"fb":"Language is a measurable demographic fact."},{"t":"They skip any video that is longer than three minutes.","bin":2,"fb":"An observed action is a behaviour."},{"t":"Most use budget Android phones with 32 GB of storage.","bin":3,"fb":"Devices and their limits are technical context."},{"t":"The mobile signal is weak in some villages on the windward coast.","bin":3,"fb":"Connectivity is technical context."},{"t":"Most respondents are aged 15 to 17 and attend school in Scarborough.","bin":0,"fb":"Age and location are measurable facts: demographic."},{"t":"\"My data runs out before the end of the week.\"","bin":4,"fb":"A frustration the user states in their own words is a pain point."},{"t":"They admire local YouTubers who explain topics in a relaxed way.","bin":1,"fb":"Interests and tastes are psychographic."}],"win":"Excellent. A Grade One persona uses all five kinds of information, and each one can be turned into a design decision."}},{"type":"learn","title":"Brainstorming: mind mapping, SCAMPER and rapid ideation","html":"<p><b>Brainstorming</b> is the ideation stage: generating as many ideas as possible before judging them. The golden rules are: go for quantity, defer judgement, build on others' ideas and welcome wild ideas. Judging comes later, in the selection stage.</p>\n<p><b>1. Mind mapping</b> (popularised by Tony Buzan) places the central problem in the middle of the page and branches out into themes, then sub-ideas. It shows connections between ideas visually. Tools include paper and markers, Miro, Canva Whiteboards and Coggle.</p>\n<svg viewBox=\"0 0 320 150\" width=\"100%\" style=\"max-width:420px\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Mind map with a central idea and four branches\">\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.5\">\n<ellipse cx=\"160\" cy=\"75\" rx=\"62\" ry=\"22\"/>\n<line x1=\"110\" y1=\"62\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"30\"/><line x1=\"210\" y1=\"62\" x2=\"260\" y2=\"30\"/>\n<line x1=\"110\" y1=\"88\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"120\"/><line x1=\"210\" y1=\"88\" x2=\"260\" y2=\"120\"/>\n</g>\n<g fill=\"currentColor\" font-size=\"11\" text-anchor=\"middle\">\n<text x=\"160\" y=\"79\">Carnival promo</text>\n<text x=\"50\" y=\"24\">Audience</text><text x=\"270\" y=\"24\">Platforms</text>\n<text x=\"50\" y=\"136\">Visual style</text><text x=\"270\" y=\"136\">Content ideas</text>\n</g>\n</svg>\n<p><b>2. SCAMPER</b> (developed by Bob Eberle from Alex Osborn's idea checklist) takes an existing product and asks seven question prompts:</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th>Letter</th><th>Prompt</th><th>Example: the Daily Bake bakery's printed menu</th></tr>\n<tr><td><b>S</b>ubstitute</td><td>What can be swapped?</td><td>Replace the paper menu with a QR-code menu.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>C</b>ombine</td><td>What can be merged?</td><td>Combine the menu with WhatsApp ordering.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>A</b>dapt</td><td>What can be borrowed from elsewhere?</td><td>Adapt a food-delivery app's photo cards.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>M</b>odify</td><td>What can be enlarged, shrunk or changed?</td><td>Magnify the photos of johnny cakes and coconut tarts.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>P</b>ut to another use</td><td>Who else could use it?</td><td>Reuse menu photos as Instagram posts.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>E</b>liminate</td><td>What can be removed?</td><td>Remove items that rarely sell to simplify choice.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>R</b>everse / Rearrange</td><td>What if the order changed?</td><td>Show today's specials first instead of last.</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<p><b>3. Rapid ideation</b> means producing many ideas very quickly under a time limit. A popular method is <b>Crazy 8s</b>: fold a sheet into eight panels and sketch eight different ideas in eight minutes. Speed stops you from over-thinking and polishing too early.</p>\n<p>Other techniques include <b>brainwriting</b> (everyone writes ideas silently, then passes them on), <b>word association</b> and <b>reverse brainstorming</b> (\"How could we make this problem worse?\").</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> If asked to \"explain\" a brainstorming technique, describe how it works <b>and</b> how it helps the designer. For example: \"SCAMPER helps by giving a checklist of prompts, so the team improves an existing product systematically instead of waiting for inspiration.\"</p>","plain":"<p>Brainstorming means coming up with lots of ideas before you judge any of them. A mind map puts the problem in the middle and branches out. SCAMPER uses seven prompts, such as Substitute and Combine, to improve something that already exists. Crazy 8s means sketching eight ideas in eight minutes. A roti shop in San Fernando could use SCAMPER to turn its paper menu into a WhatsApp menu.</p>","deeper":"<p>Choose the technique to fit the problem. A <b>mind map</b> suits a brand-new brief because it explores themes and connections. <b>SCAMPER</b> works best when an existing product needs improving, since every prompt starts from something real. <b>Rapid ideation</b> beats perfectionism: a team that sketches twenty rough ideas usually finds a better one than a team that polishes its first idea. A common exam mistake is to name a technique without explaining <b>how</b> it generates ideas. Another is to judge ideas during brainstorming, when criticism belongs in the selection stage. Groupthink is a real risk, so methods such as brainwriting let quieter team members contribute before louder voices take over.</p>"},{"type":"match","game":true,"title":"Match the SCAMPER prompt","intro":"<p>Malik at the Island Pulse Media is using SCAMPER to improve a Castries community library's old storytime videos. Match each SCAMPER prompt to his idea.</p>","pairs":[{"l":"Substitute","r":"Replace the live-action narrator with an animated pelican character"},{"l":"Combine","r":"Merge the storytime video with a short quiz at the end"},{"l":"Adapt","r":"Borrow the \"choose your path\" idea from interactive video games"},{"l":"Modify","r":"Shorten each 20-minute video into 3-minute episodes"},{"l":"Put to another use","r":"Use the recorded audio as a podcast for car journeys"},{"l":"Eliminate","r":"Remove the long title sequence at the start"},{"l":"Reverse / Rearrange","r":"Start with the story's exciting ending, then go back to the beginning"}],"win":"Excellent. You can now use SCAMPER as a structured checklist to improve an existing product, which is exactly how designers use it.","tutor":[{"h":"Swap or merge?","p":"<p>Substitute swaps one thing for another. Combine joins two things together.</p>"},{"h":"Change size or remove?","p":"<p>Modify changes something (shorter, bigger). Eliminate takes it away completely.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Match SCAMPER (Foundation)","pairs":[{"l":"Substitute","r":"Replace the narrator with a cartoon character"},{"l":"Combine","r":"Add a quiz to the end of the video"},{"l":"Eliminate","r":"Remove the long title sequence"},{"l":"Put to another use","r":"Turn the audio into a podcast"}]},"up":{"title":"Match the SCAMPER prompt (Challenge)","bloom":"Analyse","intro":"<p>Harder ideas, and some prompts look alike. Malik is improving a Belize City tour company's printed walking-tour leaflet. Match each SCAMPER prompt to the idea that fits it BEST.</p>","pairs":[{"l":"Substitute","r":"Swap the printed leaflet for a QR code at each stop that plays a 60-second audio clip"},{"l":"Combine","r":"Join the walking tour with a food-tasting stop and sell them as one ticket"},{"l":"Adapt","r":"Borrow the badge-collecting idea from fitness apps so walkers unlock a stamp at each site"},{"l":"Modify","r":"Enlarge the map and add bold colour-coded routes for visitors with poor eyesight"},{"l":"Put to another use","r":"Offer the tour videos to schools as a local history lesson"},{"l":"Eliminate","r":"Drop the three least-visited stops so the tour fits into one hour"},{"l":"Reverse / Rearrange","r":"Start the tour at the water taxi terminal, where visitors arrive, instead of ending there"}],"win":"Excellent. You told apart prompts that look alike: Substitute swaps one part for another, Adapt borrows an idea from somewhere else, and Modify changes size, shape or emphasis."}},{"type":"learn","title":"Planning designs: rough sketches, mood boards and style guides","html":"<p>Once an idea is selected, designers plan how it will <b>look and feel</b> before producing anything. Three planning documents are named in the course outline.</p>\n<p><b>1. Rough sketches</b></p>\n<p>Quick, loose drawings that explore layout, composition and characters. They are deliberately unfinished. <b>Thumbnails</b> are very small sketches used to try many layouts fast; <b>roughs</b> are larger and more developed. Sketches are cheap: Zara at the Island Pulse Media will sketch twenty poster layouts on paper in the time it takes to build one in Illustrator. Use pencil and paper, a black marker for final lines, or a tablet in Krita or Procreate. Annotate sketches with notes about colours, fonts and sizes.</p>\n<p><b>2. Mood boards</b></p>\n<p>A <b>mood board</b> is a collage of images, colours, textures, fonts and words that captures the intended <b>feeling</b> and visual direction of a project. It is an inspiration tool, not a final design. A mood board for a Vincy Mas promo might include photos of costumes, feathers and sequins, a bright tropical palette, bold display type and words such as \"energy\", \"heritage\" and \"jump-up\". Tools include Canva, Pinterest boards, Milanote and Figma. Mood boards help a client agree on the direction early, before money is spent.</p>\n<p><b>3. Style guides</b></p>\n<p>A <b>style guide</b> (or brand guide) is the rulebook that keeps every part of a product consistent. It usually specifies:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Logo</b> usage: versions, minimum size and clear space.</li>\n<li><b>Colour palette</b> with exact values: hex for screens, CMYK for print (you will learn these in the next unit).</li>\n<li><b>Typography</b>: fonts, sizes and weights for headings and body text.</li>\n<li><b>Imagery</b>: photo style, icon style and illustration style.</li>\n<li><b>Tone of voice</b>: friendly, formal, playful.</li>\n<li><b>UI components</b> for websites and apps: buttons, spacing, grids.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th></th><th>Rough sketch</th><th>Mood board</th><th>Style guide</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Purpose</td><td>Explore layout and ideas</td><td>Set the feeling and direction</td><td>Fix the rules for consistency</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Finish</td><td>Loose, unfinished</td><td>Collage of found material</td><td>Precise and exact values</td></tr>\n<tr><td>When</td><td>Early ideation and planning</td><td>Early, before designs</td><td>Before and during production</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> Paper 02 may ask you to <b>draw</b> a rough sketch or storyboard with pencil and black marker. Label it clearly: title, dimensions, colour notes and the position of text. Neat labels earn marks even if your drawing is simple.</p>","plain":"<p>Designers plan the look before they build. Rough sketches are quick drawings to try out layouts. A mood board is a collage of pictures, colours and words that shows the feeling you want. A style guide is the rulebook, with exact fonts, colours and logo rules. Think of a Carnival band: sketches of costumes, a mood board of feathers and colours, then exact rules for the people sewing.</p>","deeper":"<p>The three documents answer different questions. Sketches ask \"what could it look like?\", mood boards ask \"how should it feel?\" and style guides state \"how must it always look?\". A mood board is not a design: copying its images straight into a final product can breach copyright, so it guides direction only. Style guides matter most when several people work on a product over time; a tourism authority in St. Kitts may use one logo and palette across its website, buses and Instagram for years. In Paper 02, label sketches with dimensions, colour notes and text positions. When you write a style guide entry, give exact values, such as a hex code and a font size, not just \"blue\" or \"big\".</p>"},{"type":"sort","game":true,"title":"Sketch, mood board or style guide?","intro":"<p>Maya at the Island Pulse Media is planning a website for the Punda Market Collective in Willemstad. Sort each planning item.</p>","main":"Which planning document does this belong in?","bins":["Rough sketch","Mood board","Style guide"],"items":[{"t":"Primary colour: #E07A1F; never place the logo on a busy photo.","bin":2,"fb":"Exact colour values and logo rules are style guide content."},{"t":"Eight tiny thumbnail layouts drawn in eight minutes.","bin":0,"fb":"Thumbnails are small rough sketches for exploring many layouts."},{"t":"All buttons have rounded corners and 12 px padding.","bin":2,"fb":"Rules for UI components keep the site consistent, so they belong in the style guide."},{"t":"Heading font: Montserrat Bold, 32 px; body: Open Sans, 16 px.","bin":2,"fb":"Exact typography rules belong in the style guide."},{"t":"Words like \"fresh\", \"bilingual\" and \"market morning\" beside fabric textures.","bin":1,"fb":"Mood words and textures set the direction, so this is a mood board."},{"t":"A quick pencil drawing showing the menu bar at the top and three product boxes below.","bin":0,"fb":"A loose layout drawing that explores structure is a rough sketch."},{"t":"Photos of spice stalls, woven baskets and sunset colours pinned together.","bin":1,"fb":"A collage of inspiring images that captures a feeling is a mood board."},{"t":"A loose drawing of the homepage hero area with notes saying \"photo here\".","bin":0,"fb":"An annotated, unfinished layout is a rough sketch."}],"win":"Well done. Rough sketches explore, mood boards inspire and set the feeling, and style guides fix exact rules for consistency.","tutor":[{"h":"Exact numbers","p":"<p>If it gives precise values (hex codes, pixel sizes, font names), it is a rule.</p>"},{"h":"Feeling or layout?","p":"<p>Collected images and words that set a mood are different from your own drawing of a layout.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Planning documents (Foundation)","items":[{"t":"A quick pencil drawing of a page layout.","bin":0,"fb":"Rough sketch."},{"t":"A collage of inspiring photos and colours.","bin":1,"fb":"Mood board."},{"t":"Heading font: Montserrat Bold, 32 px.","bin":2,"fb":"Style guide rule."},{"t":"Primary colour: #E07A1F.","bin":2,"fb":"Style guide rule."}]},"up":{"title":"Sketch, mood board or style guide? (Challenge)","bloom":"Analyse","intro":"<p>Colours, fonts and layouts appear in all three documents, so ask whether each item <b>explores</b>, <b>inspires</b> or <b>fixes an exact rule</b>. Maya is planning a website for a reggae festival in Montego Bay.</p>","main":"Which planning document does this belong in?","bins":["Rough sketch","Mood board","Style guide"],"items":[{"t":"Tone of voice: warm and proud; no slang in headlines.","bin":2,"fb":"Tone of voice rules are part of a style guide."},{"t":"Three loose colour swatches beside sunset photos to suggest a warm evening mood.","bin":1,"fb":"Colours without exact values, used for feeling, belong on the mood board."},{"t":"Photos of old sound-system posters, red, gold and green fabric and the word \"roots\".","bin":1,"fb":"A collage of images and words that sets the feeling is a mood board."},{"t":"An annotated rough of the line-up page, with a note saying \"photos to be decided\".","bin":0,"fb":"Still open and unfinished, so it is a rough sketch."},{"t":"A pencil layout of the ticket page with arrows saying \"button goes here\".","bin":0,"fb":"An annotated, unfinished layout explores structure: a rough sketch."},{"t":"Six stamp-sized thumbnail layouts of the homepage.","bin":0,"fb":"Thumbnails explore many layouts quickly: rough sketches."},{"t":"The logo must always have clear space around it equal to the height of its first letter.","bin":2,"fb":"Logo clear-space rules belong in the style guide."},{"t":"A photo of a hand-painted dancehall sign, pinned to suggest a hand-made feel.","bin":1,"fb":"It inspires a direction without fixing a rule: mood board."},{"t":"Primary green is #1B7F3B on screen, with a matching CMYK value for printed posters.","bin":2,"fb":"Exact colour values are a style guide rule."}],"win":"Excellent. The same topic, such as colour, can appear in every document. What matters is whether it explores (sketch), inspires (mood board) or fixes an exact rule (style guide)."}},{"type":"learn","title":"Storyboarding: planning the visual sequence","html":"<p>A <b>storyboard</b> is a sequence of drawn frames (panels) that shows what the audience will see and hear, shot by shot, in a video, animation or interactive product. The SBA mark scheme rewards a storyboard, rough design, wireframe or mock-up.</p>\n<p><b>What each storyboard frame should include</b></p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Frame or scene number</b> and the <b>duration</b> (for example, Scene 3, 0:08 to 0:12).</li>\n<li>A <b>sketch</b> of the visual: subject, background and composition.</li>\n<li><b>Shot type</b>: extreme long shot (ELS), long shot (LS), medium shot (MS), close-up (CU), extreme close-up (ECU).</li>\n<li><b>Camera angle and movement</b>: high angle, low angle, eye level; pan, tilt, zoom, tracking.</li>\n<li><b>Audio</b>: dialogue or narration, music and sound effects.</li>\n<li><b>On-screen text</b> or graphics, and the <b>transition</b> to the next frame (cut, fade, dissolve).</li>\n</ul>\n<p><b>Example: a 30-second ad for Rum Point Bay Tours in the Cayman Islands (your SBA ad must also be 30 seconds)</b></p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th>Frame</th><th>Time</th><th>Visual and shot</th><th>Audio</th></tr>\n<tr><td>1</td><td>0:00 to 0:05</td><td>ELS, drone pan over the North Sound</td><td>Soft steel pan music fades in</td></tr>\n<tr><td>2</td><td>0:05 to 0:12</td><td>MS, smiling guide on the boat, eye level</td><td>VO: \"Discover the island like a local.\"</td></tr>\n<tr><td>3</td><td>0:12 to 0:20</td><td>CU, guests tasting Cayman rum cake; quick cuts</td><td>Laughter, music builds</td></tr>\n<tr><td>4</td><td>0:20 to 0:30</td><td>Logo, website and WhatsApp number on brand colour; fade out</td><td>VO: \"Book today.\"</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<p><b>Related planning tools</b></p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Script</b>: the written words, dialogue and directions. A storyboard is usually drawn from a script.</li>\n<li><b>Shot list</b>: a checklist of every shot needed, used on the day of filming.</li>\n<li><b>Animatic</b>: the storyboard frames timed to a rough soundtrack, to test pacing before animation (common in studios that work with Blender or Adobe Animate).</li>\n<li><b>Wireframe and flowchart</b>: for websites and apps, the \"storyboard\" is a set of screen layouts plus a flowchart showing how users move between screens (Modules 2 and 3).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Storyboards let the whole team, and the client, agree on the sequence before anyone films. Malik at the Island Pulse Media says one hour of storyboarding saves a day of reshoots.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> In Paper 02, a storyboard with stick figures still scores well if every frame has a number, timing, shot type and audio note. Drawing skill is not the main thing being marked; planning is.</p>","plain":"<p>A storyboard is a set of drawn boxes that shows what the viewer will see and hear, one shot at a time. Each frame has a number, a time, a sketch, the shot type, the sound and any words on screen. It works like a comic strip plan. A team filming a cricket promo in Guyana would storyboard the bowler's run-up, the shot and the crowd before filming.</p>","deeper":"<p>A storyboard is a communication tool, so clarity matters more than drawing skill. Show camera movement with arrows, mark the transition between frames and give timings that add up to the total length: a 30-second advert whose frames add up to 42 seconds shows poor planning. Grade One answers also show how the documents link. The script supplies the words, the storyboard shows the visuals, the shot list turns the frames into a checklist for the shoot and the animatic tests the timing. For interactive products a storyboard alone is not enough, because users choose their own path; a wireframe and a flowchart are needed to show the screens and how users move between them.</p>"},{"type":"pick","game":true,"title":"What belongs in a storyboard frame?","intro":"<p>Malik is checking a student's storyboard for a 30-second Emancipation Day video for the Guyana Heritage Collective. Pick the details that a well-annotated storyboard frame should include.</p>","q":"Select ALL the details that belong in a storyboard frame annotation.","lines":["Scene 2, 0:06 to 0:12","Close-up of hands beating a drum, low angle","Narration: \"We remember, we rise.\"","Transition: slow dissolve to Scene 3","The total budget for the whole project","A list of every team member's phone number","On-screen text: \"1 July: Emancipation Day\"","The client's invoice and payment terms"],"correct":[0,1,2,3,6],"win":"Correct. A storyboard frame shows number and timing, the visual with shot type and angle, audio, on-screen text and the transition. Budgets, contacts and invoices belong in other planning documents.","miss":"Not quite. Ask: does this detail describe what the audience will see or hear at this moment? Budgets, phone lists and invoices are project management documents, not storyboard content.","tutor":[{"h":"See and hear","p":"<p>A storyboard frame records what appears on screen and what is heard, and when.</p>"},{"h":"Other documents","p":"<p>Money and contacts belong in the budget and the work plan.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Storyboard details (Foundation)","q":"Select the details that belong in a storyboard frame.","lines":["Scene 2, 0:06 to 0:12","Close-up of a drum, low angle","The total project budget","Narration: \"We remember, we rise.\""],"correct":[0,1,3],"win":"Yes. Timing, shot and audio belong in the frame.","miss":"The budget is not part of a storyboard frame. Try again."},"up":{"title":"What belongs in a storyboard frame? (Challenge)","bloom":"Analyse","intro":"<p>Malik is checking a storyboard for a 30-second Emancipation Day video. This time some details belong in OTHER planning documents: the production schedule, the equipment list, the clearances file or the budget. Pick only what belongs in a storyboard frame.</p>","q":"Select ALL the details that belong in a storyboard frame annotation, and none that belong in other planning documents.","lines":["Frame 4, 0:15 to 0:20","MS, low angle, slow tilt up from the drum to the drummer's face","SFX: crowd cheering under the music","Cut to Frame 5","Caption: \"Emancipation Day, Georgetown, 1 August\"","Crew call time: 6:30 a.m. at the National Park","Equipment: two lavalier microphones and one gimbal","Release forms collected from all the drummers","Narration: \"Our story, our rhythm.\"","The editor's day rate and invoice date"],"correct":[0,1,2,3,4,8],"win":"Correct. Frame number and timing, the shot with its angle and movement, sound, captions, narration and the transition all describe what the audience sees and hears. Call times go in the production schedule, gear in the equipment list, releases in the clearances file and rates in the budget.","miss":"Not quite. For each line ask: does it describe what the audience will see or hear at this moment? Call times, equipment, release forms and rates are real planning details, but they belong in other documents."}},{"type":"learn","title":"Timelines, Gantt charts and the production schedule","html":"<p>You must be able to set <b>timelines</b>, a <b>production schedule</b> and a <b>work plan</b>, and use <b>Gantt charts</b> and <b>milestones</b>.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Timeline</b>: the overall sequence of phases and dates from start to launch.</li>\n<li><b>Milestone</b>: a key checkpoint with no duration, such as \"storyboard approved\" or \"proposal submitted\".</li>\n<li><b>Production schedule</b>: the detailed day-by-day plan of what is produced, where, when and by whom (for example, filming at Brimstone Hill Fortress on Saturday at 7 a.m. before the heat and crowds).</li>\n<li><b>Work plan</b>: lists every task, who is responsible, the resources needed and the deadline. In group SBAs it shows each member's contribution.</li>\n<li><b>Gantt chart</b>: a bar chart (named after Henry Gantt, early 1900s) with tasks listed down the side and time across the top. Each bar shows when a task starts and ends; overlapping bars show tasks running at the same time.</li>\n</ul>\n<svg viewBox=\"0 0 340 150\" width=\"100%\" style=\"max-width:460px\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Simple Gantt chart with five tasks across four weeks\">\n<g fill=\"currentColor\" font-size=\"10\">\n<text x=\"110\" y=\"14\">Wk 1</text><text x=\"165\" y=\"14\">Wk 2</text><text x=\"220\" y=\"14\">Wk 3</text><text x=\"275\" y=\"14\">Wk 4</text>\n<text x=\"4\" y=\"36\">Research</text><text x=\"4\" y=\"60\">Storyboard</text><text x=\"4\" y=\"84\">Filming</text><text x=\"4\" y=\"108\">Editing</text><text x=\"4\" y=\"132\">Launch</text>\n</g>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.5\">\n<rect x=\"100\" y=\"26\" width=\"60\" height=\"14\"/><rect x=\"140\" y=\"50\" width=\"50\" height=\"14\"/>\n<rect x=\"190\" y=\"74\" width=\"50\" height=\"14\"/><rect x=\"230\" y=\"98\" width=\"60\" height=\"14\"/>\n<path d=\"M300 118 l8 8 l-8 8 l-8 -8 z\"/>\n<line x1=\"100\" y1=\"20\" x2=\"100\" y2=\"140\" stroke-dasharray=\"2 3\"/>\n</g>\n</svg>\n<p>The diamond on the last row is a common symbol for a <b>milestone</b>. Notice <b>dependencies</b>: editing cannot start until filming has begun, so its bar starts later. The longest chain of dependent tasks is the <b>critical path</b>; a delay on it delays the whole project. Build in <b>buffer time</b> for Caribbean realities such as rain on a filming day, power cuts or slow uploads.</p>\n<p><b>Planning goes further than the schedule.</b> In pre-production, teams also prepare the paperwork for the <b>core elements</b>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Resources</b>: a <b>budget</b> (equipment hire, software subscriptions, props, transport, data), an <b>equipment list</b> (camera or phone, tripod, microphone, lights, storage, charged batteries), personnel, facilities and locations;</li>\n<li><b>Clearances</b>: <b>permits</b> to film in public or on private property, and <b>releases</b> (model release forms) for people filmed;</li>\n<li><b>Copyright and IP</b>: music licences or royalty-free tracks, cleared images and scripts;</li>\n<li><b>Codes of practice and regulations</b>: truthful advertising, age-appropriate content and respect for culture;</li>\n<li><b>Health and safety</b>: a risk assessment when filming near roads, the sea or at night.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Tools: Excel or Google Sheets (with coloured cells), Canva Gantt templates, Trello, Microsoft Planner and TeamGantt.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> Your SBA Progress and Process section (12 marks) rewards evidence of incremental progress. A Gantt chart updated each week, with actual versus planned dates, is excellent evidence.</p>","plain":"<p>A timeline shows the main phases and dates of a project. A Gantt chart is a bar chart: tasks go down the side, weeks go across the top, and each bar shows when a task starts and ends. A milestone is a key checkpoint, often drawn as a diamond. Add spare days, called buffer time, in case of rain or power cuts, as film crews in Dominica know.</p>","deeper":"<p>The key idea is <b>dependency</b>. If editing depends on filming and filming depends on the storyboard, those tasks form a chain. The longest chain of dependent tasks is the <b>critical path</b>: any delay on it moves the launch date, while a delay on a task off the critical path (such as designing a thumbnail) may not. That is why buffer time is placed before key milestones, not scattered at random. A common error is to draw every bar from Week 1 to the end, which shows no planning at all. Keep planned and actual dates side by side; the gap between them shows that the team monitored progress and adjusted the schedule.</p>"},{"type":"multi","game":true,"title":"Reading the production plan","intro":"<p>Jerome, Malik and Keisha at the Island Pulse Media are planning a promotional video for the Klein Bonaire Dive Centre. Answer each question about their planning.</p>","items":[{"text":"On their Gantt chart, \"Client approves storyboard\" is shown as a diamond with no length. This is a...","options":["dependency","milestone","work plan"],"answer":1,"fb":"A milestone is a key checkpoint with no duration, often drawn as a diamond."},{"text":"Editing cannot begin until underwater filming is complete. This relationship is called a...","options":["dependency","mood board","buffer"],"answer":0,"fb":"When one task must wait for another, the tasks are dependent."},{"text":"The document that lists each task, who is responsible and the deadline is the...","options":["storyboard","work plan","style guide"],"answer":1,"fb":"A work plan assigns tasks, people, resources and deadlines."},{"text":"Filming is booked for 7 a.m. Saturday at the reef, with a back-up date the next weekend. Where is this recorded?","options":["the mood board","the production schedule","the problem statement"],"answer":1,"fb":"The production schedule gives the detailed when, where and who of production."},{"text":"The divers who appear in the video must sign a form allowing their image to be used. This is a...","options":["safety briefing","shot list","model release form"],"answer":2,"fb":"A model release form gives legal permission to use a person's image, a key legal consideration in pre-production."},{"text":"Two extra days are added before the deadline in case of rough seas. This is...","options":["buffer time","a milestone","the critical path"],"answer":0,"fb":"Buffer time protects the deadline from delays such as bad weather."}],"win":"Excellent. You can read and explain a production plan: milestones, dependencies, work plans, schedules, buffers and legal paperwork.","tutor":[{"h":"Diamonds","p":"<p>On a Gantt chart, a diamond marks a point in time rather than a task.</p>"},{"h":"Who, what, when","p":"<p>The work plan assigns people to tasks; the production schedule fixes when and where production happens.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Production plan (Foundation)","items":[{"text":"A key checkpoint on a Gantt chart with no duration is a...","options":["task","milestone"],"answer":1,"fb":"Milestones mark key points in time."},{"text":"Extra days added in case of delays are called...","options":["a dependency","buffer time"],"answer":1,"fb":"Buffer time protects the deadline."},{"text":"A form allowing a person's image to be used is a...","options":["model release form","mood board"],"answer":0,"fb":"It gives legal permission."}]},"up":{"title":"Reading the production plan (Challenge)","bloom":"Analyse","intro":"<p>Harder questions about dependencies, the critical path and protecting the deadline for Klein Bonaire Dive Centre's promotional video.</p>","items":[{"text":"Research (Week 1), storyboard (Week 2), filming (Weeks 3 to 4) and editing (Weeks 4 to 5) each depend on the task before. The storyboard is one week late and there is no buffer. What happens to the launch?","options":["It moves back one week because the storyboard is on the critical path","It stays the same because only one task is late","Only editing is affected, not the launch","Filming can simply start without the storyboard"],"answer":0,"fb":"Every task depends on the one before, so they all lie on the critical path. With no buffer, a one-week delay moves the launch back one week."},{"text":"The music licence and the voice-over can be done while filming is happening. On the Gantt chart these bars should...","options":["be drawn as diamonds","be placed on the critical path","come after the launch milestone","overlap with the filming weeks"],"answer":3,"fb":"Tasks that can run at the same time are shown as overlapping bars."},{"text":"Which of these is a milestone rather than a task?","options":["Recording the voice-over","Editing the rough cut","Client signs off the final cut","Filming at the reef"],"answer":2,"fb":"A sign-off is a checkpoint with no duration. The others take time, so they are tasks."},{"text":"Rain is forecast for most of the filming week in September. The BEST response in the plan is to...","options":["remove the evaluation stage to save time","start editing before filming","add buffer time and a back-up filming date to the production schedule","delete the sign-off milestone"],"answer":2,"fb":"Buffer time and a back-up date protect the deadline without cutting quality or approval."},{"text":"A group of four sixth-form students shares the work. Which document BEST shows the teacher who was responsible for each task?","options":["the mood board","the style guide","the work plan","the storyboard"],"answer":2,"fb":"A work plan names the person responsible, the resources and the deadline for every task."},{"text":"Which statement about the critical path is correct?","options":["It is the longest chain of dependent tasks, so a delay on it delays the whole project","It lists only the tasks that have buffer time","It is the shortest set of tasks in the project","It is another name for the production schedule"],"answer":0,"fb":"The critical path is the longest chain of dependent tasks; it sets the earliest possible finish date."}],"win":"Excellent. You can analyse a production plan: dependencies, overlapping tasks, milestones, buffer time, the critical path and who is responsible for what."}},{"type":"learn","title":"Factors affecting creation and the virtuous cycle","html":"<p>Even a well-planned project is shaped by things the team must manage. There are <b>seven factors affecting the creation of digital media content</b>.</p>\n<ol>\n<li><b>Technical factors</b>: equipment quality (cameras, microphones, editing software) and Internet bandwidth for online production or distribution.</li>\n<li><b>Creative direction</b>: style, tone, target audience and branding requirements; artistic vision (colour palette, sound design, visual effects).</li>\n<li><b>Budget constraints</b>: may limit location choices, talent, gear or post-production capabilities.</li>\n<li><b>Time constraints</b>: deadlines may rush planning, leading to reduced quality.</li>\n<li><b>Legal and ethical factors</b>: compliance with copyright, image rights and content regulations.</li>\n<li><b>Team collaboration</b>: communication and coordination affect the smooth flow of production.</li>\n<li><b>External conditions</b>: weather (for outdoor shoots), availability of key personnel, or current events.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>For example, a hurricane watch in September (external condition) can cancel a beach shoot, and slow upload speeds (technical) can delay sending large video files to a client in Aruba.</p>\n<p><b>The virtuous cycle</b></p>\n<svg viewBox=\"0 0 320 220\" width=\"100%\" style=\"max-width:420px\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Virtuous cycle: media, technology, audience and impact in a loop, with product and optimise beside technology, markets and research beside impact, strategy and business development beside media\">\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.5\">\n<rect x=\"120\" y=\"10\" width=\"80\" height=\"30\" rx=\"6\"/><rect x=\"230\" y=\"95\" width=\"80\" height=\"30\" rx=\"6\"/><rect x=\"120\" y=\"180\" width=\"80\" height=\"30\" rx=\"6\"/><rect x=\"10\" y=\"95\" width=\"80\" height=\"30\" rx=\"6\"/>\n<path d=\"M200 30 Q260 40 268 92\"/><path d=\"M268 128 Q260 185 202 192\"/><path d=\"M118 192 Q60 185 52 128\"/><path d=\"M52 92 Q60 40 118 28\"/>\n<path d=\"M262 86 l6 8 l4 -9\"/><path d=\"M210 186 l-9 6 l9 4\"/><path d=\"M58 136 l-6 -8 l-4 9\"/><path d=\"M110 24 l9 4 l-8 5\"/>\n</g>\n<g fill=\"currentColor\" font-size=\"11\" text-anchor=\"middle\">\n<text x=\"160\" y=\"29\">Media</text><text x=\"270\" y=\"114\">Technology</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"199\">Impact</text><text x=\"50\" y=\"114\">Audience</text>\n<text x=\"160\" y=\"62\" font-size=\"9\">Strategy, Biz Dev</text><text x=\"250\" y=\"150\" font-size=\"9\">Product, Optimise</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"165\" font-size=\"9\">Markets, Research</text><text x=\"80\" y=\"76\" font-size=\"9\">Research, Strategy</text>\n</g>\n</svg>\n<p>In this diagram four core areas, <b>media, technology, audience and impact</b>, interact dynamically to drive growth and improvement. Around them are key business functions:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Product and Optimise</b> enhance technology.</li>\n<li><b>Markets and Research</b> influence impact.</li>\n<li><b>Strategy and Biz Dev</b> (business development) support media.</li>\n<li><b>Audience</b> is informed by research and strategy.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Each function feeds into and strengthens the others, creating <b>continuous feedback loops</b> that fuel innovation, efficiency and scalability. At Island Pulse Media, a new podcast (media) is streamed through an improved app (technology), grows a Caribbean listenership (audience), and listener data and sponsorship (impact) fund the next series.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> \"Discuss factors\" questions reward a factor, an example and its effect. Pair factors for depth: \"Budget constraints meant no drone, so the team used a phone gimbal; time constraints then left only one day for editing.\"</p>","plain":"<p>Many things can affect a media project: equipment and Internet speed, the creative style, money, time, the law, how well the team works together and outside events such as weather. The virtuous cycle shows how media, technology, audience and impact feed each other. A good podcast attracts listeners, listeners attract sponsors, and the sponsors' money pays for better equipment and the next series.</p>","deeper":"<p>Factors rarely act alone, and examiners reward answers that show the chain reaction. A small budget can force cheaper equipment (technical), which needs more time in post-production (time), which may hurt quality (creative direction). Strong answers also say how each factor could be managed: a back-up date for weather, shared cloud folders for team collaboration, royalty-free music for legal risk. The virtuous cycle is a business idea that explains why media companies reinvest. If one link breaks, for example poor audience research leading to weak impact, the whole cycle slows. In a \"discuss\" question, give the factor, an example from the scenario, its effect and a way to reduce it.</p>"},{"type":"match","game":true,"title":"Factors affecting creation and the virtuous cycle","intro":"<p>Malik at the Island Pulse Media is reviewing problems from a shoot for the Jacmel Food Festival in Haiti. Match each problem to its factor, then complete the virtuous cycle links.</p>","pairs":[{"l":"Technical factors","r":"The old microphone picked up wind noise, and the slow connection delayed the upload"},{"l":"Creative direction","r":"The client wanted a bright, upbeat tone with the festival's brand colours"},{"l":"Budget constraints","r":"There was no money to hire a professional chef as presenter"},{"l":"Time constraints","r":"A two-day deadline rushed the planning and lowered the quality"},{"l":"Legal and ethical factors","r":"Diners' faces could not be shown without image rights cleared"},{"l":"External conditions","r":"A tropical storm warning moved the outdoor shoot"},{"l":"Product and Optimise","r":"Enhance technology in the virtuous cycle"},{"l":"Strategy and Biz Dev","r":"Support media in the virtuous cycle"}],"win":"Excellent. You can identify the factors affecting digital media creation and explain how business functions feed the virtuous cycle.","tutor":[{"h":"Rights, not money","p":"<p>When the problem is permission or regulation, it is not a budget problem.</p>"},{"h":"The cycle","p":"<p>Which functions improve the technology, and which ones support the media?</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Factors (Foundation)","pairs":[{"l":"External conditions","r":"Bad weather cancels an outdoor shoot"},{"l":"Budget constraints","r":"No money to hire extra gear"},{"l":"Team collaboration","r":"Poor communication slows production"},{"l":"Markets and Research","r":"Influence impact in the virtuous cycle"}]},"up":{"title":"Factors affecting creation and the virtuous cycle (Challenge)","bloom":"Analyse","intro":"<p>Harder: all seven factors appear, the examples are subtler, and two virtuous-cycle links are included. Match each card to the factor or link it shows.</p>","pairs":[{"l":"Technical factors","r":"The 4K files were too large to upload over the hotel Wi-Fi, so the client in Aruba waited two days"},{"l":"Creative direction","r":"The brand guide demanded calm pastel colours, but the director wanted neon Carnival tones"},{"l":"Budget constraints","r":"The team filmed with a phone gimbal because hiring a drone cost too much"},{"l":"Time constraints","r":"The client moved the launch forward a week, so the colour grade was skipped"},{"l":"Legal and ethical factors","r":"A vendor asked not to be filmed, so the scene was reshot without her stall"},{"l":"Team collaboration","r":"The editor cut last week's script because the update was only sent to the director"},{"l":"External conditions","r":"The lead presenter fell ill on the shoot day, and a back-up host stepped in"},{"l":"Markets and Research","r":"Influence impact in the virtuous cycle"},{"l":"Audience","r":"Is informed by research and strategy in the virtuous cycle"}],"win":"Excellent. You identified all seven factors from realistic problems and placed two links of the virtuous cycle correctly."}},{"type":"learn","title":"Prototypes, idea validation and implementation","html":"<p>A <b>prototype</b> is an early, testable version of a product used to check an idea before full production. <b>Idea validation</b> is testing that prototype with real members of the target audience to confirm that the idea solves the problem and that people want and can use it.</p>\n<p><b>Levels of fidelity</b></p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th>Type</th><th>What it looks like</th><th>Best for</th></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Low-fidelity</b></td><td>Paper sketches, sticky notes, a rough storyboard or animatic</td><td>Testing the concept and flow quickly and cheaply</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Medium-fidelity</b></td><td>Clickable wireframes in Figma, a rough video cut</td><td>Testing navigation, timing and structure</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>High-fidelity</b></td><td>Near-final colours, images and interactions; a polished draft video</td><td>Testing look, feel and detailed usability</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<p>A <b>minimum viable product (MVP)</b> is the simplest working version that delivers the core value, released to learn from real users.</p>\n<p><b>How to validate an idea</b></p>\n<ol>\n<li>Decide what you want to learn (for example, \"Can visitors find the nearest craft stall in under one minute?\").</li>\n<li>Recruit five to eight real target users, such as classmates, customers at the client's shop or relatives in the right age group.</li>\n<li>Give them tasks and <b>observe</b> without helping. Use a think-aloud approach: they say what they are thinking.</li>\n<li>Collect feedback: short surveys, interviews, ratings, A/B tests of two versions, or analytics such as clicks and watch time.</li>\n<li><b>Iterate</b>: fix the problems and test again. This is the feedback loop you met in Unit 1.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Example: Jerome at the Island Pulse Media tested a paper prototype of a Paramaribo bus-route app with commuters at the Heiligenweg bus station. Users kept looking for the fare first, not the map, so he moved fares to the home screen before writing any code.</p>\n<p><b>Implementation</b> is moving from a validated prototype to the finished product. It covers:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Production</b>: filming, recording, illustrating, coding and building assets, following the style guide and storyboard.</li>\n<li><b>Post-production</b>: editing, colour correction, sound mixing, testing on different devices, exporting in the right formats.</li>\n<li><b>Launch or distribution</b>: publishing to YouTube, Instagram, a website or an app store, with a promotion plan.</li>\n<li><b>Evaluation</b>: measuring results against the goal set in the problem definition, then recommending improvements.</li>\n</ul>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> \"Justify the use of a prototype\" questions want reasons: it reduces cost and risk, reveals usability problems early, gets client approval and gives evidence that users want the product. Always tie the reason to the scenario.</p>","plain":"<p>A prototype is an early, rough version of a product that you can test. It can be paper sketches or a clickable mock-up. Validation means testing it with real users to see whether the idea works and whether they want it. Then you fix the problems and test again. A ferry company in St. Lucia could test a paper booking screen with passengers before paying for an app.</p>","deeper":"<p>Match the prototype's fidelity to the question you need answered. A paper prototype answers \"does the flow make sense?\" cheaply, but it cannot tell you whether a colour scheme looks professional; that needs a high-fidelity version. Testing too late is a common trap: once a product is fully built, people are reluctant to throw work away, so problems survive. Five to eight users usually reveal most usability problems, but they must be real target users, not the design team. An MVP is different from a prototype: it is a working product released to the public to learn from real use. In a \"justify\" answer, link the prototype to cost, risk and the scenario.</p>"},{"type":"scenario","game":true,"title":"Pre-production for a Carnival app","intro":"<p>You are a junior designer at Island Pulse Media. Carnival City Committee wants a digital product to help people enjoy Antigua Carnival. Zara asks you to lead pre-production. Make the best decisions.</p>","nodes":{"start":{"text":"<p>The committee chair says: \"Just build us an app with everything on it, fast.\" What do you do first?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Open Android Studio and start coding an app straight away.","to":"bad1","fb":"Jumping to production skips analysis and definition, so you may build the wrong product."},{"t":"Hold a short meeting to agree the brief: the problem, the users, the goal, the budget and the deadline.","to":"n2","fb":"Good. Acceptance and definition come first; a clear brief prevents wasted work."}]},"n2":{"text":"<p>You survey 60 residents and visitors. Most want to know when and where bands and jump-ups pass, and many have limited data. Which problem statement do you write?</p>","choices":[{"t":"\"Carnival-goers need a quick, low-data way to know where and when events happen because they miss events and waste data searching social media.\"","to":"n3","fb":"Strong. It names users, need and insight, and keeps the solution open."},{"t":"\"The committee needs a beautiful app with a 3D Carnival City.\"","to":"bad2","fb":"This names a solution and ignores the users' data limits found in your research."}]},"n3":{"text":"<p>In ideation your team suggests a full app, a WhatsApp alert channel, a printable QR map and a live TikTok feed. You select a low-data web map with WhatsApp alerts. What next?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Skip planning: Carnival is only six weeks away.","to":"bad3","fb":"A tight deadline is exactly why you need a schedule. Without one, tasks clash and deadlines slip."},{"t":"Create rough sketches, a mood board and a style guide, a storyboard for the 30-second promo, and a Gantt chart with milestones and buffer time.","to":"n4","fb":"Excellent. Planning designs and timelines before production keeps the team aligned and on time."}]},"n4":{"text":"<p>Maya has a clickable Figma prototype of the map. Before full development, what do you do?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Ask the team if they like it, then launch.","to":"bad4","fb":"The team are not the target audience. Only real users can validate the idea."},{"t":"Test it with eight real Carnival-goers on their own phones, observe them, collect feedback and fix problems.","to":"good","fb":"Perfect. Validating with target users catches problems while they are still cheap to fix."}]},"good":{"text":"<p><b>Success.</b> The map launches on time and works well on low data. A Grade One answer would explain that you followed the full pre-production process: brief, research, problem definition, ideation and selection, planning designs, storyboard, schedule, then prototype validation before implementation.</p>","end":"good"},"bad1":{"text":"<p><b>Wrong product.</b> After two weeks of coding, the committee realises they wanted event times, not a ticket shop. Try again and define the problem first.</p>","end":"bad"},"bad2":{"text":"<p><b>Too heavy.</b> The 3D Carnival City drains data and battery, and few people download it. Try again and let your audience research shape the problem statement.</p>","end":"bad"},"bad3":{"text":"<p><b>Missed deadline.</b> Filming and coding clash, the promo is late and nobody agrees on colours. Try again and plan before you produce.</p>","end":"bad"},"bad4":{"text":"<p><b>Confused users.</b> On launch day visitors cannot find the \"Now playing\" button. Try again and validate the prototype with real users.</p>","end":"bad"}},"tutor":[{"h":"Order matters","p":"<p>Brief and research, then problem, then ideas, then plans, then prototype.</p>"},{"h":"Who decides?","p":"<p>Real target users, not the team, validate a prototype.</p>"},{"h":"Constraints","p":"<p>Data limits and deadlines should shape your choices, not be ignored.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Pre-production for a Carnival app (Foundation)","intro":"<p>A shorter version: the Carnival committee wants a digital product.</p>","nodes":{"start":{"text":"<p>The chair says \"Build an app now.\" What do you do first?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Agree the brief and research the users.","to":"n2","fb":"Yes. Define before you design."},{"t":"Start coding immediately.","to":"bad","fb":"You might build the wrong thing."}]},"n2":{"text":"<p>You have a prototype. What next?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Launch it without testing.","to":"bad","fb":"Untested products often confuse users."},{"t":"Test it with real Carnival-goers.","to":"good","fb":"Yes. Real users validate the idea."}]},"good":{"text":"<p><b>Well done.</b> You defined the problem and validated the prototype.</p>","end":"good"},"bad":{"text":"<p><b>Not quite.</b> Plan and test before you produce. Try again.</p>","end":"bad"}}},"up":{"title":"Pre-production for a Carnival app (Challenge)","bloom":"Evaluate","intro":"<p>A tougher brief. Carnival City Committee has <b>US$3,000</b> and <b>six weeks</b>. Every choice below sounds reasonable; pick the one a Grade One designer would defend. Some choices end the project early with a partial result.</p>","nodes":{"start":{"text":"<p>The committee chair says: \"Build us an app with everything on it, fast. Budget US$3,000, six weeks.\" Zara asks you to open the project. Which first step shows the best judgement?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Hold a briefing to agree the problem, the users, a measurable goal, the budget, the deadline and who signs off each stage.","to":"n2","fb":"Good. Acceptance and definition come first, and agreeing sign-offs now prevents arguments later."},{"t":"Write a detailed technical specification for a native app so the developer can start on Monday.","to":"bad1","fb":"This chooses the solution before anyone has analysed the problem or the users."},{"t":"Ask the chair for a list of features and promise to deliver all of them.","to":"ok1","fb":"Listening to the client matters, but promising every feature with no analysis risks the budget and the deadline."}]},"n2":{"text":"<p>Your survey of 60 people finds that most want live band locations, 70% use prepaid data, many visitors speak Spanish or French, and older residents dislike small text. Which problem statement and goal do you write?</p>","choices":[{"t":"\"Carnival-goers need a way to find events because they miss them. Goal: make Carnival better.\"","to":"ok2","fb":"The user and need are there, but your research findings and a measurable goal are missing."},{"t":"\"The committee needs a 3D Carnival City app with augmented reality floats.\"","to":"bad2","fb":"This names a heavy solution and ignores the data limits your research found."},{"t":"\"Carnival-goers need a low-data, multilingual way to find where bands and jump-ups are, because they miss events and waste data searching social media. Goal: 3,000 users, and 80% of tested users finding the next event in under one minute, by the last day of Carnival.\"","to":"n3","fb":"Strong. Users, need and insight come from your research, the format is left open and the goal is measurable."}]},"n3":{"text":"<p>Ideation produced a native app, a WhatsApp alert channel, a printable QR map, a live TikTok feed and a low-data web map. How do you select?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Choose the native app because it will look most professional at the launch.","to":"bad3","fb":"Looks are not a selection criterion on their own. A native app is the most expensive and slowest option."},{"t":"Score each idea against the goal for feasibility in six weeks, cost within US$3,000, data use and risk. The low-data web map with WhatsApp alerts scores highest.","to":"n4","fb":"Excellent. Selection compares options against the objectives, not personal taste."},{"t":"Choose the TikTok feed because TikTok is the most popular platform with young people.","to":"ok3","fb":"Popular, but it ignores older residents, visitors without the app and the data problem."}]},"n4":{"text":"<p>The map needs the parade route from the police (due Week 2), and filming the 30-second promo needs Carnival City to be built (Week 3). What goes into your Gantt chart?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Tasks linked by their dependencies (route data before the map build; Carnival City built before filming), sign-off milestones and a buffer week before launch.","to":"n5","fb":"Good. Dependencies and buffer time protect the critical path."},{"t":"One bar for each team member, all running from Week 1 to Week 6.","to":"bad4","fb":"Bars that cover the whole project show no sequence, no dependencies and no milestones."}]},"n5":{"text":"<p>Maya's clickable Figma prototype is ready. Who do you test it with, and how?</p>","choices":[{"t":"The five members of the design team, on the office Wi-Fi.","to":"bad5","fb":"The team knows the design too well, and office Wi-Fi hides the data problem."},{"t":"Eight Carnival-goers of different ages, including two Spanish or French speakers and two older residents, on their own prepaid phones, with set tasks and think-aloud.","to":"good","fb":"Perfect. The test group matches the audience your research found, on their real devices."},{"t":"The committee chair only, because she is the client and must approve it.","to":"ok4","fb":"Client approval matters, but one person is not a typical user. You still need to validate with the audience."}]},"good":{"text":"<p><b>Grade One project.</b> The map launches on time, works on prepaid data and in three languages, and 84% of tested users find the next event in under a minute. You can justify every pre-production decision with evidence from your research.</p>","end":"good"},"ok1":{"text":"<p><b>Over budget.</b> The feature list grows every week and the project runs out of money before testing. A partial success: next time, define the problem before agreeing the features.</p>","end":"ok"},"ok2":{"text":"<p><b>Hard to evaluate.</b> The product launches, but with no measurable goal nobody can say whether it worked. Next time, turn your research into a precise statement and a measurable goal.</p>","end":"ok"},"ok3":{"text":"<p><b>Narrow reach.</b> Young users enjoy the feed, but older residents and many visitors never see it. Next time, select against the whole audience and the goal.</p>","end":"ok"},"ok4":{"text":"<p><b>Approved but untested.</b> The chair likes the map, but visitors struggle with the language menu on launch day. Next time, validate with real users as well as the client.</p>","end":"ok"},"bad1":{"text":"<p><b>Wrong product.</b> After two weeks of coding, the committee realises they wanted event times, not a ticket shop. Try again and define the problem first.</p>","end":"bad"},"bad2":{"text":"<p><b>Too heavy.</b> The 3D app drains data and battery and few people download it. Try again and let your research shape the problem statement.</p>","end":"bad"},"bad3":{"text":"<p><b>Out of time.</b> The native app is only half built when Carnival opens. Try again and select against feasibility, cost and the deadline.</p>","end":"bad"},"bad4":{"text":"<p><b>Missed deadline.</b> The map build waits for route data nobody chased, and the promo shoot clashes with editing. Try again and plan the dependencies.</p>","end":"bad"},"bad5":{"text":"<p><b>Confused users.</b> On launch day, visitors on slow prepaid data cannot load the map. Try again and validate with real users on their own phones.</p>","end":"bad"}}}},{"type":"mcq","title":"Why validate a prototype?","q":"Bridgetown Fit Club in Barbados has a paper prototype of a workout app. The owner wants to skip testing and go straight to full development. Which is the BEST justification for validating the prototype first?","options":[{"t":"Testing with real members reveals usability problems and confirms demand while changes are still cheap, reducing the risk of building an app nobody uses.","ok":true,"fb":"Correct. This links validation to cost, risk and user needs, which is what \"justify\" requires."},{"t":"Testing is required by app stores before any app is published.","fb":"App stores review apps for policy and security, but they do not require prototype testing with users."},{"t":"Testing makes the app look more professional.","fb":"Validation is about whether the idea works for users, not about appearance."},{"t":"Testing means the team will not need a Gantt chart.","fb":"Validation and scheduling are separate pre-production tasks. You still need a schedule."}],"tutor":[{"h":"Cost of change","p":"<p>Is it cheaper to change paper or finished code?</p>"},{"h":"Justify","p":"<p>A justification gives a reason and its benefit for this business.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Why validate? (Foundation)","q":"Why should a team test a prototype with real users?","options":[{"t":"To make it look nicer.","fb":"Testing is about usability and demand, not looks."},{"t":"Because app stores require it.","fb":"App stores do not require user testing."},{"t":"To find problems early, when they are cheap to fix.","ok":true,"fb":"Yes. Early fixes save time and money."}]},"up":{"title":"Why validate a prototype? (Challenge)","bloom":"Evaluate","q":"Bridgetown Fit Club in Barbados has a paper prototype of a class-booking app. The owner says: \"Testing is a waste of money. We will fix any problems after launch.\" Which response BEST justifies validating the prototype first?","options":[{"t":"Validation guarantees the app will succeed, so no evaluation will be needed after launch.","fb":"No test guarantees success. Evaluation after launch still measures the results against the goal."},{"t":"Testing the paper prototype with five to eight real members reveals usability problems and confirms demand while changes are still cheap, and gives the owner evidence before he pays for full development.","ok":true,"fb":"Correct. It links validation to cost, risk and real users, and answers the owner's concern about money."},{"t":"Testing with the owner's friends will show whether the app looks attractive, which is the main reason apps fail.","fb":"Friends are not typical users, and appearance is not the main thing a prototype test checks."},{"t":"A high-fidelity test is more accurate, so the club should finish the app first and test it afterwards.","fb":"Testing later is still useful, but finishing first throws away the main benefit of a prototype: cheap, early changes."}]}},{"type":"checkin","title":"Quick check-in"},{"type":"write","title":"Plan the pre-production of a digital product","genre":"Structured response: pre-production planning (Paper 02 style)","stimulus":"<p class=\"passage\">The Grande Riviere Turtle Watch group in Trinidad wants a <b>30-second animated public-service video</b> to teach beachgoers how to behave near turtle nests. The video will be shared on Instagram, Facebook and on screens at Piarco International Airport. The group has a small budget and needs the video before nesting season begins in eight weeks.</p>","prompt":"<p>As the pre-production lead, write a response that:</p><ol type=\"a\"><li>writes a <b>problem statement</b> with a measurable goal;</li><li><b>describes</b> the target audience, using at least two kinds of audience information;</li><li><b>explains</b> how ONE brainstorming technique (mind mapping or SCAMPER) could generate ideas for the video;</li><li><b>outlines</b> TWO planning documents the team should produce before animation begins (for example, a storyboard and a Gantt chart), and ONE core element of pre-production they must plan for (resources, clearances, copyright and IP, codes of practice, or health and safety);</li><li><b>explains</b> how the team could validate the idea before final production.</li></ol>","min":220,"max":450,"frame":"<p><b>(a)</b> Beachgoers ... need ... because ... Goal: ...<br><b>(b)</b> The target audience is ... (demographic). They ... (behaviour or psychographic). This means the video should ...<br><b>(c)</b> Using SCAMPER / a mind map, the team would ... This helps because ...<br><b>(d)</b> First, a storyboard showing ... Second, a Gantt chart showing ... The team must also plan for ... (core element) by ...<br><b>(e)</b> The team should build an animatic / rough version and test it with ... by ... then ...</p>","checklist":["My problem statement names the user, the need and a reason, and includes a measurable goal.","I described the audience with at least two types of information and explained a design effect.","I explained HOW the brainstorming technique works and WHY it helps.","I outlined two planning documents with specific details for this video, and one core element of pre-production (for example copyright and IP).","I explained a validation method with real target users and iteration.","I linked my points to the turtle video scenario throughout."],"rubric":[{"criterion":"Problem definition and audience analysis","marks":5,"look":["need","because","goal|reduce|increase|%|measur","demograph|age|tourist|visitor|resident","behavio|psychograph|value|phone|instagram|scroll"],"min":3,"strong":"Your problem statement and audience description are precise. Next step: explain how each audience detail changes the design.","weak":"Write the problem statement as \"[user] need [need] because [reason]\" and add a measurable goal. Then describe the audience with demographics (age, tourist or resident) and behaviours (watching Reels with the sound off)."},{"criterion":"Brainstorming and planning documents","marks":5,"look":["scamper|mind ?map|branch|substitut|combin","storyboard|frame|scene","shot|close-?up|narrat|caption|audio","gantt|timeline|milestone|schedule","week|deadline|buffer|depend","budget|resource|personnel|clearance|permit|release","copyright|licen|intellectual property|royalty","code of practice|regulation|risk assess|health and safety","ideation|selection|creative process|branched|cyclic|linear|stepped"],"min":3,"strong":"You explained the technique and planning documents clearly with specific details.","weak":"Explain how SCAMPER or a mind map actually works, then give specific contents for your storyboard (frames, timing, shot types, audio) and Gantt chart (tasks, weeks, milestones)."},{"criterion":"Validation and application to the scenario","marks":5,"look":["prototype|animatic|draft|rough","test|feedback|survey|observ","beach|turtle|nest|riviere|matura","improv|iterat|fix|revis|change"],"min":3,"strong":"Strong validation plan, well tied to the turtle scenario.","weak":"Describe a prototype (such as an animatic), who will test it (real beachgoers), how you will gather feedback and what you will do with it."}],"model":"<p><b>(a)</b> Beachgoers at Grande Riviere and Matura Beach need a quick, memorable way to learn how to behave near turtle nests because many disturb nests without realising it. Goal: reduce disturbances reported by the Turtle Watch group by 50% in the coming nesting season.</p>\n<p><b>(b)</b> The audience includes residents and tourists aged roughly 16 to 45 (demographic). Many scroll Instagram and Facebook with the sound off and watch only a few seconds of each video (behaviour), and many visitors care about protecting nature (psychographic). The video must therefore deliver its key message in the first five seconds, use large captions and simple icons so it works without sound, and use clear, friendly English with a Spanish or French tagline for airport viewers.</p>\n<p><b>(c)</b> Using SCAMPER, the team would take an existing beach safety poster and ask prompts such as Substitute (replace the warning text with an animated baby turtle character), Combine (combine the safety rules with a countdown to hatching) and Eliminate (remove all but the three most important rules). SCAMPER helps because it gives a structured checklist, so the team improves a known idea systematically instead of waiting for inspiration.</p>\n<p><b>(d)</b> First, a <b>storyboard</b> of about six frames, each with timing (for example 0:00 to 0:05), shot type, the animated action, on-screen caption, music and the transition. This lets the client approve the sequence before animation begins. Second, a <b>Gantt chart</b> over eight weeks showing research, storyboard, animatic, animation, sound, review and export, with milestones such as \"storyboard approved\" in Week 2 and buffer time in Week 7 in case of delays. The team must also plan for <b>copyright and intellectual property</b>: the music and any turtle photos must be original or properly licensed (for example a royalty-free track with its licence saved), so the client can share the video on Instagram and at the airport without infringement.</p>\n<p><b>(e)</b> The team should build an <b>animatic</b>, the storyboard frames timed to a rough voice-over, and show it on a phone to about eight real beachgoers at Grande Riviere. They would observe whether viewers can recall the three rules afterwards and ask short survey questions. If viewers miss a rule, the team would revise the frames and test again before final animation, which saves time and money compared with fixing a finished video.</p>","teacherTip":"<p>Structured questions in Paper 02 award marks part by part. Label each part (a), (b), (c) and use the command word: \"describe\" needs details, \"explain\" needs a reason (\"because\", \"this helps by ...\"). Keep every point tied to the scenario: turtles, beaches, Instagram, the airport screens.</p>","easy":{"title":"Plan the pre-production of a digital product (Foundation)","bloom":"Understand","genre":"Structured response: pre-production planning, Foundation (10 marks)","prompt":"<p>Answer each part in one or two sentences. Use the sentence starters to help you.</p><ol type=\"a\"><li><b>Complete</b> the problem statement: who needs what, and why? <i>(2 marks)</i></li><li><b>Give</b> TWO facts about the target audience: one demographic fact (such as age) and one behaviour (something they do). <i>(2 marks)</i></li><li><b>Name</b> ONE planning document the team should make before animation begins, and <b>state</b> ONE thing it will show. <i>(2 marks)</i></li><li><b>Name</b> ONE core element of pre-production and give ONE example of it for this video. <i>(2 marks)</i></li><li><b>State</b> who should watch a rough version of the video before it is finished, and why. <i>(2 marks)</i></li></ol>","min":80,"max":250,"frame":"<p><b>(a)</b> Beachgoers at Grande Riviere need ... because ...<br><b>(b)</b> One demographic fact is ... One behaviour is ...<br><b>(c)</b> The team should make a ... It will show ...<br><b>(d)</b> One core element is ... For this video, the team must ...<br><b>(e)</b> The team should show a rough version to ... because ...</p>","checklist":["My problem statement says who, what they need and why.","I gave one demographic fact and one behaviour.","I named a planning document and said what it shows.","I named a core element and gave an example for the turtle video.","I said who should test the rough version and why."],"rubric":[{"criterion":"Problem statement","marks":2,"look":["need","because"],"min":2,"strong":"Your statement names the users, their need and a reason.","weak":"Use the pattern: \"Beachgoers need ... because ...\"."},{"criterion":"Target audience","marks":2,"look":["age|tourist|visitor|resident|year|teen|adult","scroll|watch|instagram|facebook|phone|sound off|airport"],"min":2,"strong":"You gave a demographic fact and a behaviour.","weak":"Give one fact such as age or \"tourist\", and one thing they do, such as scrolling Instagram with the sound off."},{"criterion":"Planning document","marks":2,"look":["storyboard|gantt|timeline|schedule|script|mood board","frame|scene|shot|week|task|deadline|order|sequence|show"],"min":2,"strong":"You named a planning document and what it shows.","weak":"Name a storyboard (it shows each scene in order) or a Gantt chart (it shows the tasks and the weeks)."},{"criterion":"Core element of pre-production","marks":2,"look":["resource|budget|clearance|permit|release|copyright|licen|code|health|safety","music|image|photo|turtle|beach|airport|money|staff|animator|form"],"min":2,"strong":"You named a core element and gave a fitting example.","weak":"Name a core element such as copyright and IP, then say what it means for this video, such as licensing the music."},{"criterion":"Testing the idea","marks":2,"look":["beachgoer|visitor|tourist|resident|user|people","because|so |find|problem|fix|check|understand|clear"],"min":2,"strong":"You chose real viewers and gave a reason.","weak":"Say that real beachgoers should watch a rough version, so the team can fix problems before the final video."}],"model":"<p><b>(a)</b> Beachgoers at Grande Riviere need a quick way to learn how to behave near turtle nests because many people disturb nests without knowing it.</p><p><b>(b)</b> One demographic fact is that many viewers are tourists aged about 18 to 45. One behaviour is that they scroll Instagram with the sound off.</p><p><b>(c)</b> The team should make a storyboard. It will show each scene of the video in order, with its timing and the words on screen.</p><p><b>(d)</b> One core element is copyright and IP. For this video, the team must use music they have a licence for, such as a royalty-free track.</p><p><b>(e)</b> The team should show a rough version to a few real beachgoers because they can say whether the message is clear before the final video is made.</p>","teacherTip":"<p>Foundation version (10 marks): 2 marks per part, 1 for the idea and 1 for a detail or reason linked to the turtle scenario. Encourage students to use every sentence starter and to underline the scenario word they used (beach, turtle, Instagram, airport). Move them on to the Core task once they can complete each part without the frame.</p>"},"up":{"title":"Plan the pre-production of a digital product (Challenge)","bloom":"Evaluate","genre":"Extended response: pre-production planning, Grade One extension (25 marks)","stimulus":"<p class=\"passage\">The Grande Riviere Turtle Watch group in Trinidad wants a <b>30-second animated public-service video</b> to teach beachgoers how to behave near turtle nests. It will be shared on Instagram and Facebook and shown on screens at Piarco International Airport, where the screens play <b>without sound</b>. The group has <b>US$1,200</b>, two volunteer animators who can only work in the evenings, and <b>eight weeks</b> before nesting season begins. Last year a printed poster campaign reached many residents but very few tourists.</p>","prompt":"<p>As the pre-production lead, write an extended response that:</p><ol type=\"a\"><li>writes a <b>problem statement</b> with a SMART goal and <b>justifies</b> why it does not name the solution <i>(4 marks)</i>;</li><li><b>analyses</b> the target audience using THREE kinds of audience information, explaining the design decision each one leads to <i>(5 marks)</i>;</li><li><b>compares</b> mind mapping and SCAMPER for this brief and <b>recommends</b> one, with a reason <i>(4 marks)</i>;</li><li><b>plans</b> the pre-production: the contents of the storyboard, a Gantt chart showing dependencies, the critical path and buffer time, and how the team will manage TWO core elements of pre-production <i>(6 marks)</i>;</li><li><b>evaluates</b> a low-fidelity and a high-fidelity prototype for validating this video and <b>recommends</b> a validation plan with a measurable test of success <i>(6 marks)</i>.</li></ol>","min":400,"max":750,"frame":"<p><b>(a)</b> Beachgoers ... need ... because ... Goal: ... by ... The statement does not name a video because ...<br><b>(b)</b> Demographic: ... so the video will ... Behaviour: ... so ... Technical context / psychographic: ... so ...<br><b>(c)</b> A mind map would ... whereas SCAMPER would ... I recommend ... because ...<br><b>(d)</b> Storyboard: ... frames, each with ... Gantt chart: ... depends on ... The critical path is ... Buffer: ... Core elements: ... managed by ... and ... managed by ...<br><b>(e)</b> A low-fidelity animatic would ... but ... A high-fidelity draft would ... but ... I recommend ... Success means ...</p>","checklist":["My problem statement has a SMART goal, and I justified why it does not name the solution.","I used three different kinds of audience information, and each one leads to a design decision.","I compared both brainstorming techniques and made a recommendation tied to the brief.","My plan names storyboard contents, dependencies, the critical path, buffer time and two core elements with how to manage them.","I weighed low- against high-fidelity prototypes and gave a validation plan with a measurable success test.","Every part uses details from the stimulus: no sound at the airport, US$1,200, evening-only animators, eight weeks, tourists."],"rubric":[{"criterion":"Problem statement and justification","marks":4,"look":["need","because","%|reduce|increase|measur|by (the )?(end|week|start)","solution|format|open|ideation|not (say|name|mention)"],"min":3,"strong":"SMART goal and a clear justification for keeping the solution open.","weak":"Add a measurable, time-bound goal and explain that naming the solution too early closes off ideation."},{"criterion":"Audience analysis","marks":5,"look":["demograph|age|tourist|visitor|resident","behavio|scroll|sound off|watch","psychograph|value|care|nature|eco","technical|phone|data|screen|airport","so |therefore|caption|icon|first (five|5|few) seconds|design"],"min":4,"strong":"Three kinds of information, each turned into a design decision.","weak":"Use three kinds of information (for example demographic, behaviour, technical context) and say what each one changes in the video."},{"criterion":"Brainstorming comparison","marks":4,"look":["mind ?map","scamper","whereas|however|while|but|compared","recommend|better|best|suit"],"min":3,"strong":"Both techniques compared, with a justified recommendation.","weak":"Explain how each technique works, compare them, then recommend one for this brief. SCAMPER suits improving last year's poster."},{"criterion":"Pre-production plan","marks":6,"look":["storyboard|frame","timing|0:0|second|shot|caption","gantt|week","depend|before|after","critical path","buffer","copyright|licen|resource|budget|clearance|release|code|health|safety"],"min":5,"strong":"A complete, realistic plan that fits the budget and the volunteers' hours.","weak":"Give storyboard contents, at least one dependency, the critical path, where the buffer goes, and two core elements with how each will be managed."},{"criterion":"Prototype evaluation and validation plan","marks":6,"look":["low.?fidelity|animatic|paper|rough","high.?fidelity|polished|near.?final","cheap|cost|quick|time","test|observ|survey|recall","beachgoer|tourist|visitor|airport","recommend|on balance|therefore","%|out of|measur|success"],"min":5,"strong":"Balanced evaluation and a measurable, realistic validation plan.","weak":"Give a strength and a weakness of each fidelity, recommend one (or both in sequence), and set a measurable test such as \"6 of 8 viewers recall the three rules\"."}],"model":"<p><b>(a)</b> Tourists and residents at Grande Riviere and Matura Beach need a quick, memorable way to learn how to behave near turtle nests, because most disturbances happen when visitors do not know the rules, and last year's posters reached few tourists. Goal: reduce disturbances reported by Turtle Watch volunteers by 50% during the coming nesting season, and have 70% of surveyed airport arrivals recall at least two rules. The statement deliberately does not say \"video\". Keeping the format open lets ideation consider other options (such as an airport loop, a sticker or a Reel series) and stops the team defending a solution before the problem is understood.</p>\n<p><b>(b)</b> <i>Demographic:</i> many viewers are tourists aged 18 to 45 arriving by air, plus local residents. The video therefore uses simple English with short Spanish and French captions. <i>Behaviour and technical context:</i> on Instagram, viewers scroll with the sound off, and the airport screens have no sound at all, so the key message must appear in the first five seconds as large captions and clear icons, with narration only as an extra. <i>Psychographic:</i> many visitors value nature and choose eco-friendly activities, so the tone is positive (\"help the hatchlings\") rather than a list of fines.</p>\n<p><b>(c)</b> A mind map would place \"turtle-safe beaches\" in the centre and branch into audiences, messages and settings, which is useful for a brand-new idea. SCAMPER starts from an existing product, last year's poster, and asks prompts such as Substitute (an animated hatchling in place of warning text), Eliminate (keep only three rules) and Put to another use (reuse the frames as stickers). I recommend SCAMPER, because the team already has a product that worked with residents and needs to adapt it for tourists, and SCAMPER improves it systematically in a single evening session that suits the volunteers.</p>\n<p><b>(d)</b> <i>Storyboard:</i> six frames from 0:00 to 0:30, each with timing, shot type, the animated action, the caption, music and the transition, ending on a logo frame. <i>Gantt chart:</i> research (Week 1); storyboard (Week 2, milestone \"storyboard approved\"); animatic and test (Week 3); animation (Weeks 4 to 6); sound and captions (Week 6); review and export (Week 7); buffer (Week 8). Animation depends on the approved storyboard, and sound depends on the picture lock, so research, storyboard, animatic, animation, review and export form the critical path. The buffer sits before the launch milestone because the animators work evenings only and any illness hits the critical path. <i>Core elements:</i> resources: the US$1,200 covers a royalty-free music licence and a small payment for a voice artist, using free software (Blender or OpenToonz) to save money; copyright and IP: the music licence and any reference photos are saved in a clearances folder so the airport and Instagram can show the video without infringement.</p>\n<p><b>(e)</b> A low-fidelity animatic (the storyboard frames timed to a rough voice-over) is quick and costs nothing but volunteer time, so it can be changed easily; however, it cannot show whether the final colours and icons read well on a bright airport screen. A high-fidelity draft shows the real look, but it takes weeks of evening work, so changes at that stage threaten the deadline. On balance I recommend testing the animatic first in Week 3, then checking one high-fidelity frame on an airport-sized screen. Eight viewers (four tourists, four residents) watch the animatic on a phone with the sound off; success means at least six of the eight can state the three rules afterwards. If they cannot, the team revises the frames and retests before animation begins.</p>","teacherTip":"<p>Challenge version (25 marks: 4 + 5 + 4 + 6 + 6). Reward evaluation over description: a Grade One response weighs options and reaches a justified recommendation in (c) and (e), and its Gantt chart in (d) is realistic for evening-only volunteers. Insist that every part uses at least one stimulus detail (no sound at the airport, US$1,200, eight weeks, tourists). Students who list without explaining should cap at about half marks.</p>"}}]},{"id":"M1U2","title":"Elements of visual design and colour theory","objectives":["Identify and apply the elements of visual design: line, shape, form, space, texture, value and colour.","Explain the colour wheel, primary, secondary and tertiary colours, and the harmony rules: complementary, analogous, triadic, split-complementary and tetradic.","Create tints, shades, tones and gradients, and explain colour mixing (RGB additive and CMYK subtractive), hex codes and new colour creation.","Discuss colour psychology and the visual impact of specific colour combinations in a digital format, including contrast and accessibility.","Use colour theory to design a character that reads clearly as a hero or a villain."],"videos":[{"title":"The elements of art and design","q":"seven elements of art line shape form space texture value colour explained"},{"title":"Colour theory basics","q":"color theory basics color wheel harmonies complementary analogous triadic"},{"title":"RGB vs CMYK explained","q":"RGB vs CMYK explained additive subtractive color for designers"},{"title":"Colour in character design","q":"color theory in character design hero villain color palette"}],"screens":[{"type":"learn","title":"The elements of visual design","html":"<p>The <b>elements of visual design</b> are the basic building blocks of every image, logo, web page, animation and app screen. The <b>principles</b> (composition, balance, contrast and so on) are the rules for arranging these elements; you will study composition in the next unit. You should be able to name, explain and apply all seven elements.</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th>Element</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Caribbean digital example</th></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Line</b></td><td>A mark connecting two points. Lines can be straight, curved, thick, thin, implied or directional. They lead the eye and suggest mood: horizontal lines feel calm, diagonal lines feel energetic.</td><td>Curved wave lines across the header of a Bathsheba surf school website.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Shape</b></td><td>A flat, two-dimensional enclosed area. <b>Geometric</b> shapes (circles, squares) feel ordered; <b>organic</b> shapes (leaves, waves) feel natural.</td><td>A round app icon with a flat hibiscus silhouette.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Form</b></td><td>A three-dimensional object, or the illusion of 3D using light and shadow (a sphere instead of a circle).</td><td>A 3D rotating conch shell modelled in Blender.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Space</b></td><td>The area around and between elements. <b>Positive space</b> is occupied by the subject; <b>negative (white) space</b> is empty. Space can also create depth.</td><td>A clean landing page for an Oranjestad jeweller with plenty of empty space around each ring.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Texture</b></td><td>How a surface feels or appears to feel. <b>Actual</b> texture can be touched; in digital media we use <b>visual (implied)</b> texture.</td><td>A sand or woven-straw texture behind the menu of a beach bar.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Value</b></td><td>How light or dark a colour or tone is, from white to black. Value creates contrast, depth and focus.</td><td>A dark storm-grey background behind a bright yellow hurricane alert.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Colour</b></td><td>Light reflected or emitted, described by <b>hue</b> (the colour name), <b>saturation</b> (intensity) and <b>value</b> (lightness). Colour carries emotion and meaning.</td><td>Black, green and gold in a Jamaican Independence Day graphic.</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<p>Elements work together. A poster for the Island Pulse Media's Carnival campaign might use diagonal <b>lines</b> for energy, organic feather <b>shapes</b>, a glitter <b>texture</b>, strong light and dark <b>values</b> for contrast, and bold saturated <b>colour</b>, all arranged with enough <b>space</b> to read the date.</p>\n<p><b>Shape or form?</b> This is a common exam trap. Shape is 2D (length and width); form is 3D (length, width and depth) or looks 3D because of shading.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> When asked to \"analyse\" a design, go element by element and say what each one <b>does</b>: \"The diagonal lines create a sense of movement, suiting the energy of a Carnival event.\" Naming an element without its effect earns little credit.</p>","plain":"<p>The elements of visual design are the building blocks of every design: line, shape, form, space, texture, value and colour. Line leads the eye. Shape is flat; form looks 3D. Space is the room around things. Texture is how a surface seems to feel. Value is how light or dark something is. Colour carries feeling. A hand-painted fruit stall sign in Bridgetown uses all seven.</p>","deeper":"<p>Elements become powerful when they work with or against each other. Strong value contrast can make a small element dominant, and a single diagonal line in an otherwise horizontal layout pulls attention to itself. Grade One analysis explains <b>effect and purpose</b>: not \"the poster uses texture\", but \"a rough woven texture behind the menu suggests hand-made local food, which suits a beach bar's relaxed brand\". Watch two traps. Shape is 2D, while form is 3D or shaded to look 3D. And every colour has a value: a yellow and a grey can share the same value, so they blur together in greyscale or for viewers with low vision. Checking a design in greyscale tests its value structure.</p>"},{"type":"match","game":true,"title":"Match the element of design","intro":"<p>Zara at the Island Pulse Media is reviewing designs for the Grace Bay Beach Festival. Match each element to the design decision that uses it.</p>","pairs":[{"l":"Line","r":"Diagonal stripes that lead the eye towards the ticket button"},{"l":"Shape","r":"A flat circle and triangle combined into a simple sun-and-sail logo"},{"l":"Form","r":"A shaded, rotating 3D steel pan in the opening animation"},{"l":"Space","r":"Wide empty margins around the headline so it can breathe"},{"l":"Texture","r":"A photo-realistic sand pattern behind the schedule"},{"l":"Value","r":"A very dark background so the white date stands out"},{"l":"Colour","r":"Hot pink and turquoise chosen to feel fun and tropical"}],"win":"Well matched. You linked each element of visual design to what it actually does in a design.","tutor":[{"h":"2D or 3D?","p":"<p>Flat outlines are one element; objects with depth and shading are another.</p>"},{"h":"Light and dark","p":"<p>Lightness and darkness is its own element, separate from hue.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Match the element (Foundation)","pairs":[{"l":"Line","r":"Diagonal stripes leading the eye"},{"l":"Form","r":"A shaded, 3D steel pan"},{"l":"Texture","r":"A sand pattern background"},{"l":"Space","r":"Empty margins around the headline"}]},"up":{"title":"Match the element of design (Challenge)","bloom":"Analyse","intro":"<p>Harder: each card describes an <b>effect</b>, not an object. Zara is reviewing designs for a resort in Negril, Jamaica. Match each element to the card that shows it at work.</p>","pairs":[{"l":"Line","r":"The model's gaze and pointing arm create an implied path that leads to the \"Book\" button"},{"l":"Shape","r":"Flat leaf and wave silhouettes with no shading give the eco-resort logo a natural look"},{"l":"Form","r":"Soft shadows and one highlight turn a flat circle into a glossy coconut"},{"l":"Space","r":"The empty area around the logo is deliberately larger than the logo itself"},{"l":"Texture","r":"A scanned linen weave behind the menu suggests a hand-made, rustic feel"},{"l":"Value","r":"The poster still reads clearly in greyscale because of its light and dark contrast"},{"l":"Colour","r":"A desaturated version of the palette marks the buttons that cannot be used yet"}],"win":"Excellent. You recognised each element from its effect, including implied line, the illusion of form and value working without any colour."}},{"type":"learn","title":"The colour wheel: primary, secondary and tertiary","html":"<p>The <b>colour wheel</b> arranges hues in a circle so designers can see how colours relate. Isaac Newton first bent the spectrum into a circle in the early 1700s. Artists traditionally use the <b>RYB</b> (red, yellow, blue) wheel, which is the one most exam questions on harmony rules use.</p>\n<svg viewBox=\"0 0 200 200\" width=\"100%\" style=\"max-width:240px\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Twelve-part colour wheel labelled P for primary, S for secondary and T for tertiary\">\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.5\">\n<circle cx=\"100\" cy=\"100\" r=\"90\"/><circle cx=\"100\" cy=\"100\" r=\"40\"/>\n<line x1=\"100\" y1=\"10\" x2=\"100\" y2=\"60\"/><line x1=\"100\" y1=\"140\" x2=\"100\" y2=\"190\"/>\n<line x1=\"10\" y1=\"100\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"100\"/><line x1=\"140\" y1=\"100\" x2=\"190\" y2=\"100\"/>\n<line x1=\"36\" y1=\"36\" x2=\"72\" y2=\"72\"/><line x1=\"164\" y1=\"164\" x2=\"128\" y2=\"128\"/>\n<line x1=\"164\" y1=\"36\" x2=\"128\" y2=\"72\"/><line x1=\"36\" y1=\"164\" x2=\"72\" y2=\"128\"/>\n</g>\n<g fill=\"currentColor\" font-size=\"10\" text-anchor=\"middle\">\n<text x=\"100\" y=\"30\">Red (P)</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"72\">Orange (S)</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"140\">Yellow (P)</text>\n<text x=\"100\" y=\"180\">Green (S)</text><text x=\"42\" y=\"140\">Blue (P)</text><text x=\"42\" y=\"72\">Violet (S)</text>\n<text x=\"100\" y=\"98\">Tertiaries</text><text x=\"100\" y=\"110\">sit between</text>\n</g>\n</svg>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Primary colours</b> cannot be made by mixing other colours. On the RYB wheel they are <b>red, yellow and blue</b>.</li>\n<li><b>Secondary colours</b> are made by mixing two primaries in equal amounts: red + yellow = <b>orange</b>; yellow + blue = <b>green</b>; blue + red = <b>violet (purple)</b>.</li>\n<li><b>Tertiary colours</b> are made by mixing a primary with a neighbouring secondary. There are six, and each is named primary first: <b>red-orange, yellow-orange, yellow-green, blue-green, blue-violet and red-violet</b>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>That gives the standard <b>12-part wheel</b>: 3 primaries, 3 secondaries and 6 tertiaries.</p>\n<p><b>Warm and cool colours.</b> Reds, oranges and yellows are <b>warm</b>: they feel energetic and appear to come forward. Blues, greens and violets are <b>cool</b>: they feel calm and appear to recede. A Caribbean sunset poster leans warm; a spa in Soufrière might choose cool blues and greens.</p>\n<p><b>Three properties of colour</b></p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Hue</b>: the pure colour family (red, blue-green).</li>\n<li><b>Saturation (chroma)</b>: how intense or pure it is, from vivid to dull grey.</li>\n<li><b>Value (brightness)</b>: how light or dark it is.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>In GIMP, Inkscape and Photoshop you will see these as <b>HSV</b> or <b>HSL</b> sliders.</p>\n<p><b>Important:</b> screens do not mix colour like paint. Screens use the <b>RGB</b> model, where the primaries are red, green and blue. You will compare RGB and CMYK later in this unit, so always say which model you mean.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> Paper 01 often asks \"Which colour is tertiary?\" Look for a hyphenated name that combines a primary and a secondary, such as blue-green. Orange, green and violet are secondary, never tertiary.</p>","plain":"<p>The colour wheel shows how colours relate. On the painter's (RYB) wheel the primaries are red, yellow and blue. Mix two primaries to get a secondary: orange, green or violet. Mix a primary with the secondary next to it to get a tertiary, such as blue-green. Warm colours (reds, oranges) feel lively; cool colours (blues, greens) feel calm, like the sea at Grand Anse in Grenada.</p>","deeper":"<p>Three colour models give three different sets of primaries, and examiners test whether you know which applies. <b>RYB</b> is the artist's wheel used for harmony rules; <b>RGB</b> (red, green and blue light) is used by screens; <b>CMY(K)</b> (cyan, magenta and yellow ink, plus black) is used in print. \"Yellow is a primary\" is only right if you name the RYB model. Keep the three properties separate: changing hue moves around the wheel, changing saturation moves towards grey and changing value moves towards white or black. A dull turquoise is the same hue at lower saturation, not a different colour. Warm and cool are also relative: red-violet looks cool beside orange but warm beside blue.</p>"},{"type":"multi","game":true,"title":"Colour wheel quick-fire","intro":"<p>Students at the Sol Studio in Georgetown are mixing paint for a mural of Kaieteur Falls. Answer each question using the traditional RYB colour wheel.</p>","items":[{"text":"Mixing yellow and blue in equal amounts gives...","options":["violet","green","orange"],"answer":1,"fb":"Yellow + blue = green, a secondary colour."},{"text":"Which of these is a tertiary colour?","options":["blue-green","orange","red"],"answer":0,"fb":"Blue-green is a primary (blue) mixed with a neighbouring secondary (green)."},{"text":"How many tertiary colours are on a standard 12-part wheel?","options":["twelve","three","six"],"answer":2,"fb":"There are six tertiaries, one between each primary and secondary."},{"text":"Which group contains only warm colours?","options":["red, blue, green","red, orange, yellow","blue, green, violet"],"answer":1,"fb":"Reds, oranges and yellows are warm; they feel energetic and seem to advance."},{"text":"A vivid turquoise is made duller and greyer. Which property has changed?","options":["saturation","hue","temperature"],"answer":0,"fb":"Saturation is intensity. The hue stays turquoise, but it becomes less pure."},{"text":"Red, yellow and blue are primary on the RYB wheel because...","options":["they cannot be made by mixing other colours","they are always used in logos","they are the brightest colours"],"answer":0,"fb":"Primary colours are the starting colours that cannot be mixed from others in that model."}],"win":"Excellent. You can identify primary, secondary and tertiary colours, warm and cool colours, and the properties hue, saturation and value.","tutor":[{"h":"Hyphen clue","p":"<p>Tertiary names combine a primary and a secondary with a hyphen.</p>"},{"h":"Colour name or intensity?","p":"<p>Hue is the colour family; saturation is how strong it is.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Colour wheel (Foundation)","items":[{"text":"Red + yellow makes...","options":["green","orange"],"answer":1,"fb":"Red + yellow = orange."},{"text":"Which is tertiary?","options":["violet","red-violet"],"answer":1,"fb":"Red-violet mixes a primary and a secondary."},{"text":"Which is a cool colour?","options":["orange","blue"],"answer":1,"fb":"Blue is cool and calming."}]},"up":{"title":"Colour wheel quick-fire (Challenge)","bloom":"Apply","intro":"<p>Harder quick-fire on the 12-part RYB wheel: tertiaries, complements and properties. Read carefully: the last question is about a <b>screen</b>, so it uses RGB, not RYB.</p>","items":[{"text":"Which tertiary sits between yellow and green?","options":["red-violet","yellow-green","blue-green","yellow-orange"],"answer":1,"fb":"Yellow (primary) mixed with green (its neighbouring secondary) gives yellow-green."},{"text":"What is the complement of yellow-orange on the 12-part RYB wheel?","options":["red-violet","blue-green","blue-violet","violet"],"answer":2,"fb":"Count six steps round the wheel: directly opposite yellow-orange is blue-violet."},{"text":"Which pair contains one secondary colour AND one tertiary colour?","options":["green and red-orange","red and blue","orange and violet","blue-violet and yellow-green"],"answer":0,"fb":"Green is secondary; red-orange is tertiary. Red and blue are primaries, orange and violet are both secondaries, and the last pair are both tertiaries."},{"text":"A designer keeps a sea-green button the same hue but makes it lighter for the hover state. Which property changed?","options":["hue","temperature","saturation","value"],"answer":3,"fb":"Lighter or darker is value. The hue is still sea-green."},{"text":"Placed next to a bright orange, which colour looks COOLEST?","options":["red-orange","blue-green","yellow","red"],"answer":1,"fb":"Blue-green sits on the cool side of the wheel, almost opposite orange."},{"text":"On a phone screen, full red light plus full green light (with blue off) produces...","options":["yellow","black","orange","brown"],"answer":0,"fb":"Screens mix light additively (RGB): red + green = yellow. The RYB paint wheel would not apply here."}],"win":"Excellent. You can find complements and tertiaries by position on the wheel, separate hue, saturation and value, and switch to RGB when the medium is a screen."}},{"type":"learn","title":"Colour harmony rules","html":"<p><b>Colour harmony</b> is a pleasing arrangement of colours. <b>Harmony rules</b> (colour schemes) use the positions of hues on the colour wheel to choose colours that work together. There are five schemes you must know.</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th>Scheme</th><th>How to find it on the wheel</th><th>Example (RYB)</th><th>Effect and use</th></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Monochromatic</b> (bonus)</td><td>One hue with its tints, shades and tones</td><td>Navy, blue, sky blue</td><td>Calm, unified, elegant</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Complementary</b></td><td>Two colours <b>directly opposite</b> each other</td><td>Blue and orange; red and green; yellow and violet</td><td>Maximum contrast and energy; great for a call-to-action button</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Analogous</b></td><td>Two to four colours <b>next to each other</b></td><td>Yellow, yellow-orange, orange</td><td>Harmonious and natural, like a sunset over Negril</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Triadic</b></td><td>Three colours <b>evenly spaced</b> (120 degrees apart, a triangle)</td><td>Red, yellow, blue; or orange, green, violet</td><td>Vibrant but balanced; popular in children's media</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Split-complementary</b></td><td>A base colour plus the <b>two colours either side of its complement</b></td><td>Blue with yellow-orange and red-orange</td><td>Strong contrast with less tension than complementary; safer for beginners</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Tetradic</b> (double complementary)</td><td><b>Two complementary pairs</b>, forming a rectangle (or a square if evenly spaced)</td><td>Red, green, orange and blue</td><td>Rich and varied, but hard to balance</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<p><b>Using schemes well</b></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Let <b>one colour dominate</b>. A common guide is the <b>60-30-10 rule</b>: 60% main colour, 30% secondary colour, 10% accent.</li>\n<li>With complementary and tetradic schemes, reduce saturation or use tints and shades of some colours so they do not fight.</li>\n<li>Use the accent colour for the most important action, such as \"Book now\".</li>\n</ul>\n<p><b>Caribbean examples.</b> A Trinidad and Tobago Carnival band site might use a triadic palette for maximum vibrancy. A Dominica eco-lodge might use an analogous palette of greens and blue-greens drawn from the rainforest. Keisha at the Island Pulse Media designed a Jamaica-themed reggae event post in green with a gold-yellow accent and plenty of black, picking up colours associated with the Jamaican flag.</p>\n<p>Online tools such as Adobe Color and Coolors let you choose a base colour and generate each scheme, with the hex codes ready to copy into your style guide.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> Split-complementary and tetradic are the two schemes students confuse. Split-complementary has <b>three</b> colours (base + two neighbours of the complement); tetradic has <b>four</b> (two full complementary pairs).</p>","plain":"<p>Harmony rules are recipes for colours that look good together, based on where they sit on the colour wheel. Complementary colours are opposite each other. Analogous colours sit side by side. Triadic colours form a triangle. Split-complementary uses one colour plus the two beside its opposite. Tetradic uses two opposite pairs. A rainforest lodge in Guyana might choose analogous greens and blue-greens.</p>","deeper":"<p>Knowing the rule is the start; using it well earns the top marks. Complementary pairs give the most contrast, but at equal amounts and full saturation they fight, so let one colour dominate and use the other as an accent (the 60-30-10 guide). Triadic and tetradic schemes are vivid but hard to balance, so desaturate two of the colours or use their tints. Split-complementary gives strong contrast with less tension, which is why it is safer for beginners. A common error is counting: split-complementary has <b>three</b> colours and tetradic has <b>four</b>. Remember too that the rules are worked out on the RYB wheel; tools such as Adobe Color use an RGB wheel, so their complements differ slightly.</p>"},{"type":"match","game":true,"title":"Name that harmony rule","intro":"<p>Maya at the Island Pulse Media is choosing palettes for five client websites. Match each harmony rule to its palette description.</p>","pairs":[{"l":"Complementary","r":"Blue and orange, directly opposite on the wheel, for a dive shop's \"Book a dive\" button"},{"l":"Analogous","r":"Yellow, yellow-orange and orange, side by side, for a sunset cruise site"},{"l":"Triadic","r":"Red, yellow and blue, evenly spaced, for a children's reading app"},{"l":"Split-complementary","r":"Violet with yellow-orange and yellow-green for a jazz festival poster"},{"l":"Tetradic","r":"Two opposite pairs, red and green plus orange and blue, for a busy Carnival band site"},{"l":"Monochromatic","r":"Navy, blue and sky blue only, for a calm insurance company page"}],"win":"Very good. You can identify colour schemes by their positions on the colour wheel: opposite, adjacent, evenly spaced, split, double pairs or one hue.","tutor":[{"h":"Count the colours","p":"<p>Two opposite = one scheme. Three = triadic or split-complementary. Four = two pairs.</p>"},{"h":"Split","p":"<p>If a base colour is paired with the neighbours of its opposite, the complement has been split.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Harmony rules (Foundation)","pairs":[{"l":"Complementary","r":"Two colours opposite each other"},{"l":"Analogous","r":"Colours next to each other"},{"l":"Triadic","r":"Three colours evenly spaced"},{"l":"Tetradic","r":"Two complementary pairs"}]},"up":{"title":"Name that harmony rule (Challenge)","bloom":"Analyse","intro":"<p>Harder: each palette is given by colour names only. Work out where each colour sits on the 12-part RYB wheel, then match the palette to its harmony rule.</p>","pairs":[{"l":"Complementary","r":"Red-orange and blue-green"},{"l":"Analogous","r":"Blue, blue-green and green"},{"l":"Triadic","r":"Yellow-orange, red-violet and blue-green"},{"l":"Split-complementary","r":"Yellow with red-violet and blue-violet"},{"l":"Tetradic","r":"Yellow, orange, violet and blue"},{"l":"Monochromatic","r":"A deep shade, the pure hue and a pale tint of green"}],"win":"Excellent. You worked out each scheme from wheel positions: opposite, adjacent, evenly spaced, split, two pairs or one hue."}},{"type":"learn","title":"Tints, shades, tones and gradients","html":"<p>You can create many new colours from a single hue by changing its <b>value</b> and <b>saturation</b>. Know all four: tints, shades, tones and gradients.</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th>Term</th><th>Made by</th><th>Effect</th><th>Example from a teal hue</th></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Tint</b></td><td>Adding <b>white</b> to a hue</td><td>Lighter, softer, fresher; often used for backgrounds</td><td>Pale aqua like shallow water at Grace Bay</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Shade</b></td><td>Adding <b>black</b> to a hue</td><td>Darker, deeper, more serious or mysterious</td><td>Deep teal like the sea at night</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Tone</b></td><td>Adding <b>grey</b> (white and black) to a hue</td><td>Muted, less saturated, sophisticated</td><td>Dusty sea-green for a boutique hotel</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<p>A memory trick: a <b>t</b>int goes <b>t</b>owards white; a <b>sh</b>ade goes into the <b>sh</b>adows; a tone is \"toned down\" with grey.</p>\n<p><b>Gradients</b></p>\n<p>A <b>gradient</b> is a smooth, gradual transition from one colour (or value) to another. Each colour in a gradient is a <b>colour stop</b>.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Linear gradient</b>: colours change along a straight line (top to bottom, left to right or at an angle), such as a sky going from deep blue to pale blue at the horizon.</li>\n<li><b>Radial gradient</b>: colours spread outwards from a central point in a circle, such as a glowing sun or a spotlight effect.</li>\n<li><b>Angular (conic)</b> gradients sweep around a centre point; some apps also offer <b>diamond</b> or <b>reflected</b> gradients.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Gradients add depth, suggest light and make flat shapes look more three-dimensional (form). In GIMP, use the <b>Gradient tool (G)</b>: set the foreground and background colours, choose the shape (linear, radial and others) in Tool Options, then drag across the canvas. In Inkscape, open <b>Fill and Stroke</b> and choose linear or radial gradient. On the web, CSS creates gradients without any image file:</p>\n<pre><code>.hero {\n  background: linear-gradient(to bottom, #0B3D91, #7FD1F5);\n}\n.sun {\n  background: radial-gradient(circle, #FFD23F, #F26B1D);\n}</code></pre>\n<p>Malik at the Island Pulse Media uses a warm orange-to-pink linear gradient as the background of every Sunset Sessions promo, so the series is recognisable instantly.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> In a Paper 02 practical you may be asked to \"apply a gradient\" in GIMP or Inkscape. Name the tool, the gradient type and the two colour stops in your written answer, and export as PNG to keep the smooth transition sharp. Heavy JPEG compression can cause visible <b>banding</b> (stripes) in gradients.</p>","plain":"<p>You can make many colours from one hue. Add white to get a tint, which is lighter. Add black to get a shade, which is darker. Add grey to get a tone, which is softer and more muted. A gradient is a smooth blend from one colour to another, like the sky over Barbados fading from deep blue overhead to pale blue at the horizon.</p>","deeper":"<p>Tints, shades and tones let designers build a whole palette from one brand colour while keeping it unified. A typical app uses the pure hue for buttons, a pale tint for backgrounds, a deep shade for text and a tone for disabled states. Strictly, a tone mainly lowers saturation while tints and shades change value, but all three reduce saturation a little, which is why heavy tinting can make a brand colour look washed out. With gradients, check the export: JPEG compression can cause <b>banding</b>, visible stripes where the blend should be smooth, so export as PNG. A gradient between two complements often passes through a muddy grey in the middle; adding a middle colour stop keeps it clean.</p>"},{"type":"sort","game":true,"title":"Tint, shade or tone?","intro":"<p>Jerome at the Island Pulse Media is building a colour palette for the Sorrel Express delivery app, starting from one rich sorrel red. Sort each variation.</p>","main":"How was this variation of the base red created?","bins":["Tint (added white)","Shade (added black)","Tone (added grey)"],"items":[{"t":"A very pale blush for the splash-screen glow.","bin":0,"fb":"Very light versions of a hue are tints."},{"t":"A dusty, muted rose for disabled buttons.","bin":2,"fb":"Muted and greyish means grey was added, so it is a tone."},{"t":"Strawberry milk pink for a \"special offer\" sticker.","bin":0,"fb":"Red plus white makes a light pink tint."},{"t":"Near-black maroon for text that must be dark but still warm.","bin":1,"fb":"Lots of black added to red produces a deep shade."},{"t":"A greyish red that feels calm and sophisticated.","bin":2,"fb":"Grey reduces saturation without just lightening or darkening, which creates a tone."},{"t":"A dark burgundy shadow beneath the logo.","bin":1,"fb":"Burgundy is a dark shade of red."},{"t":"A deep wine colour for the header bar.","bin":1,"fb":"Darkening red with black gives a shade such as wine or maroon."},{"t":"A soft pink used for the app's background cards.","bin":0,"fb":"Pink is red lightened with white, so it is a tint."}],"win":"Well sorted. Tints add white, shades add black and tones add grey, which gives you a whole palette from one hue.","tutor":[{"h":"Lighter or darker?","p":"<p>Lighter versions point to one bin, darker versions to another.</p>"},{"h":"Muted","p":"<p>Words like dusty, greyish or muted suggest the colour has been dulled rather than lightened or darkened.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Tint, shade or tone? (Foundation)","items":[{"t":"Soft pink made from red.","bin":0,"fb":"Red + white = tint."},{"t":"Deep wine colour made from red.","bin":1,"fb":"Red + black = shade."},{"t":"Dusty, greyish rose.","bin":2,"fb":"Red + grey = tone."},{"t":"Dark burgundy.","bin":1,"fb":"Red + black = shade."}]},"up":{"title":"Tint, shade or tone? (Challenge)","bloom":"Analyse","intro":"<p>Jerome's base colour is the sorrel red <b>#C0392B</b>. Some cards give hex codes: compare each pair with the base (higher towards FF = white added; lower towards 00 = black added; closer to 80 = grey added). A fourth bin catches colours that are not variations of this red at all.</p>","main":"How was this colour made from the base red, if it was?","bins":["Tint (added white)","Shade (added black)","Tone (added grey)","Different hue"],"items":[{"t":"A deep teal, used as a complementary accent.","bin":3,"fb":"Teal is a different hue, opposite red on the wheel."},{"t":"A muted, greyish red with almost the same lightness as the base.","bin":2,"fb":"Less saturation without much change in value is a tone."},{"t":"#3A110D, a near-black wine for body text.","bin":1,"fb":"The channels are much lower but in the same proportion: a deep shade."},{"t":"#C0782B, an orange of similar lightness.","bin":3,"fb":"The green channel has risen on its own, so the hue has shifted to orange. That is a different hue, not a tint, shade or tone."},{"t":"#ECC4BF, a pale blush for background cards.","bin":0,"fb":"Every channel has moved up towards FF, so white was added: a tint."},{"t":"#A05C55, a dusty rose for disabled buttons.","bin":2,"fb":"Each channel has moved towards 80 (mid-grey), so grey was added: a tone."},{"t":"A soft pink made by raising value and lowering saturation in the HSV sliders.","bin":0,"fb":"Lighter and paler with the same hue is the effect of adding white: a tint."},{"t":"#601C15, a deep maroon for the header.","bin":1,"fb":"Every channel has been halved towards 00, so black was added: a shade."}],"win":"Excellent. You can read tints, shades and tones from hex values, and you can tell a variation of a hue from a change of hue."}},{"type":"learn","title":"Colour mixing: RGB, CMYK, hex codes and new colours","html":"<p><b>Colour mixing</b> works differently for light (screens) and for ink (print). You should be able to explain colour mixing and new colour creation, and know which model suits which output.</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th></th><th>RGB: additive</th><th>CMYK: subtractive</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Used for</td><td>Screens: phones, monitors, TVs, projectors, websites, apps, video</td><td>Print: flyers, posters, banners, magazines, packaging</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Primaries</td><td>Red, Green, Blue light</td><td>Cyan, Magenta, Yellow ink, plus Key (black)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>How it works</td><td>Light is <b>added</b>; more light makes it brighter</td><td>Ink <b>absorbs (subtracts)</b> light reflected from white paper; more ink makes it darker</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Mixing results</td><td>R + G = yellow; G + B = cyan; R + B = magenta; R + G + B = <b>white</b></td><td>C + M = blue; M + Y = red; C + Y = green; C + M + Y = a muddy dark brown, so black (K) ink is added</td></tr>\n<tr><td>No colour</td><td>All off = black</td><td>No ink = white paper</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<p><b>Hex codes</b></p>\n<p>On the web, RGB colours are usually written as a <b>hexadecimal (hex) code</b>: a hash sign then six characters, <b>#RRGGBB</b>. Each pair is one channel from <b>00</b> (none) to <b>FF</b> (full, which is 255 in decimal). That is 256 levels per channel, so 256 x 256 x 256 gives about <b>16.7 million</b> possible colours.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><span style=\"display:inline-block;width:1em;height:1em;background:#FF0000;border:1px solid currentColor;vertical-align:middle\"></span> <b>#FF0000</b> = pure red (red full, green and blue off), the same as rgb(255, 0, 0).</li>\n<li><span style=\"display:inline-block;width:1em;height:1em;background:#FFFF00;border:1px solid currentColor;vertical-align:middle\"></span> <b>#FFFF00</b> = yellow (red + green light).</li>\n<li><span style=\"display:inline-block;width:1em;height:1em;background:#000000;border:1px solid currentColor;vertical-align:middle\"></span> <b>#000000</b> = black; <span style=\"display:inline-block;width:1em;height:1em;background:#FFFFFF;border:1px solid currentColor;vertical-align:middle\"></span> <b>#FFFFFF</b> = white; equal pairs such as <b>#808080</b> are greys.</li>\n<li><span style=\"display:inline-block;width:1em;height:1em;background:#1FA3A3;border:1px solid currentColor;vertical-align:middle\"></span> <b>#1FA3A3</b> = a teal: little red, plenty of green and blue.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><b>Creating new colours.</b> Digitally, you create a new colour by adjusting RGB or HSV sliders, typing a hex code, sampling a colour from a photo with the <b>eyedropper (colour picker)</b>, or blending two colours. Zara at the Island Pulse Media sampled the exact orange of a flamboyant tree in bloom from a photo to create a signature colour for a client, then saved it in the style guide as a hex code for screens and a CMYK value for print.</p>\n<p><b>Screen to print.</b> Some bright RGB colours, such as vivid neon greens, are <b>out of gamut</b> for CMYK and print duller. Design in RGB for screens; convert to or check CMYK before sending a flyer to a print shop, and always ask the printer for their required profile.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> A classic Paper 01 trap: \"Which colours are the primaries for a computer monitor?\" The answer is red, green and blue, not red, yellow and blue. Match the model to the medium: light = RGB, ink = CMYK, paint = RYB.</p>","plain":"<p>Screens make colour with light, so they use RGB: red, green and blue light added together. Printers make colour with ink, so they use CMYK: cyan, magenta, yellow and black. A hex code such as #FF0000 is a screen colour written as code. If a restaurant in Ocho Rios wants its logo on Instagram and on printed menus, the designer needs an RGB version and a CMYK version.</p>","deeper":"<p>Explain the models with the words <b>additive</b> and <b>subtractive</b>. RGB adds light, so all three channels at full strength make white. CMYK inks subtract light reflected from paper, so more ink makes it darker, and black (K) is added because C + M + Y gives only a muddy dark brown. Read a hex code as three pairs: #1FA3A3 has a low red pair (1F) and high, equal green and blue pairs (A3), so it is a teal. RGB has a wider <b>gamut</b> than CMYK, so vivid screen colours can print dull, which is why designers check a printed proof. A common error is \"convert to CMYK for the website\": CMYK is for print only, and web images use sRGB.</p>"},{"type":"mcq","title":"Screen or print?","q":"Regent Street Bookshop in Georgetown has a logo with the hex colour #00A86B. The owner wants the logo printed on 500 paper bags and also shown on the shop's Instagram page. Which statement is MOST accurate?","options":[{"t":"The logo should be designed in CMYK for Instagram because CMYK has more colours.","fb":"Instagram is viewed on screens, which use RGB. RGB generally has a wider gamut of bright colours than CMYK."},{"t":"The same hex code works perfectly on screens and paper because colours are universal.","fb":"Screens emit light (RGB) and paper reflects light through ink (CMYK), so colours can shift between them."},{"t":"The hex code is an RGB screen value, so it is right for Instagram, but the logo should be converted to a CMYK value and proof-checked before printing because ink mixes subtractively.","ok":true,"fb":"Correct. Hex codes describe additive RGB light for screens. Printing uses subtractive CMYK ink, so the colour must be converted and checked."},{"t":"The printer will use red, yellow and blue ink to match the hex code exactly.","fb":"Commercial printing uses cyan, magenta, yellow and black (CMYK), not RYB paint primaries."}],"tutor":[{"h":"Light or ink?","p":"<p>Instagram is seen on a lit screen. A paper bag reflects light through ink.</p>"},{"h":"Hex means...","p":"<p>Hex codes are a way of writing red, green and blue channel values.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Screen or print? (Foundation)","q":"Which colour model should be used for a flyer that will be printed?","options":[{"t":"RGB","fb":"RGB is for screens that emit light."},{"t":"Hex codes only","fb":"Hex codes are RGB values for screens."},{"t":"CMYK","ok":true,"fb":"Yes. Printers mix cyan, magenta, yellow and black ink."}]},"up":{"title":"Screen or print? (Challenge)","bloom":"Analyse","q":"A Port of Spain events company's brand orange is #FF6A00. On the newly printed posters it looks noticeably duller than on Instagram, although the printer followed the file exactly. What is the MOST likely cause, and the best fix?","options":[{"t":"The printer used CMYK instead of RYB, so the posters should be printed with red, yellow and blue paint.","fb":"Commercial printing uses CMYK inks. RYB is the painter's model and does not apply to presses."},{"t":"The hex code was typed wrongly, because #FF6A00 is a brown.","fb":"#FF6A00 is full red, some green and no blue: a vivid orange. The code is not the problem."},{"t":"The vivid RGB orange lies outside the CMYK gamut, so the nearest printable colour was used; the designer should set a CMYK (or spot colour) value for print and approve a printed proof first.","ok":true,"fb":"Correct. Light can produce colours that ink cannot. Choosing a print value and checking a proof manages the gamut difference."},{"t":"Instagram adds extra saturation to every image, so the posters actually show the true colour.","fb":"The screen shows the RGB colour as designed. The difference comes from light versus ink, not from Instagram."}]}},{"type":"order","game":true,"title":"Create and save a new colour in GIMP","intro":"<p>In a Paper 02 practical, you must create a custom colour sampled from a photo of a Montserrat sunset and save it for reuse. Put the GIMP steps in the correct order.</p>","blocks":[{"t":"Open the sunset photo in GIMP (File > Open)."},{"t":"Select the Color Picker tool (O) and click the orange area of the sky to sample it."},{"t":"Click the foreground colour swatch to open the Change Foreground Color dialog."},{"t":"Fine-tune the colour with the HSV or RGB sliders and note the hex value in the HTML notation box."},{"t":"Click OK, then add the colour to a new palette in the Palettes dialog (Windows > Dockable Dialogs > Palettes)."},{"t":"Apply the colour to your design and export as PNG with a clear file name, such as Candidate123_Sunset_Palette.png."}],"win":"Correct. Sample, refine, record the hex value, save it to a palette, then apply and export: that is how designers create and reuse new colours.","tutor":[{"h":"Sample first","p":"<p>You need the image open and a colour sampled before you can refine it.</p>"},{"h":"Export last","p":"<p>Saving and exporting always comes at the end of a practical task.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"New colour in GIMP (Foundation)","intro":"<p>Put these four steps in order.</p>","blocks":[{"t":"Open the photo."},{"t":"Sample a colour with the Color Picker."},{"t":"Adjust it and note the hex code."},{"t":"Apply it and export as PNG."}]},"up":{"title":"Create and save a new colour in GIMP (Challenge)","bloom":"Apply","intro":"<p>Harder: eight steps, including creating and naming a palette so the colour can be reused across a whole campaign. Put the GIMP workflow in order.</p>","blocks":[{"t":"Open the Montserrat sunset photo in GIMP (File > Open)."},{"t":"Choose the Color Picker tool (O) and click the orange area of the sky."},{"t":"Click the foreground colour swatch to open the Change Foreground Color dialog."},{"t":"Raise the saturation slightly with the HSV sliders, then copy the hex value from the HTML notation box."},{"t":"Open the Palettes dialog (Windows > Dockable Dialogs > Palettes) and create a new palette named Sunset_Brand."},{"t":"Add the foreground colour to the new palette and name the swatch with its hex code."},{"t":"Fill the poster's headline shape with the saved colour."},{"t":"Export the design as PNG (File > Export As) with a clear file name."}],"win":"Correct. Sample, refine, record the hex value, create and name a palette, save the swatch, apply it and export: the colour is now reusable and documented."}},{"type":"learn","title":"Colour psychology and meaning","html":"<p><b>Colour psychology</b> is the study of how colours influence emotions, perceptions and behaviour. Build colour psychology into pre-production planning, and be ready to explain the visual impact of colour. Meanings are <b>tendencies, not fixed rules</b>, and they change with culture, context and personal experience.</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th>Colour</th><th>Common associations</th><th>Typical digital use</th></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Red</b></td><td>Energy, passion, urgency, danger, love</td><td>Sale banners, alerts, food brands, Carnival</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Orange</b></td><td>Friendliness, warmth, fun, affordability</td><td>Call-to-action buttons, youth brands</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Yellow</b></td><td>Optimism, sunshine, attention, caution</td><td>Highlights, warnings, tourism</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Green</b></td><td>Nature, health, growth, money, \"go\"</td><td>Eco-tourism, health apps, banking</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Blue</b></td><td>Trust, calm, security, the sea and sky</td><td>Banks, telecoms, airlines, social media</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Purple / violet</b></td><td>Royalty, luxury, creativity, mystery</td><td>Beauty products, premium services, fantasy</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Black</b></td><td>Power, elegance, sophistication, mourning, evil</td><td>Luxury brands, dramatic film posters</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>White</b></td><td>Purity, simplicity, cleanliness, space</td><td>Minimalist sites, health care</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<p><b>Culture and context matter.</b> In many Western traditions black is worn for mourning, while in parts of East Asia white is linked with funerals. Colours also gain local meaning: national flag colours carry pride, and supporters often wear their team or party colours. In Trinidad and Tobago, the red, white and black of the flag feature strongly in Independence Day and other national celebrations, while colours associated with the Pan-African movement (red, gold and green) often appear in Emancipation Day designs across the region. Always check your audience before assuming what a colour means.</p>\n<p><b>Colour and brand.</b> Consistent colour helps people recognise a brand quickly. Many banks and telecoms around the world, and in the Caribbean, use blues to suggest trust and reliability. When you choose a brand colour, explain it with a reason linked to the audience and purpose, not simply \"I like it\".</p>\n<p><b>Colour and behaviour.</b> Colours guide action: a high-contrast accent button stands out; red badges on app icons create urgency; calm blues and greens suit a mental health app. Keisha at Island Pulse Media A/B-tests two button colours on a hotel booking page and keeps the one that gets more taps, rather than relying on assumptions.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> \"Discuss\" questions on colour psychology reward balance. State the common association, apply it to the product, then add a limitation: \"However, colour meanings vary between cultures, so the designer should test the palette with the target audience.\"</p>","plain":"<p>Colours make people feel things. Red feels urgent and exciting, blue feels calm and trustworthy, green feels natural and healthy, and purple feels luxurious. But meanings change between cultures and places. National colours carry pride: think of the black, gold and green of Jamaica's flag. Always check what a colour means to your audience before you use it.</p>","deeper":"<p>Colour psychology describes a <b>tendency</b>, not a law, and a Grade One answer says so. The red that signals urgency on a sale banner can signal danger on a health app, so context decides the meaning. Brand colour also depends on competitors: if every bank in a territory uses blue, a new credit union may choose green to stand out while still suggesting growth and money. Avoid stereotyped claims such as \"pink is for girls\"; many audiences now reject them. The strongest evidence is testing with the real audience, for example an A/B test of two button colours or a short survey. In a \"discuss\" question, give the association, apply it to the product, then add a limitation.</p>"},{"type":"multi","game":true,"title":"Colour psychology for clients","intro":"<p>The Island Pulse Media has five new clients across the Caribbean. Choose the most suitable main colour for each brief.</p>","items":[{"text":"Calm Waters counselling app for teenagers in Barbados wants users to feel safe and relaxed.","options":["bright red","soft blue and green","black and neon yellow"],"answer":1,"fb":"Soft cool colours are associated with calm and trust; red and neon would feel urgent."},{"text":"Flash Sale Friday campaign for a Spanish Town electronics shop needs urgency.","options":["bold red with yellow accents","pastel lavender","beige"],"answer":0,"fb":"Red signals urgency and energy, and yellow grabs attention."},{"text":"Nature Isle Treks company in Dominica promotes rainforest hikes.","options":["silver and black","hot pink","greens and earthy browns"],"answer":2,"fb":"Greens and earth tones connect directly with nature and the rainforest."},{"text":"Velvet Nights luxury spa in Rodney Bay, St. Lucia, wants a premium, elegant feel.","options":["deep purple, black and gold","primary red, yellow and blue","lime green and orange"],"answer":0,"fb":"Purple, black and gold suggest luxury and sophistication; primary triads feel playful."},{"text":"A designer assumes white will mean \"pure\" to every visitor of a global wedding site. What is the risk?","options":["white is linked to mourning in some cultures","white cannot be shown on screens","none: colours mean the same everywhere"],"answer":0,"fb":"Colour meanings vary by culture, so a global audience needs testing."}],"win":"Excellent. You matched colours to emotions and purposes, and remembered that culture changes colour meaning.","tutor":[{"h":"Feel first","p":"<p>Decide what feeling the client wants, then pick the colours most associated with it.</p>"},{"h":"Audience","p":"<p>Who is viewing? Meanings shift between cultures.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Colour psychology (Foundation)","items":[{"text":"A calm, relaxing app should mainly use...","options":["bright red","soft blue"],"answer":1,"fb":"Soft blue suggests calm."},{"text":"A flash sale needs urgency. Use...","options":["red","pale grey"],"answer":0,"fb":"Red suggests urgency."},{"text":"A rainforest tour company suits...","options":["silver","greens"],"answer":1,"fb":"Green links to nature."}]},"up":{"title":"Colour psychology for clients (Challenge)","bloom":"Evaluate","intro":"<p>Harder briefs: each one has a trade-off. Choose the BEST colour decision for each Island Pulse Media client.</p>","items":[{"text":"A credit union in St. Vincent wants to look trustworthy but stand out from three rival banks that all use blue.","options":["neon purple","the same navy blue as the banks","bright red","a deep green with gold accents"],"answer":3,"fb":"Green suggests growth and money and gold adds value; both look serious but differ from the blue rivals."},{"text":"A hurricane-alert app must show its most urgent warning level. Which approach is BEST?","options":["soft blue to keep people calm","yellow text on white","red plus a warning icon and the words \"Evacuate now\"","red alone"],"answer":2,"fb":"Red signals urgency, and the icon and words make sure everyone, including colour-blind users, understands."},{"text":"A children's reading app in Belize for ages 5 to 8 should feel playful but not overwhelming.","options":["a single brown hue","a triadic palette with one colour dominant and the other two as tints","pure red, yellow and blue in equal amounts on every screen","black and grey only"],"answer":1,"fb":"A triad is playful; letting one colour dominate and softening the others with tints keeps it calm enough for reading."},{"text":"A villa company in Anguilla wants an elegant site with charcoal-grey text on a black background. Older guests will read it on tablets outdoors. What is the main risk?","options":["low value contrast makes the text hard to read and fails accessibility checks","charcoal is unlucky in every culture","charcoal cannot be shown in RGB","there is no risk because luxury brands use dark colours"],"answer":0,"fb":"Dark grey on black has very little value contrast, which gets worse in sunlight and for older eyes."},{"text":"A wedding-planning site in Curaçao serves couples from many countries and uses white for \"purity\". What should the designer do?","options":["test the palette with the target cultures, because white is linked to mourning in some","switch every colour to black","nothing, because white means purity everywhere","use only red"],"answer":0,"fb":"Colour meaning depends on culture, so a global audience needs testing."},{"text":"Keisha's team cannot agree between orange and green for a \"Book now\" button on a blue hotel page. What settles it BEST?","options":["blue, to match the page","the director's favourite colour","an A/B test that measures which button gets more taps","alternating the colours daily without measuring"],"answer":2,"fb":"Evidence from real users beats opinion. An A/B test measures the effect directly."}],"win":"Excellent. You balanced colour meaning against competitors, accessibility, culture and evidence, which is what a \"discuss\" or \"evaluate\" question rewards."}},{"type":"learn","title":"Visual impact of colour combinations in digital formats","html":"<p>You must be able to explain the <b>visual impact of specific colour combinations</b> in a <b>digital format</b>. On screens, colours glow, are viewed on many different devices and must be readable by everyone. Five issues matter most.</p>\n<p><b>1. Contrast and legibility.</b> Text must stand out from its background. Strong value contrast (dark on light, or light on dark) is more important than hue. The Web Content Accessibility Guidelines (WCAG) recommend a contrast ratio of at least <b>4.5:1</b> for normal text and <b>3:1</b> for large text. Free tools such as the WebAIM Contrast Checker test this using hex codes.</p>\n<p><b>2. Vibrating colours.</b> Saturated complementary colours of similar value, such as pure red text on pure green, seem to <b>vibrate</b> or flicker at their edges on screen and cause eye strain. Use complements for accents and shapes, not for body text on each other.</p>\n<p><b>3. Colour vision deficiency.</b> Roughly 1 in 12 men and 1 in 200 women have some form of colour vision deficiency, most often red-green. Never use colour alone to carry meaning: add icons, labels or patterns. A hurricane-warning map from the Island Pulse Media uses colour <b>and</b> labels such as \"Zone A\" so every viewer can read it. Figma, Photoshop and browser developer tools can simulate colour-blind views.</p>\n<p><b>4. Device and context.</b> The same colours look different on a cheap phone in bright Caribbean sunlight, on a calibrated monitor and on a projector. Pale tints may disappear outdoors; very dark designs may hide detail on low-brightness screens. Many apps now offer <b>dark mode</b>, so designers prepare a palette that works on both light and dark backgrounds. Use the <b>sRGB</b> colour profile for web images so colours display consistently.</p>\n<p><b>5. Hierarchy and focus.</b> Colour tells the viewer what to look at first. A neutral layout with one bright accent draws the eye straight to the call to action. Too many saturated colours fight for attention and make a page feel chaotic.</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th>Combination</th><th>Impact on screen</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Black or navy text on white or a pale tint</td><td>Excellent legibility for body text</td></tr>\n<tr><td>White text on a dark teal or deep blue banner</td><td>Strong, calm and readable</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Yellow text on white</td><td>Very poor contrast; nearly invisible</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Red text on green (similar values)</td><td>Vibrates; unreadable for many colour-blind users</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Light grey text on white</td><td>Looks stylish but fails contrast checks</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Orange button on a blue page</td><td>Complementary accent; the button pops</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<p>Next unit (M1U2B) builds on this with the rules of composition, such as the rule of thirds and the golden ratio, and the five principles of interactive design.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> When you justify a colour combination in Paper 02 or your SBA, mention <b>contrast</b> and <b>accessibility</b>, not just mood. \"White text on deep blue gives high contrast for readability on phones in bright sunlight\" is a Grade One reason.</p>","plain":"<p>On screens, colour must be easy to read for everyone. Text needs strong contrast with its background, such as dark text on a light page. Bright opposite colours, like red text on green, seem to shake. Some people cannot tell red from green, so never use colour alone to show meaning. Phones used in bright Caribbean sunshine need even stronger contrast.</p>","deeper":"<p>Contrast is measured, not guessed. WCAG asks for at least 4.5:1 for normal text and 3:1 for large text, and a checker such as WebAIM calculates the ratio from two hex codes. Contrast depends on <b>value</b>, not hue: pure yellow (#FFFF00) on white scores barely above 1:1, while navy on white scores over 14:1. A common misconception is that colour-blind users see only black and white. Most see colour but confuse particular pairs, so the fix is a second cue, such as an icon, a label or a pattern. Dark mode is not simply inverted colours: saturated hues glow on dark backgrounds, so designers often use slightly desaturated tints in dark themes.</p>"},{"type":"pick","game":true,"title":"Spot the colour problems","intro":"<p>Maya is auditing the Port Side Eats food-ordering website before launch. Several colour decisions could cause problems on screen.</p>","q":"Select ALL the colour decisions that will cause legibility or accessibility problems.","lines":["Pale yellow menu text on a white background.","Navy body text on a very pale cream background.","Order status shown only as a red or green dot, with no words.","Bright red headings on a bright green banner of similar lightness.","White \"Order now\" text on a dark teal button.","Light grey prices on a white background to look minimal.","An orange accent button on an otherwise calm blue page.","Error messages in red with a warning icon and the word \"Error\"."],"correct":[0,2,3,5],"win":"Correct. Low contrast (yellow or light grey on white), colour-only meaning and vibrating complements all hurt legibility and accessibility. Dark-on-light text, a contrasting accent and colour plus icon plus words all work well.","miss":"Not quite. For each line ask: is the value contrast strong, and would someone with red-green colour blindness still understand it? Colour plus words or icons is safe; colour alone is not.","tutor":[{"h":"Squint test","p":"<p>Squint at the combination. If the text and background blur together, the value contrast is too low.</p>"},{"h":"Colour alone","p":"<p>Meaning that depends only on red versus green fails many users.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Spot the colour problems (Foundation)","q":"Select the combinations that are hard to read.","lines":["Yellow text on a white background.","Black text on a white background.","Light grey text on a white background.","White text on a dark blue button."],"correct":[0,2],"win":"Yes. Yellow and light grey on white have poor contrast.","miss":"Look for light text on a light background. Try again."},"up":{"title":"Spot the colour problems (Challenge)","bloom":"Evaluate","intro":"<p>Maya is auditing a food-delivery app for a client in Castries before launch. This audit includes contrast ratios, dark mode and charts. Some decisions look stylish but fail; others look plain but pass.</p>","q":"Select ALL the colour decisions that will cause legibility or accessibility problems.","lines":["Delivery status shown by a green, amber or red dot with no label.","Error fields outlined in red AND marked with an icon and the message \"Phone number missing\".","Light grey (#AAAAAA) prices on white, measuring about 2.3:1.","Navy (#1B2A4A) body text on white, measuring well above 4.5:1.","A bright red \"Sale\" label on a bright green banner of the same lightness.","In dark mode, pure blue (#0000FF) links on a near-black background.","A sales chart that uses a red line and a green line with no labels or patterns.","White text on a dark teal (#0F5E5E) \"Order now\" button.","Opening hours shown only in white text on a pale tint banner.","One orange accent button on an otherwise calm blue and white page."],"correct":[0,2,4,5,6,8],"win":"Correct. Colour-only meaning (status dots, unlabelled chart lines), low contrast (light grey or white on pale, pure blue on near-black) and vibrating complements all fail. Navy on white, white on dark teal, colour plus icon plus words, and a single contrasting accent all pass.","miss":"Not quite. For each line ask two questions: is the value contrast strong enough (4.5:1 for normal text), and would someone with red-green colour deficiency still understand it? Pure blue looks bright but is very dark in value, so it fails on near-black."}},{"type":"learn","title":"Designing a hero and a villain with colour","html":"<p>Try a classic activity: <b>design a character for a game, book, comic or animation using colour theory to show whether it is a hero or a villain</b>. Audiences read colour, value and shape in a fraction of a second, so character designers use them to signal personality before a single word is spoken.</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th>Design choice</th><th>Typical hero</th><th>Typical villain</th></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Hue</b></td><td>Blues (trust), warm golds and yellows (hope), clean whites, friendly greens</td><td>Blacks, deep purples, blood reds, sickly yellow-greens</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Value</b></td><td>Lighter values and tints; bright, open faces</td><td>Darker values and shades; faces in shadow</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Saturation</b></td><td>Clear, bright, healthy colours</td><td>Either very desaturated (cold, lifeless) or harsh, glowing accents</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Harmony</b></td><td>Often analogous or a calm, balanced scheme</td><td>Clashing or high-tension complementary accents (a green glow on purple)</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Shape and line</b></td><td>Rounded shapes (friendly) and squares (strong, dependable)</td><td>Sharp triangles, spikes and jagged diagonal lines (danger)</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Texture and lighting</b></td><td>Smooth surfaces, warm front lighting</td><td>Rough or cracked textures, low under-lighting, dark rim light</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<p><b>Contrast between them.</b> Heroes and villains are often given <b>opposing palettes</b>, sometimes near-complementary, so that every fight scene is easy to read: a gold-and-blue hero against a purple-and-green villain. Many well-known animated villains combine purple, black and green.</p>\n<p><b>Break the rules on purpose.</b> Rules can be broken for surprise: a villain in innocent pastel pink feels unsettling. Just be ready to explain the intention.</p>\n<p><b>Caribbean inspiration.</b> Regional folklore is full of characters to reinterpret: Anansi the trickster spider, Papa Bois the forest guardian and the fiery Soucouyant from Trinidad and Tobago, the Ol' Higue of Guyana and the jumbies told of across the islands. Malik at the Island Pulse Media is designing an animated short where a young Barbudan fisher girl, drawn with sea blues, a sunny yellow headwrap and rounded shapes, faces a storm spirit drawn with storm-grey shades, a sickly green glow and jagged lines inspired by Hurricane Irma's clouds.</p>\n<p><b>Digital technique.</b> Sketch the character in pencil, scan or photograph it, then colour it in GIMP, Krita or Inkscape on separate layers. Build a small palette (three to five colours) with hex codes, test the character against the game or comic background for contrast, and do a <b>greyscale check</b>: convert the image to greyscale to confirm the value contrast still separates hero from villain.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> In a written justification, link each colour choice to a <b>meaning</b> and a <b>theory term</b>: \"The villain's cloak uses a deep shade of violet, associated with mystery and power, with a complementary yellow-green glow to create unsettling tension.\"</p>","plain":"<p>Character designers use colour to show who is good and who is bad before anyone speaks. Heroes often get light, bright, warm or trustworthy colours and rounded shapes. Villains often get dark shades, harsh glowing accents and sharp, spiky shapes. Think of Papa Bois, the forest guardian, drawn in calm greens, against a fiery Soucouyant in burning reds and black.</p>","deeper":"<p>The most important tool is <b>value</b>, not hue. If the hero and villain look the same when converted to greyscale, players will confuse them in fast action, whatever their colours. Strong designs give the two characters opposing palettes, often near-complementary, so every confrontation reads clearly. Colour must also work against the <b>background</b>: a blue hero in a blue sky disappears. Breaking the conventions can be powerful, for example a villain in sweet pastels, but a Grade One justification states the intention and its effect. Finally, check accessibility. If danger is shown only by turning red, players with red-green colour deficiency miss it, so pair colour with shape, movement or an icon.</p>"},{"type":"scenario","game":true,"title":"Colour a hero and a villain","intro":"<p>The Island Pulse Media is making a mobile game, <i>Guardians of the Salt Pond</i>, set in Bonaire. Zara asks you to choose the colours for the hero, a young pelican named Pip, and the villain, a pollution monster called Smog. Make the best decisions.</p>","nodes":{"start":{"text":"<p>First, Pip the hero. Players are aged 9 to 14 and should trust and cheer for Pip instantly. Which palette do you choose?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Dark grey and black with red eyes to look cool.","to":"bad1","fb":"Dark values and red eyes are villain signals. Players would distrust Pip."},{"t":"Warm golden-yellow and soft sea blue with white highlights; light values and rounded shapes.","to":"n2","fb":"Good. Light values, hopeful yellows and trustworthy blues read clearly as a hero."}]},"n2":{"text":"<p>Now Smog the villain. Smog must look threatening and must stand out clearly from Pip in every scene. Which palette?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Deep purple and charcoal shades with a sickly yellow-green glow, sharp jagged shapes.","to":"n3","fb":"Excellent. Dark shades, tense accents and spiky shapes read as danger and contrast strongly with Pip."},{"t":"The same golden-yellow and sea blue as Pip, but slightly darker.","to":"bad2","fb":"If hero and villain share a palette, players cannot tell them apart quickly in action."}]},"n3":{"text":"<p>The game background is a bright blue sky and turquoise salt pond. Malik worries Pip's blue may blend in. What do you do?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Run a greyscale and contrast check, then give Pip a darker outline and more yellow so Pip stands out from the background.","to":"n4","fb":"Smart. Checking value contrast against the background keeps the hero readable."},{"t":"Ignore it; players will find Pip eventually.","to":"bad3","fb":"Poor contrast with the background makes the hero hard to follow, especially on small phone screens."}]},"n4":{"text":"<p>Keisha notes that some players may have red-green colour vision deficiency. Smog's attacks are shown only by turning red. What do you change?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Make the red brighter.","to":"bad4","fb":"Brighter red still relies on colour alone, which some players cannot distinguish."},{"t":"Add a shape change and an icon to each attack, so it does not rely on colour alone, and record all hex codes in the style guide.","to":"good","fb":"Perfect. Colour plus shape and icons makes the game accessible, and the style guide keeps it consistent."}]},"good":{"text":"<p><b>Success.</b> Testers instantly cheer for Pip and boo Smog. A Grade One justification would explain hue meanings, value and saturation, contrasting palettes, background contrast and accessibility, all supported with colour theory terms and hex codes.</p>","end":"good"},"bad1":{"text":"<p><b>Mixed signals.</b> Testers think Pip is the bad guy. Try again and use lighter, hopeful colours for the hero.</p>","end":"bad"},"bad2":{"text":"<p><b>Who is who?</b> In battle scenes players keep confusing Pip and Smog. Try again and give the villain a contrasting palette.</p>","end":"bad"},"bad3":{"text":"<p><b>Lost hero.</b> On small screens in sunlight, Pip disappears into the sky. Try again and check contrast against the background.</p>","end":"bad"},"bad4":{"text":"<p><b>Excluded players.</b> Colour-blind testers still cannot see when an attack is coming. Try again and add a non-colour cue.</p>","end":"bad"}},"tutor":[{"h":"Light vs dark","p":"<p>Heroes usually get lighter values and hopeful hues; villains darker shades and tense accents.</p>"},{"h":"Read at a glance","p":"<p>Hero, villain and background must all be clearly separated by colour and value.</p>"},{"h":"Not colour alone","p":"<p>Add shape, icons or words so every player gets the message.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Colour a hero and a villain (Foundation)","intro":"<p>A shorter version: choose colours for Pip the hero and Smog the villain.</p>","nodes":{"start":{"text":"<p>Which palette suits Pip the hero?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Black and grey with red eyes.","to":"bad","fb":"Those are villain signals."},{"t":"Golden-yellow and sea blue, light and rounded.","to":"n2","fb":"Yes. Bright, hopeful colours suit a hero."}]},"n2":{"text":"<p>Which palette suits Smog the villain?</p>","choices":[{"t":"The same colours as Pip.","to":"bad","fb":"Players could not tell them apart."},{"t":"Deep purple and charcoal with a sickly green glow.","to":"good","fb":"Yes. Dark shades and tense accents signal danger."}]},"good":{"text":"<p><b>Well done.</b> Your hero and villain are clearly different through colour.</p>","end":"good"},"bad":{"text":"<p><b>Not quite.</b> Think about what each colour signals. Try again.</p>","end":"bad"}}},"up":{"title":"Colour a hero and a villain (Challenge)","bloom":"Evaluate","intro":"<p>A tougher brief for <i>Guardians of the Salt Pond</i>. Players are aged 9 to 14 and play on budget phones, often outdoors. The game background is a bright blue sky over a turquoise pond. Every option sounds reasonable; some end with only a partial result.</p>","nodes":{"start":{"text":"<p>First, Pip the pelican hero. Players must trust Pip instantly AND see Pip clearly against the blue background. Which palette do you propose?</p>","choices":[{"t":"A warm golden-yellow body, a soft sea-blue wing tip, a dark navy outline and white highlights, with rounded shapes.","to":"n2","fb":"Strong. Yellow reads as hopeful and stands out from the blue background, and the outline adds value contrast."},{"t":"Charcoal and red with sharp beak lines, so Pip looks tough.","to":"bad1","fb":"Dark values, red and sharp lines are villain signals."},{"t":"Sky blue and turquoise to match the Salt Pond, so Pip feels part of the island.","to":"ok1","fb":"Friendly colours, but Pip will blend into the background."}]},"n2":{"text":"<p>Now Smog, the pollution monster. Smog must look threatening and must contrast strongly with Pip. Which palette and harmony do you choose?</p>","choices":[{"t":"A darker golden-yellow and blue, close to Pip's palette.","to":"bad2","fb":"A shared palette makes hero and villain hard to tell apart."},{"t":"Deep violet and charcoal shades with a sickly yellow-green glow and jagged shapes; violet sits opposite Pip's yellow on the wheel.","to":"n3","fb":"Excellent. Dark shades, a tense accent and spikes read as danger, and the complementary relationship separates the two characters."},{"t":"Bright red and orange flames in an analogous scheme, with rounded, bubbly shapes.","to":"ok2","fb":"Red-orange is menacing, but it is warm like Pip's yellow, and rounded shapes soften the threat."}]},"n3":{"text":"<p>On the studio laptop, Pip and Smog look clearly different. Before launch, which check matters MOST for this audience?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Ask the art director whether the colours look nice.","to":"ok3","fb":"Expert opinion helps, but it does not test the players' real screens or conditions."},{"t":"Increase the saturation of everything so it pops.","to":"bad3","fb":"More saturation does not add value contrast and can make colours vibrate."},{"t":"A greyscale value check plus a test on a budget phone outdoors, to make sure both characters stay readable on small, sunlit screens.","to":"n4","fb":"Smart. Value contrast and real devices in real light are what this audience will experience."}]},"n4":{"text":"<p>Smog's attacks are shown only by Smog turning red. About 1 in 12 boys has some colour vision deficiency. What do you change?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Add a shape change (spikes grow) and a warning icon to every attack, and record all hex codes and rules in the style guide.","to":"n5","fb":"Good. A non-colour cue makes the warning accessible, and the style guide keeps it consistent."},{"t":"Make the red brighter and make it flash.","to":"bad4","fb":"It still relies on colour, and fast flashing can be a risk for some players."}]},"n5":{"text":"<p>Zara asks you to justify the palettes in the design document. Which justification is strongest?</p>","choices":[{"t":"\"Pip uses warm yellow (#F2B705) for hope and a navy outline for contrast against the sky. Smog uses deep violet (#3D1A5B), Pip's complement, with a yellow-green glow for tension. Value contrast keeps them distinct in greyscale, and attacks use shape and icons as well as colour.\"","to":"good","fb":"Excellent. Each choice has a colour, a theory term and an effect on the player."},{"t":"\"Pip is yellow because yellow is a happy colour, and Smog is purple because purple looks cool.\"","to":"ok4","fb":"There is a meaning for Pip, but no theory terms, no hex codes and no reason for Smog."}]},"good":{"text":"<p><b>Grade One design.</b> Testers cheer for Pip and boo Smog, colour-blind testers spot every attack, and your justification links hue, value, saturation, harmony, background contrast and accessibility with exact hex codes.</p>","end":"good"},"ok1":{"text":"<p><b>Lost in the sky.</b> Players like Pip but keep losing Pip against the background. A partial success: check contrast against the scene, not just the character's meaning.</p>","end":"ok"},"ok2":{"text":"<p><b>Warm against warm.</b> Smog is scary, but in busy scenes the two warm characters blur. A partial success: give the villain a palette that contrasts with the hero.</p>","end":"ok"},"ok3":{"text":"<p><b>Fine on the laptop only.</b> The director approves, but players outdoors struggle to see Smog's dark shapes. A partial success: test on the real devices and in the real light.</p>","end":"ok"},"ok4":{"text":"<p><b>Weak justification.</b> The game works, but the design document would score low. A partial success: give each colour a hex code, a theory term and an effect.</p>","end":"ok"},"bad1":{"text":"<p><b>Mixed signals.</b> Testers think Pip is the bad guy. Try again and give the hero lighter, hopeful colours and rounded shapes.</p>","end":"bad"},"bad2":{"text":"<p><b>Who is who?</b> In battle scenes players keep confusing Pip and Smog. Try again and give the villain a contrasting palette.</p>","end":"bad"},"bad3":{"text":"<p><b>Eye strain.</b> The over-saturated colours vibrate on small screens and the characters still blend. Try again and check value contrast.</p>","end":"bad"},"bad4":{"text":"<p><b>Excluded players.</b> Colour-blind testers still cannot see when an attack is coming. Try again and add a non-colour cue.</p>","end":"bad"}}}},{"type":"checkin","title":"Quick check-in"},{"type":"write","title":"Justify a hero and villain colour scheme","genre":"Structured response: applying colour theory to character design (Paper 02 style)","stimulus":"<p class=\"passage\">The Antigua-based studio Wadadli Comics is producing a digital comic, published on its website and on Instagram, based on Caribbean folklore. The hero is a young Anansi-inspired spider girl who protects her village; the villain is a fiery Soucouyant-inspired spirit. The studio wants the characters to be recognised instantly on phone screens.</p>","prompt":"<p>Write a design justification for the studio that:</p><ol type=\"a\"><li><b>describes</b> a colour palette for the hero and a palette for the villain, naming at least two colours each with a hex code;</li><li><b>identifies</b> the harmony rule (for example, analogous, complementary, triadic, split-complementary or tetradic) used in each palette, or between the two characters;</li><li><b>explains</b> how hue, value (tints and shades) and saturation communicate hero or villain;</li><li><b>explains</b> TWO considerations for displaying these colours in a digital format (for example, contrast, colour-blind users, RGB vs CMYK, device screens).</li></ol>","min":200,"max":420,"frame":"<p><b>(a)</b> The hero's palette is ... (#......) and ... (#......). The villain's palette is ... (#......) and ... (#......).<br><b>(b)</b> The hero uses a(n) ... scheme because ... Between the two characters, ... creates contrast.<br><b>(c)</b> The hero's ... hue suggests ... and lighter tints make her ... In contrast, the villain's darker shades of ... and ...<br><b>(d)</b> First, ... Second, ...</p>","checklist":["I named at least two colours for each character with valid hex codes (#RRGGBB).","I named a harmony rule correctly and explained how it is found on the colour wheel.","I used the terms hue, value, saturation, tint or shade correctly.","I linked each colour to a meaning and to hero or villain.","I explained two digital-format considerations such as contrast or accessibility.","I referred to the comic, the folklore characters or phone screens throughout."],"rubric":[{"criterion":"Colour palette and harmony rule","marks":5,"look":["#[0-9a-f]{6}","complement|analog|triad|split|tetrad|monochrom","wheel|opposite|adjacent|next to|evenly","blue|gold|yellow|green|teal|white","red|orange|purple|violet|black|crimson|ember"],"min":3,"strong":"Your palettes are specific and your harmony rule is correctly identified. Next step: explain why the scheme suits each character.","weak":"Name real colours with hex codes (for example #1E5FA8 for a sea blue) and identify a harmony rule by its position on the colour wheel: opposite, adjacent or evenly spaced."},{"criterion":"Use of colour theory to communicate character","marks":5,"look":["hue","value|light|dark","tint|shade|tone","saturat|vivid|intens|muted","trust|hope|danger|evil|menace|warm|calm|threat"],"min":3,"strong":"You used colour theory terms precisely to explain how the characters read as hero and villain.","weak":"Use the terms hue, value, saturation, tint and shade, and link each to a feeling, for example \"dark shades of crimson suggest danger\"."},{"criterion":"Digital format considerations","marks":5,"look":["contrast","colou?r.?blind|deficien|accessib|wcag","rgb|screen|phone|device|srgb","cmyk|print|background|instagram|website"],"min":2,"strong":"Strong understanding of how colour behaves on screens and for different users.","weak":"Explain at least two digital issues: contrast against the background, colour-blind users (do not rely on colour alone), RGB for screens versus CMYK for print, or different phone screens."}],"model":"<p><b>(a)</b> The hero's palette is a sea blue (#1E5FA8) for her body, a warm gold (#F2B705) for her headwrap and web patterns, and a pale aqua tint (#BFE9F2) for highlights. The villain's palette is a deep crimson (#8B0A1A), an ember orange (#F2541B) for her flames and a charcoal shade (#2B2B2B) for shadows.</p>\n<p><b>(b)</b> The villain uses an <b>analogous</b> scheme of crimson, red-orange and orange, which sit next to each other on the colour wheel and work together like a single blaze of fire. The hero's blue and gold come from opposite sides of the wheel, so they form a near-<b>complementary</b> pair, which makes her pop. Between the two characters, the hero's cool blue and the villain's warm red-orange are also close to complementary, so every confrontation panel has strong, readable contrast.</p>\n<p><b>(c)</b> The hero's blue <b>hue</b> is associated with trust and calm, and the gold suggests hope and warmth. Lighter <b>tints</b> and high, clean <b>saturation</b> make her face open and friendly. In contrast, the villain's crimson is a darker <b>shade</b> of red, associated with danger and anger, while the glowing, highly saturated orange flames feel aggressive. Her face sits in the charcoal shade, so low <b>value</b> makes her menacing. A greyscale check confirms that the hero is light and the villain is dark even without colour.</p>\n<p><b>(d)</b> First, <b>contrast</b>: the comic is read on phones, often outdoors in bright sunlight, so the speech bubbles will use near-black text (#1A1A1A) on white, which passes the WCAG 4.5:1 guideline, and the hero's dark outline keeps her visible against the blue sky. Second, <b>colour vision deficiency</b>: readers with red-green colour blindness may find the villain's reds hard to tell apart, so her flames will also have a jagged shape and flickering texture, so the meaning never depends on colour alone. Because the comic is digital, all colours are defined in RGB with the sRGB profile; if the studio later prints a book, they must convert to CMYK and check that the bright ember orange has not dulled.</p>","teacherTip":"<p>Colour justifications earn marks when every choice has three parts: the colour (with a hex code), the theory term (hue, shade, complementary) and the effect on the audience. Avoid \"I chose blue because it looks nice\". Add a digital consideration, such as contrast or accessibility, to reach the top band.</p>","easy":{"title":"Justify a hero and villain colour scheme (Foundation)","bloom":"Understand","genre":"Structured response: colour theory for character design, Foundation (10 marks)","prompt":"<p>Answer each part in one or two sentences. Use the sentence starters.</p><ol type=\"a\"><li><b>Name</b> TWO colours for the hero. Give a hex code for at least one of them. <i>(2 marks)</i></li><li><b>Name</b> TWO colours for the villain. Give a hex code for at least one of them. <i>(2 marks)</i></li><li>For ONE hero colour and ONE villain colour, <b>state</b> the feeling each colour gives. <i>(2 marks)</i></li><li><b>Explain</b> whether the villain's colours should be lighter or darker than the hero's. Use the word <b>tint</b> or <b>shade</b>. <i>(2 marks)</i></li><li><b>Give</b> ONE thing the studio should check so the comic is easy to read on a phone. <i>(2 marks)</i></li></ol>","min":80,"max":220,"frame":"<p><b>(a)</b> The hero's colours are ... (#......) and ...<br><b>(b)</b> The villain's colours are ... (#......) and ...<br><b>(c)</b> The hero's ... makes readers feel ... The villain's ... makes readers feel ...<br><b>(d)</b> The villain's colours should be ... because a ... is ...<br><b>(e)</b> The studio should check ... because ...</p>","checklist":["I named two hero colours with at least one hex code.","I named two villain colours with at least one hex code.","I gave a feeling for one colour of each character.","I used the word tint or shade correctly.","I gave one phone-screen check and a reason."],"rubric":[{"criterion":"Hero colours","marks":2,"look":["blue|gold|yellow|green|teal|white|aqua","#[0-9a-f]{6}"],"min":2,"strong":"Two hero colours with a hex code.","weak":"Name two colours for the hero and write one as a hex code, such as #1E5FA8 for sea blue."},{"criterion":"Villain colours","marks":2,"look":["red|crimson|orange|purple|violet|black|charcoal|ember","#[0-9a-f]{6}"],"min":2,"strong":"Two villain colours with a hex code.","weak":"Name two colours for the villain and write one as a hex code, such as #8B0A1A for deep crimson."},{"criterion":"Colour meaning","marks":2,"look":["trust|hope|calm|safe|friend|kind|happy|warm","danger|anger|evil|scary|threat|fear|menac"],"min":2,"strong":"You linked a colour of each character to a feeling.","weak":"Say what each colour makes readers feel: blue can feel trustworthy; dark red can feel dangerous."},{"criterion":"Tint or shade","marks":2,"look":["tint|shade","dark|light|black|white"],"min":2,"strong":"You used tint or shade correctly.","weak":"A shade is a colour with black added, so it is darker. Villains usually use darker shades."},{"criterion":"Phone-screen check","marks":2,"look":["contrast|read|colou?r.?blind|background|bright|sun","because|so |easy|clear|see"],"min":2,"strong":"A sensible check with a reason.","weak":"Check that the text has strong contrast with its background so readers can see it on a small phone screen."}],"model":"<p><b>(a)</b> The hero's colours are sea blue (#1E5FA8) and warm gold.</p><p><b>(b)</b> The villain's colours are deep crimson (#8B0A1A) and charcoal black.</p><p><b>(c)</b> The hero's sea blue makes readers feel she can be trusted. The villain's deep crimson makes readers feel danger and anger.</p><p><b>(d)</b> The villain's colours should be darker, because a shade is a colour with black added, and dark shades look more threatening than the hero's light tints.</p><p><b>(e)</b> The studio should check that the speech-bubble text has strong contrast with its background, because readers use small phone screens, often in bright sunlight.</p>","teacherTip":"<p>Foundation version (10 marks): 2 marks per part. Accept any sensible colours, but at least one valid #RRGGBB code is needed in (a) and (b). In (d), insist on the correct meaning of tint (white added) or shade (black added). Students ready for more can add a harmony rule to their answer, which leads into the Core task.</p>"},"up":{"title":"Justify a hero and villain colour scheme (Challenge)","bloom":"Evaluate","genre":"Extended response: colour theory for character design, Grade One extension (25 marks)","stimulus":"<p class=\"passage\">The Antigua-based studio Wadadli Comics is producing a digital comic based on Caribbean folklore, published on its website (which has a dark mode) and on Instagram. The hero is a young Anansi-inspired spider girl who protects her village; the villain is a Soucouyant-inspired spirit who, in the folklore, appears as an old woman by day and becomes a ball of fire at night. The studio wants the characters recognised instantly on phone screens, and plans a printed collector's edition next year.</p>","prompt":"<p>Write a design justification for the studio that:</p><ol type=\"a\"><li><b>specifies</b> a palette of three colours for each character, with hex codes, and states how the 60-30-10 guide applies to each <i>(5 marks)</i>;</li><li><b>analyses</b> the harmony relationships within each palette AND between the two characters, using positions on the colour wheel <i>(5 marks)</i>;</li><li><b>evaluates</b> how hue, value, saturation, shape and lighting together communicate hero or villain, including the result of a greyscale check <i>(5 marks)</i>;</li><li><b>evaluates</b> THREE digital-format considerations (for example contrast with a WCAG ratio, colour vision deficiency, dark mode, RGB to CMYK for the print edition) and proposes a solution for each <i>(6 marks)</i>;</li><li><b>justifies</b> ONE deliberate choice that breaks a colour convention, explaining its effect on readers <i>(4 marks)</i>.</li></ol>","min":380,"max":700,"frame":"<p><b>(a)</b> Hero: ... (#......, 60%), ... (#......, 30%), ... (#......, 10%). Villain: ...<br><b>(b)</b> Within the hero's palette ... sit ... on the wheel, forming ... The villain's ... are ... Between the characters ...<br><b>(c)</b> The hero's hue ... her light values ... In contrast ... In greyscale ...<br><b>(d)</b> First, contrast: ... Second, ... Third, ...<br><b>(e)</b> One convention I break is ... because ... Readers will ...</p>","checklist":["Each character has three colours with valid hex codes and a 60-30-10 split.","I analysed harmony within each palette and between the characters, using wheel positions.","I evaluated hue, value, saturation, shape and lighting together, with a greyscale result.","I evaluated three digital considerations, each with a practical solution.","I justified one convention-breaking choice and its effect on readers.","I referred to the folklore, the phone screens, dark mode and the print edition."],"rubric":[{"criterion":"Palettes with 60-30-10","marks":5,"look":["#[0-9a-f]{6}","60","30","10","hero|spider|anansi","villain|soucouyant|spirit"],"min":4,"strong":"Precise palettes with hex codes and clear proportions.","weak":"Give three hex codes per character and say which is the 60% main colour, the 30% secondary and the 10% accent."},{"criterion":"Harmony analysis","marks":5,"look":["complement|split|analog|triad|tetrad","opposite|adjacent|next to|either side|evenly","wheel","between|across|both characters|contrast"],"min":3,"strong":"Harmony analysed within and between palettes using wheel positions.","weak":"Name the scheme in each palette, show where the colours sit on the wheel, then explain the relationship between the hero and the villain."},{"criterion":"Colour theory evaluation","marks":5,"look":["hue","value","saturat","shape|line|round|jagged|spik","light|rim|shadow","greyscale|grayscale"],"min":4,"strong":"Several properties evaluated together, confirmed by a greyscale check.","weak":"Explain hue, value, saturation, shape and lighting together, and say what a greyscale test shows."},{"criterion":"Digital considerations","marks":6,"look":["contrast|wcag|4\\.5","colou?r.?blind|deficien|accessib","dark mode|dark theme","cmyk|print|proof|gamut","srgb|rgb|screen|phone"],"min":4,"strong":"Three considerations evaluated, each with a workable solution.","weak":"Choose three: contrast (with a ratio), colour vision deficiency, dark mode or print conversion. Give a problem and a solution for each."},{"criterion":"Justified convention-breaking choice","marks":4,"look":["convention|rule|expect|usual|surprise","because|so that|therefore","reader|audience|viewer","day|old woman|pastel|soft|disguise|foreshadow"],"min":3,"strong":"A deliberate choice, clearly justified by its effect on readers.","weak":"Describe one choice that breaks a convention, why you made it and how readers will react."}],"model":"<p><b>(a)</b> <i>Hero:</i> sea blue #1E5FA8 (60%, body and costume), warm gold #F2B705 (30%, headwrap and web patterns) and pale aqua tint #BFE9F2 (10%, highlights in her eyes and web). <i>Villain (night form):</i> charcoal #2B2B2B (60%, body and shadows), deep crimson #8B0A1A (30%, robes) and ember orange #F2541B (10%, the glowing flames). In each palette the 10% accent sits where I want the reader's eye: the hero's eyes and the villain's fire.</p>\n<p><b>(b)</b> The villain's crimson (red) and ember orange (red-orange) sit next to each other on the wheel, so they are <b>analogous</b> and read as one blaze of fire; the charcoal is neutral and simply deepens the value. The hero's blue and gold (close to yellow-orange) form one half of blue's <b>split-complementary</b> scheme, giving her strong but not harsh contrast. Across both characters the relationship is even neater: blue's split-complement is yellow-orange and red-orange, so the hero's gold and the villain's ember complete one split-complementary scheme. Every confrontation panel therefore sets cool blue against warm fire.</p>\n<p><b>(c)</b> The hero's blue <b>hue</b> suggests trust and calm, and her gold suggests hope. Her light <b>values</b> and clean, high <b>saturation</b> keep her face open, and rounded shapes with soft front lighting feel friendly. The villain's crimson is a dark <b>shade</b> linked to danger; her face sits in charcoal with low under-lighting and an ember rim light, and her flames are jagged, highly saturated shapes that feel aggressive. In a greyscale check the hero reads as mid-to-light grey and the villain as near-black with bright flame edges, so the two remain distinct even without colour.</p>\n<p><b>(d)</b> <i>Contrast:</i> speech bubbles use near-black #1A1A1A on white, about 17:1, far above the WCAG 4.5:1 guideline for readers on sunlit phones. <i>Colour vision deficiency:</i> red-green colour-blind readers may not separate crimson from charcoal easily, so the villain's danger is also carried by flickering, jagged flame shapes and a crackling sound-effect lettering style, never by colour alone. <i>Dark mode:</i> on the website's near-black theme, the charcoal villain could vanish, so each panel has its own light border and the villain has an ember rim light. <i>Print edition:</i> the comic is drawn in sRGB; the vivid ember orange may fall outside the CMYK gamut and print duller, so the studio should choose a CMYK value and approve a printed proof.</p>\n<p><b>(e)</b> I break the \"villains are dark\" convention in the daytime panels. Following the folklore, the Soucouyant appears as a kindly old woman in soft, muted tones of lavender and cream, with lighter values than the hero. Readers will trust her at first, which builds suspense, and the reveal at nightfall, when she bursts into crimson and ember, has far more impact. To keep it fair, one small crimson accent, a scarf, appears in every daytime panel as foreshadowing, so attentive readers can spot the clue.</p>","teacherTip":"<p>Challenge version (25 marks: 5 + 5 + 5 + 6 + 4). Grade One responses go beyond naming schemes: they analyse the relationship between the two characters, confirm value contrast with a greyscale test and give a practical solution for each digital issue. Check every hex code is a valid #RRGGBB value and every harmony claim is correct on the 12-part RYB wheel. In (e), reward a choice that is justified by its effect on readers, not just described.</p>"}}]},{"id":"M1U2B-x","title":"🔒 Composition and principles of interactive design","locked":true,"objectives":["Apply the rules of composition: the rule of thirds, the golden ratio, the golden rectangle and the golden section.","Explain how point of interest and the prominence of the subject are created in a frame or layout.","Use composition elements: perspective, camera angles, lighting, repetition, proximity, white or negative space, balance, contrast, alignment and hierarchy.","Design a two-dimensional environment for a storyboard frame using rules of composition.","Explain and apply the principles of interactive design: consistency, perceivability, learnability, predictability and feedback, using real app and website examples."],"videos":[],"screens":[{"type":"learn","title":"Part of the full course","html":"<div class=\"pv-lock\"><div class=\"pv-lock-ic\">🔒</div><h3>Unlock this unit</h3><p>This unit is part of the full course. Choose a plan to open every section, the games, past papers, the flipbook and the exam room.</p><p><a class=\"pv-lock-btn\" href=\"../../../index.html#plans\" target=\"_top\">See plans</a></p></div>"}]},{"id":"M1U3-x","title":"🔒 Impact and motivations for indigenous digital products","locked":true,"objectives":["Explain the meaning of culture in evolving technology and how cultural identity shapes digital creation.","Use a structured screening, critique and observation method, with clear criteria, to analyse Caribbean digital products.","Use case studies of Caribbean films, animation, games, apps, music videos and digital art to determine their impact and the motivations behind them.","Compare indigenous and imported digital content, and evaluate authenticity, cultural representation and stereotyping.","Discuss how local creators are supported through policy, funding, community and festivals such as the Trinidad and Tobago Film Festival, Animae Caribe and CaribbeanTales.","Define indigenous and culture, identify where culture shows up in digital products, and screen them using six techniques (details and background, dialogue/speech, sound effects, camera angles, target audience/market, purpose)."],"videos":[],"screens":[{"type":"learn","title":"Part of the full course","html":"<div class=\"pv-lock\"><div class=\"pv-lock-ic\">🔒</div><h3>Unlock this unit</h3><p>This unit is part of the full course. Choose a plan to open every section, the games, past papers, the flipbook and the exam room.</p><p><a class=\"pv-lock-btn\" href=\"../../../index.html#plans\" target=\"_top\">See plans</a></p></div>"}]},{"id":"M1U4-x","title":"🔒 Digital media platforms and formats","locked":true,"objectives":["Explain what a digital platform is, distinguish a platform from a format, and classify platforms by how we access content (apps, websites, dynamic platforms, eLearning environments, curated content sources and social media).","Differentiate among search engine, social, knowledge, application store, marketplace, media, crowdsourcing and infrastructure platforms, giving Caribbean examples.","Select appropriate formats for a given purpose and audience, and distinguish between MP4, MP3, PDF, PNG, JPEG and GIF.","Explain how formats work in terms of compression, resolution, colour depth and file size trade-offs.","Import, export, share and embed data for different uses.","Discuss cross-platform compatibility and apply it when publishing to different devices.","Carry out format conversions accurately under Paper 02 conditions."],"videos":[],"screens":[{"type":"learn","title":"Part of the full course","html":"<div class=\"pv-lock\"><div class=\"pv-lock-ic\">🔒</div><h3>Unlock this unit</h3><p>This unit is part of the full course. Choose a plan to open every section, the games, past papers, the flipbook and the exam room.</p><p><a class=\"pv-lock-btn\" href=\"../../../index.html#plans\" target=\"_top\">See plans</a></p></div>"}]},{"id":"M1U5-x","title":"🔒 Audio and video production principles","locked":true,"objectives":["Apply basic image manipulation, editing and compositing techniques (layers, selections, masks, levels, clone and heal, export) in GIMP, Photoshop or Canva.","Record, manipulate and mix sound on multiple tracks in Audacity, and export in MP3, WAV or OGG, explaining when to use each.","Capture video with phones, tablets and cameras, using tripods or gimbals, and record clean sound indoors and outdoors with suitable microphones.","Edit video with cuts, transitions, basic effects, colour grading and text overlays, and light scenes with natural light and three-point lighting.","Export MP4 video with settings suited to YouTube, Instagram and the web.","Evaluate cloud-based authoring tools such as Figma, WeVideo, Canva, Adobe Express, Google Workspace and CapCut.","Plan, capture, edit and publish video using the key terms: the language of media, pre-production documents, the exposure triangle, audio levels and the Clean, Balance, Layer, Master mix, cutting principles and transitions, export settings, file management, licensing and publishing."],"videos":[],"screens":[{"type":"learn","title":"Part of the full course","html":"<div class=\"pv-lock\"><div class=\"pv-lock-ic\">🔒</div><h3>Unlock this unit</h3><p>This unit is part of the full course. Choose a plan to open every section, the games, past papers, the flipbook and the exam room.</p><p><a class=\"pv-lock-btn\" href=\"../../../index.html#plans\" target=\"_top\">See plans</a></p></div>"}]},{"id":"M1U6-x","title":"🔒 2D and 3D animation concepts","locked":true,"objectives":["Explain the 2D animation fundamentals of keyframing, timing, spacing and interpolation (tweening), and use them in a 2D bouncing ball exercise with squash and stretch and ease in and ease out.","Describe and apply all twelve principles of animation (Thomas and Johnston), including straight-ahead action versus pose-to-pose.","Use tweening tools in Adobe Animate and free 2D software such as Pencil2D, OpenToonz and Synfig Studio, and export animation in suitable formats.","Explain 3D modelling fundamentals: vertices, edges, faces, polygons, primitives and meshes, and apply a simple material or texture to a 3D shape.","Animate a 3D bouncing ball in Blender using keyframes, the Timeline, Dope Sheet and Graph Editor, and describe the four key poses of a basic walk cycle.","Choose appropriate render and export settings for animation in a Paper 02 practical or the SBA product's animation or 3D component.","Compare 2D and 3D animation by space, process and look (modelling, layout, rendering), weigh the strengths and challenges of each, and identify traditional, legacy (Flash, Toon Boom Studio, Creatoon, Pencil) and current free tools."],"videos":[],"screens":[{"type":"learn","title":"Part of the full course","html":"<div class=\"pv-lock\"><div class=\"pv-lock-ic\">🔒</div><h3>Unlock this unit</h3><p>This unit is part of the full course. Choose a plan to open every section, the games, past papers, the flipbook and the exam room.</p><p><a class=\"pv-lock-btn\" href=\"../../../index.html#plans\" target=\"_top\">See plans</a></p></div>"}]},{"id":"M1U7-x","title":"🔒 Interactive digital media for collaboration","locked":true,"objectives":["Identify and describe examples of interactive digital media tools used for collaboration, including Google Workspace, Microsoft Teams, Slack, Trello, Miro, Padlet and Figma, and justify a tool for a given task.","Explain what electronic communities are (online groups, forums, wikis, Discord servers and creator communities) and how Caribbean creators use them.","Discuss the advantages and disadvantages of using interactive digital media for collaboration, including real-time feedback, global reach, version control, connectivity, the digital divide, privacy and over-reliance on technology.","Distinguish between self-directed and collaborative digital activities and manage roles and responsibilities in a team.","Apply netiquette and professional conduct in digital collaborative spaces, and keep evidence of collaboration for the SBA.","Name tools for each job (keep the conversation going, make task progress visible, create the work together), explain the term electronic community, and weigh its advantages and disadvantages with examples."],"videos":[],"screens":[{"type":"learn","title":"Part of the full course","html":"<div class=\"pv-lock\"><div class=\"pv-lock-ic\">🔒</div><h3>Unlock this unit</h3><p>This unit is part of the full course. Choose a plan to open every section, the games, past papers, the flipbook and the exam room.</p><p><a class=\"pv-lock-btn\" href=\"../../../index.html#plans\" target=\"_top\">See plans</a></p></div>"}]},{"id":"M1U8-x","title":"🔒 Industry practices for promotional campaigns","locked":true,"objectives":["Apply current digital media industry practices to plan and create a promotional campaign for a digital media product or service.","Compare social networking campaigns on Facebook, Instagram, TikTok, LinkedIn and X, and choose platforms that fit a target audience.","Explain content marketing (blogs, vlogs, podcasts, newsletters), text messaging and WhatsApp, guerrilla, viral, cross-platform, email and influencer marketing, with Caribbean examples.","Use email marketing tools such as Mailchimp, Constant Contact and GetResponse to plan an email campaign.","Plan a campaign step by step: objective, audience, message, channels, content calendar and budget.","Measure campaign effectiveness using analytics, KPIs, reach, impressions, engagement rate, click-through rate and conversions.","Explain and distinguish six channels (viral, social networking, guerrilla, email, influencer and cross-platform marketing), including the eight types of email campaign, six email strategies and the principles of a unified cross-platform strategy."],"videos":[],"screens":[{"type":"learn","title":"Part of the full course","html":"<div class=\"pv-lock\"><div class=\"pv-lock-ic\">🔒</div><h3>Unlock this unit</h3><p>This unit is part of the full course. Choose a plan to open every section, the games, past papers, the flipbook and the exam room.</p><p><a class=\"pv-lock-btn\" href=\"../../../index.html#plans\" target=\"_top\">See plans</a></p></div>"}]},{"id":"M1U9-x","title":"🔒 Trends and developments in visual and interactive design","locked":true,"objectives":["Discuss the six factors that influence trends and developments in visual and interactive design: commoditisation, pervasiveness of technology, affordability, accessibility, adoption and empowerment, with examples.","Describe current interface trends: dark mode, neumorphism, glassmorphism, micro-interactions, immersive UI and motion design, and evaluate when each is appropriate.","Explain how AI-assisted design tools such as Adobe Firefly, Midjourney and DALL-E are changing design work, and discuss the ethical and copyright issues they raise.","Explain uses of augmented reality (AR) and virtual reality (VR) in visual design, with Caribbean applications.","Use research tools such as Google Trends, Behance, Awwwards, Dribbble and Pinterest Trends to discover and track design trends."],"videos":[],"screens":[{"type":"learn","title":"Part of the full course","html":"<div class=\"pv-lock\"><div class=\"pv-lock-ic\">🔒</div><h3>Unlock this unit</h3><p>This unit is part of the full course. Choose a plan to open every section, the games, past papers, the flipbook and the exam room.</p><p><a class=\"pv-lock-btn\" href=\"../../../index.html#plans\" target=\"_top\">See plans</a></p></div>"}]},{"id":"M1U10-x","title":"🔒 Considerations for a project proposal","locked":true,"objectives":["Outline the basic considerations for developing a project proposal: writing, funding, budget and pitching.","Write the main sections of a proposal: executive summary, problem statement, SMART objectives, methodology and timeline, with target audience and market analysis.","Identify and evaluate types of funding (debt and equity) and sources of funding: government grants, crowdfunding, sponsorship, angel investors, loans, venture capital, bootstrapping and regional bodies.","Prepare an itemised budget covering production, equipment, software, personnel, hosting and contingency, in XCG or USD.","Pitch a project convincingly to stakeholders using a visual pitch deck in Canva, Google Slides or PowerPoint.","Link proposal skills to the Unit 2 SBA: the 14-mark Proposal section and the 30-second short ad."],"videos":[],"screens":[{"type":"learn","title":"Part of the full course","html":"<div class=\"pv-lock\"><div class=\"pv-lock-ic\">🔒</div><h3>Unlock this unit</h3><p>This unit is part of the full course. Choose a plan to open every section, the games, past papers, the flipbook and the exam room.</p><p><a class=\"pv-lock-btn\" href=\"../../../index.html#plans\" target=\"_top\">See plans</a></p></div>"}]}],"test":{"title":"Module 1 Test","pass":70,"items":[{"q":"King Street Fabrics asks Island Pulse Media for a promotional video. Which pre-production activity should Zara's team complete FIRST?","options":["Drawing the storyboard for each scene","Booking the camera, tripod and lighting kit","Choosing transitions and text overlays in WeVideo","Defining the problem, purpose and goal of the video"],"answer":3,"why":"Problem definition comes first because every later decision (audience, message, shots, budget) depends on knowing what the product must achieve. The storyboard is tempting because it is an early planning tool, but you cannot plan scenes until you know the purpose and target audience."},{"q":"Which planning tool shows each production task as a bar against a time scale, with milestones such as 'storyboard approved'?","options":["A site map","A mood board","A storyboard","A Gantt chart"],"answer":3,"why":"A Gantt chart plots tasks against time, showing durations, overlaps and milestones, so a team can manage a production schedule. The storyboard is tempting because it is also a sequence, but it shows the order of shots in the product, not the timing of the work."},{"q":"A document that fixes the logo placement, fonts, hex colour codes and spacing rules so every designer on a Carnival campaign produces consistent work is a","options":["mood board","storyboard","rough sketch","style guide"],"answer":3,"why":"A style guide sets fixed rules that keep every item in a project consistent. A mood board is tempting because it also deals with colours and fonts, but it is a loose collection of inspiration that sets a feeling; it does not give exact rules."},{"q":"Which of the following is NOT one of the elements of visual design?","options":["Texture","Proximity","Value","Shape"],"answer":1,"why":"The elements are line, shape, form, space, texture, value and colour. Proximity is a principle of composition (how elements are arranged), not a basic element. Value is tempting because it sounds abstract, but it is the lightness or darkness of a colour and is a core element."},{"q":"The logo of the Frigate Bay Grill uses orange and blue, which sit directly opposite each other on the colour wheel. This colour scheme is","options":["analogous","triadic","complementary","split-complementary"],"answer":2,"why":"Complementary colours are directly opposite each other on the wheel and create strong contrast. Split-complementary is tempting because it is based on the complement, but it uses a base colour with the two colours either side of its complement, so it has three colours, not two."},{"q":"A photographer frames the Castries courthouse so that its cupola sits where two lines of a three-by-three grid cross. This applies","options":["repetition","the golden ratio","symmetrical balance","the rule of thirds"],"answer":3,"why":"The rule of thirds divides the frame into a three-by-three grid and places the subject on a line or intersection. The golden ratio is tempting because it is also a composition rule, but it uses a proportion of about 1 to 1.618 and a spiral, not equal thirds."},{"q":"On the ANU Ferry booking app, the 'Pay' button turns green and a message reads 'Booking confirmed' as soon as it is tapped. Which principle of interactive design is shown?","options":["Consistency","Predictability","Feedback","Learnability"],"answer":2,"why":"Feedback means the system responds visibly to every user action, confirming that it worked. Predictability is tempting, but it is about users being able to expect what will happen BEFORE they act, not the response afterwards."},{"q":"Malik is designing a villain for a Caribbean animated short and wants colour alone to signal danger and menace. Which palette is MOST suitable?","options":["Black, deep red and dark purple shades","Bright yellow and sky blue","Pale analogous greens","Pastel pink and baby blue tints"],"answer":0,"why":"Dark shades of red and purple with black are widely read as threatening, powerful and dangerous, which suits a villain. Bright yellow and sky blue is tempting because it is high contrast, but those colours suggest cheerfulness and energy, which fit a hero or a comic character better."},{"q":"Which statement BEST describes an indigenous digital product?","options":["A foreign film dubbed into a Caribbean language","Any game that is sold in a Caribbean store","Any app that can be downloaded in the Caribbean","Digital content created by local creators that reflects their own culture, identity and stories"],"answer":3,"why":"Indigenous products are made by people from the culture and express that culture from the inside. A dubbed foreign film is tempting because it is adapted for local audiences, but it is still imported content: the stories and creative decisions come from elsewhere."},{"q":"A Trinidadian studio produces an animated series featuring folklore characters such as Papa Bois and the soucouyant. What is the MAIN cultural motivation for this product?","options":["To copy the style of imported cartoons exactly","To avoid paying any production costs","To avoid copyright law because folklore is old","To preserve and share Caribbean heritage with local and global audiences"],"answer":3,"why":"Using local folklore keeps stories alive, builds cultural pride and introduces Caribbean heritage to the world. Avoiding copyright is tempting because folklore is traditional, but the studio's own scripts, characters and animation are still protected, and avoiding the law is not a cultural motivation."},{"q":"Which of the following is an example of POLICY support for local digital creators?","options":["An imported game renaming characters with Caribbean names","A viewer streaming a Hollywood film","A creator buying followers on Instagram","A government film fund offering grants to Caribbean filmmakers"],"answer":3,"why":"A government fund is a policy decision that directly finances local creative work. The renamed imported game is tempting because it looks local, but it is surface localisation of foreign content and does not support local creators."},{"q":"Wikipedia is BEST classified as which type of digital media platform?","options":["Infrastructure platform","Marketplace","Application store","Knowledge platform"],"answer":3,"why":"A knowledge platform exists to collect and share information, which is exactly Wikipedia's purpose. Infrastructure platform is tempting because Wikipedia is large and widely used, but infrastructure platforms provide underlying services (such as cloud hosting) for other products to be built on."},{"q":"Maya needs the Island Pulse Media logo on a website with a transparent background, sharp edges and no compression artefacts. Which format is BEST?","options":["MP3","GIF","PNG","JPEG"],"answer":2,"why":"PNG is lossless and supports full transparency, so edges stay crisp. GIF is tempting because it also supports transparency, but it is limited to 256 colours and only on-or-off transparency, so smooth edges and gradients look rough."},{"q":"Maya places a YouTube video of the Dominica Carnival parade inside a web page so that it plays there, without copying the video file to her own server. This is called","options":["compressing","importing","exporting","embedding"],"answer":3,"why":"Embedding displays content hosted elsewhere inside your page using code such as an iframe. Importing is tempting, but importing brings a copy of the file into your own software or project."},{"q":"Malik needs a full-quality master of a voice-over to edit in Audacity before the final export. Which format should he record and save in?","options":["OGG","WAV","MP3","MP4"],"answer":1,"why":"WAV is uncompressed, so no quality is lost while editing; you export to MP3 or OGG only at the end. OGG is tempting because it is an open format, but like MP3 it is lossy, and repeated saves in a lossy format degrade the audio."},{"q":"In three-point lighting, which light is placed behind the subject to separate it from the background?","options":["Fill light","Practical light","Back light","Key light"],"answer":2,"why":"The back light (rim or hair light) shines from behind to outline the subject. The fill light is tempting because it is also a supporting light, but it sits on the opposite side of the key light to soften shadows on the face."},{"q":"Keisha is recording an interview on a windy beach at Montego Bay. Which action BEST reduces unwanted noise?","options":["Use the camera's built-in microphone from five metres away","Turn the input gain up as high as possible","Use a lavalier microphone with a furry windscreen and shield it from the wind","Add loud background music to cover the wind"],"answer":2,"why":"A close lavalier with a windscreen captures the voice strongly while blocking wind. Turning the gain up is tempting because it makes the voice louder, but it also amplifies the wind noise and can cause clipping."},{"q":"Which export setting is MOST suitable for a 30-second reel to be posted on Instagram?","options":["MP4 (H.264), 1080 x 1920, 9:16 vertical","GIF, 4:3, 256 colours","WAV, 48 kHz","PDF, A4"],"answer":0,"why":"Instagram Reels expect vertical 9:16 video, and MP4 with H.264 gives good quality at a small file size. GIF is tempting because it can animate, but it has no sound, poor colour and a very large file size for video-length content."},{"q":"When a bouncing ball flattens as it hits the ground and lengthens as it rises, the animator is applying","options":["squash and stretch","staging","anticipation","follow-through"],"answer":0,"why":"Squash and stretch deforms the object to show weight, impact and speed while keeping its volume. Anticipation is tempting because it also involves a change of shape, but it is a preparatory movement before an action, such as crouching before a jump."},{"q":"In Adobe Animate, Malik sets the ball's position on frame 1 and frame 24, and the software creates all the frames in between. This process is called","options":["interpolation (tweening)","rigging","rendering","onion skinning"],"answer":0,"why":"Interpolation, or tweening, is the automatic generation of in-between frames between keyframes. Onion skinning is tempting because it is also used when animating, but it only shows faint previous and next frames as a guide; it does not create frames."},{"q":"In Blender, the flat surface enclosed by three or more edges of a mesh is called a","options":["face","vertex","keyframe","modifier"],"answer":0,"why":"A face is the flat surface bounded by edges; faces together form the visible surface of the model. A vertex is tempting because it is also part of a mesh, but it is a single point where edges meet."},{"q":"In a basic walk cycle, which key pose shows the legs furthest apart with both feet touching the ground?","options":["Contact","Down","Passing","Up"],"answer":0,"why":"The contact pose has the front heel and back toe both on the ground with the legs at full stride. The passing pose is tempting because it is also a key pose, but it shows one leg passing the other under the body, with the legs close together."},{"q":"Why is version history in Google Docs an ADVANTAGE for a group writing an SBA proposal?","options":["It closes the digital divide between group members","The group can see who changed what and restore an earlier version if needed","It removes the need for an internet connection","It stops group members from editing the same document"],"answer":1,"why":"Version control records every change and lets the team roll back mistakes, which protects shared work. Working without internet is tempting, but Google Docs offline mode is limited and needs syncing; version history itself has nothing to do with connectivity."},{"q":"Which of the following is a DISADVANTAGE of digital collaboration for a design team with members in Dominica and Guyana?","options":["Unreliable connectivity during storms and power cuts","Real-time feedback on shared files","Automatic saving to the cloud","Access to a wider pool of talent"],"answer":0,"why":"Connectivity problems and the digital divide can stop members from joining calls or uploading work. Access to a wider pool of talent is tempting because it involves distance, but it is an advantage (global reach), not a disadvantage."},{"q":"Spice Lane food court paints giant footprints along the St. George’s boardwalk leading from the cruise pier to its stalls. This is an example of","options":["email marketing","influencer marketing","guerrilla marketing","cross-platform marketing"],"answer":2,"why":"Guerrilla marketing uses unconventional, low-cost, surprising tactics in public spaces. Cross-platform marketing is tempting because the idea could be shared online too, but cross-platform means keeping one consistent message across several channels, not one physical stunt."},{"q":"Which KPI BEST shows whether a Mailchimp email campaign persuaded readers to visit a tour booking page?","options":["Open rate","Click-through rate","Total number of subscribers","Number of emails sent"],"answer":1,"why":"Click-through rate measures the percentage of readers who clicked the link to the booking page, which is the action the campaign wanted. Open rate is tempting, but it only shows that people opened the email, not that they acted on it."},{"q":"Smartphones, smart TVs and connected screens are now found in almost every home, school and workplace in Barbados, so designers must plan for them. Which driving factor does this BEST illustrate?","options":["Empowerment","Affordability","Commoditisation","Pervasiveness of technology"],"answer":3,"why":"Pervasiveness means technology is present everywhere in daily life. Commoditisation is tempting because cheap devices help spread technology, but commoditisation describes technology becoming standardised and cheap, not how widespread it is."},{"q":"A user interface uses frosted, semi-transparent panels floating over a blurred, colourful background. This trend is known as","options":["neumorphism","flat design","skeuomorphism","glassmorphism"],"answer":3,"why":"Glassmorphism imitates frosted glass with transparency and background blur. Neumorphism is tempting because it is also a recent soft UI trend, but it uses solid, same-colour surfaces with soft shadows so elements look pushed out of or into the background."},{"q":"A St. Lucian youth group raises money for an animated film by collecting small donations from hundreds of supporters on an online platform. This source of funding is","options":["crowdfunding","a government grant","an angel investor","sponsorship"],"answer":0,"why":"Crowdfunding raises many small contributions from the public online. Sponsorship is tempting because supporters give money, but sponsorship comes from one business or organisation in return for publicity, not from many individuals."},{"q":"Which cost belongs under 'personnel' in an itemised budget for a 30-second ad?","options":["Website hosting for one year","A new tripod","A monthly Adobe Creative Cloud subscription","The fee for a freelance voice actor"],"answer":3,"why":"Personnel costs are payments to people for their time and skills. The Adobe subscription is tempting because it is a recurring cost, but it belongs under software, and the tripod belongs under equipment."}]},"paper2":{"type":"write","title":"Paper 02 practice: design and animation for a Caribbean campaign","genre":"Paper 02 structured response: visual design, colour, composition, animation and promotional campaign","stimulus":"<p><b>Read the scenario carefully and then answer the questions that follow.</b></p>\n<p class=\"passage\"><b>Reef Guardians SLU</b> is a youth conservation group in Gros Islet, St. Lucia. It has asked Island Pulse Media to promote a beach clean-up and turtle-nesting awareness day at Grande Anse beach. The audience is students aged 13 to 19 and residents; the day falls during the busy Carnival season. Zara reviews the group's first draft materials:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>a poster with red text on a bright green background, six different fonts, and a small turtle photo placed in the exact centre, surrounded by logos with almost no empty space;</li>\n<li>a 10-second animated turtle for social media that moves across the screen at exactly the same speed throughout, with no keyframes except the first and last, and a bouncing beach ball that never changes shape;</li>\n<li>a plan to post the poster once on Facebook, with no other channels and no way of measuring success.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The group has a small budget and a deadline of three weeks.</p>","prompt":"<p>Answer ALL parts.</p><ol type=\"a\"><li>Identify <b>TWO</b> weaknesses in the draft poster. For each, name the design element or principle involved and recommend an improvement. <i>(4 marks)</i></li><li>Recommend a colour harmony scheme for the campaign. Justify your choice for the audience and explain how tints or shades could be used. <i>(3 marks)</i></li><li>Explain how <b>keyframing</b>, <b>timing and spacing</b>, and <b>squash and stretch</b> could improve the turtle and beach ball animation. <i>(4 marks)</i></li><li>Outline a cross-platform promotional campaign for the clean-up day. Describe <b>TWO</b> strategies and state <b>ONE</b> KPI that would measure the success of each. <i>(4 marks)</i></li></ol><p style=\"text-align:right\"><b>[15 marks]</b></p>","min":250,"max":450,"frame":"<p><b>(a)</b> One weakness is ... This breaks the principle of ... I recommend ... A second weakness is ... I recommend ...<br><b>(b)</b> I recommend a ... scheme using ... because the audience ... Tints/shades could be used to ...<br><b>(c)</b> Keyframing would ... Timing and spacing would ... Squash and stretch would ...<br><b>(d)</b> Strategy 1: ... measured by (KPI) ... Strategy 2: ... measured by (KPI) ... The message stays consistent because ...</p>","checklist":["I named two poster weaknesses using correct terms (contrast, colour, hierarchy, negative space, rule of thirds, point of interest) and gave a fix for each.","I recommended a named harmony scheme with specific colours, justified it for the audience and explained tints or shades.","I explained keyframing, timing and spacing, and squash and stretch separately, each applied to the turtle or the ball.","I described two different campaign strategies on more than one platform and matched a measurable KPI to each.","I labelled my answers (a) to (d) and tied every point back to Reef Guardians KIN."],"rubric":[{"criterion":"Poster critique and improvements","marks":4,"look":["contrast|legib|readab|red.{0,20}green|colou?r.?blind","font|typeface|hierarch|consisten","negative space|white space|clutter|crowd|breathe","rule of thirds|point of interest|focal|centre|center|prominen"],"min":2,"strong":"You identified two weaknesses with precise design terms and gave practical fixes.","weak":"Name the element or principle and fix it, e.g. \"Red text on bright green has poor contrast and is hard for colour-blind viewers to read; use dark navy text on a pale sand background.\""},{"criterion":"Colour harmony recommendation","marks":3,"look":["analogous|complementary|triadic|split|tetradic","blue|teal|turquoise|aqua|sand|green|coral|orange","tint|shade|tone|lighter|darker","audience|student|youth|calm|ocean|nature|energ"],"min":2,"strong":"Your scheme is named, specific and justified for the audience, with a clear use of tints or shades.","weak":"Name a scheme and colours, e.g. an analogous scheme of teal, turquoise and sea green with a coral accent, and explain that tints are used for backgrounds and darker shades for headings."},{"criterion":"Animation principles applied","marks":4,"look":["keyframe|key pose|key frame","timing|spacing|eas(e|ing)|slow in|slow out|speed","squash|stretch","turtle|ball|flipper|bounce|weight"],"min":3,"strong":"You applied all three principles accurately to the turtle and the ball.","weak":"Explain each principle in context: add keyframes for flipper strokes; use closer spacing at the start and end so the turtle eases in and out; squash the ball on impact and stretch it as it falls and rises."},{"criterion":"Cross-platform campaign and KPIs","marks":4,"look":["instagram|tiktok|whatsapp|facebook|email|mailchimp|influencer|school|radio|poster","guerrilla|viral|cross.?platform|consistent|hashtag|content marketing","kpi|reach|engagement|click|share|view|sign.?up|registration|attend|rate"],"min":2,"strong":"Your two strategies suit the audience and each has a measurable KPI.","weak":"Describe two strategies on different channels, e.g. a TikTok challenge with a #ReefGuardiansSXM hashtag (KPI: number of shares) and a Google Form sign-up linked from WhatsApp groups (KPI: registrations)."}],"model":"<p><b>(a)</b> One weakness is <b>poor colour contrast</b>. Red text on a bright green background is hard to read, vibrates visually and is almost invisible to viewers with red-green colour blindness, so the key message is lost. I recommend dark navy text on a pale sand-coloured background, which gives strong contrast and suits a beach theme. A second weakness is <b>clutter and a weak point of interest</b>. Six fonts destroy consistency and visual hierarchy, and the small turtle in the exact centre, crowded by logos, has no <b>negative space</b> around it, so the eye has nowhere to land. I recommend two fonts only (one bold display font for the headline and one clean sans-serif for details), enlarging the turtle and placing it on a <b>rule of thirds</b> intersection, and grouping the logos in a strip at the bottom with white space around the headline.</p>\n<p><b>(b)</b> I recommend an <b>analogous scheme</b> of teal, turquoise and sea green with a small <b>complementary accent</b> of coral orange for the date and call to action. The analogous blues and greens link directly to the ocean and nature, feel calm and trustworthy for a conservation message, and the coral accent draws students' eyes to the action. Light <b>tints</b> of turquoise can form the background so the text stands out, while darker <b>shades</b> of teal can be used for headlines and the logo strip to create hierarchy.</p>\n<p><b>(c)</b> <b>Keyframing</b>: the turtle has only a start and end keyframe, so it slides like a cut-out. Extra keyframes for each flipper stroke and a slight rise and fall of the shell would make the swim believable. <b>Timing and spacing</b>: the constant speed looks mechanical. Placing drawings closer together at the start and end (ease in and ease out) and further apart in the middle would make the turtle accelerate and slow naturally; for the ball, wide spacing near the ground and close spacing at the top of each arc shows gravity. <b>Squash and stretch</b>: the beach ball should squash (flatten) on impact with the sand and stretch (lengthen) as it falls and rebounds, while keeping the same volume, so it feels light and bouncy rather than rigid.</p>\n<p><b>(d)</b> The campaign will use one hashtag, <b>#ReefGuardiansSXM</b>, and the same turtle mascot and colours on every channel for <b>cross-platform consistency</b>. <b>Strategy 1: a TikTok and Instagram Reels challenge</b> in which students post a 15-second clip picking up litter with the hashtag, encouraged by a local student influencer. KPI: number of shares and hashtag uses before the event. <b>Strategy 2: a low-cost guerrilla stunt</b>: chalk turtle tracks leading from the Gros Islet bus stop to a QR code that opens a Google Form sign-up, also shared in school WhatsApp groups. KPI: number of registrations through the QR code, compared with attendance on the day.</p>","teacherTip":"<p>In design questions, examiners reward the <b>correct term plus a specific fix</b>. Do not write \"the poster is ugly\"; write \"poor contrast between red text and green background reduces legibility\". For animation, treat each principle separately and apply it to the object in the scenario. For campaigns, always pair a strategy with a <b>measurable KPI</b> (shares, click-through rate, registrations), because \"it will be popular\" earns no marks.</p>","easy":{"title":"Paper 02 practice: design and animation for a Caribbean campaign (Foundation)","bloom":"Understand","genre":"Paper 02 structured response, Foundation: visual design, colour, animation and promotion (10 marks)","prompt":"<p>Answer ALL parts in one or two sentences each. Use the sentence starters to help you.</p><ol type=\"a\"><li><b>State</b> ONE problem with the poster's colours and ONE way to fix it. <i>(2 marks)</i></li><li><b>State</b> ONE problem with the poster's layout (fonts, clutter or the position of the turtle) and ONE way to fix it. <i>(2 marks)</i></li><li><b>Name</b> a colour scheme for the campaign and TWO colours you would use. <i>(2 marks)</i></li><li><b>Describe</b> ONE change that would make the turtle move more naturally, and what squash and stretch would do for the beach ball. <i>(2 marks)</i></li><li><b>Name</b> ONE more platform for the campaign and ONE thing you would count to see if it worked. <i>(2 marks)</i></li></ol><p style=\"text-align:right\"><b>[10 marks]</b></p>","min":100,"max":280,"frame":"<p><b>(a)</b> One problem with the colours is ... To fix it, I would ...<br><b>(b)</b> One problem with the layout is ... To fix it, I would ...<br><b>(c)</b> I would use a ... scheme with ... and ...<br><b>(d)</b> To make the turtle move more naturally, I would ... Squash and stretch would make the ball ...<br><b>(e)</b> I would also use ... and I would count ...</p>","checklist":["I named a colour problem (such as poor contrast) and a fix.","I named a layout problem (fonts, clutter or the centred turtle) and a fix.","I named a colour scheme and two colours.","I described one animation fix for the turtle and what squash and stretch does for the ball.","I named another platform and something to count."],"rubric":[{"criterion":"Colour problem and fix","marks":2,"look":["contrast|read|red.{0,20}green|colou?r.?blind|clash","navy|dark|white|light|sand|change|use"],"min":2,"strong":"A clear colour problem with a sensible fix.","weak":"Red text on bright green is hard to read; use dark navy text on a pale background instead."},{"criterion":"Layout problem and fix","marks":2,"look":["font|clutter|crowd|logo|centre|center|space|small","two fonts|fewer|space|third|bigger|move|group|remove"],"min":2,"strong":"A clear layout problem with a sensible fix.","weak":"Six fonts look messy; use two fonts only. Or: the turtle is small and crowded; make it bigger and give it space."},{"criterion":"Colour scheme","marks":2,"look":["analogous|complementary|triadic|split|monochrom","blue|teal|turquoise|green|sand|coral|orange"],"min":2,"strong":"A named scheme with two suitable colours.","weak":"For example: an analogous scheme of turquoise and sea green."},{"criterion":"Animation fixes","marks":2,"look":["keyframe|ease|slow in|slow out|speed up|slow down|spacing|timing|flipper","squash|stretch|flatten|bouncy|weight"],"min":2,"strong":"One turtle fix and a correct use of squash and stretch.","weak":"Add keyframes for each flipper stroke, and squash the ball when it hits the sand so it looks bouncy."},{"criterion":"Platform and measure","marks":2,"look":["tiktok|instagram|whatsapp|email|school|radio|youtube","count|share|view|like|sign.?up|registration|people|attend"],"min":2,"strong":"A suitable platform and a measurable count.","weak":"For example: post on TikTok and count how many people sign up for the clean-up."}],"model":"<p><b>(a)</b> One problem with the colours is that red text on a bright green background is hard to read, especially for colour-blind viewers. To fix it, I would use dark navy text on a pale sand-coloured background.</p><p><b>(b)</b> One problem with the layout is that the small turtle is crowded by logos in the centre. To fix it, I would make the turtle bigger, move it to one side and put the logos in a strip at the bottom.</p><p><b>(c)</b> I would use an analogous scheme with turquoise and sea green, because they remind people of the sea.</p><p><b>(d)</b> To make the turtle move more naturally, I would add keyframes for each flipper stroke so it speeds up and slows down. Squash and stretch would make the ball flatten when it hits the sand and stretch as it bounces, so it looks light and bouncy.</p><p><b>(e)</b> I would also use TikTok, and I would count how many students sign up for the clean-up day.</p>","teacherTip":"<p>Foundation version (10 marks): 2 marks per part, 1 for the problem or choice and 1 for the fix, detail or reason. Insist on correct terms (contrast, analogous, keyframe, squash and stretch). Students who manage every part without the sentence starters are ready for the Core question; ask them to add a KPI with a number next time.</p>"},"up":{"title":"Paper 02 practice: design and animation for a Caribbean campaign (Challenge)","bloom":"Create","genre":"Paper 02 extended response, Grade One extension: visual design, colour, composition, animation and promotional campaign (25 marks)","stimulus":"<p><b>Read the scenario carefully and then answer the questions that follow.</b></p>\n<p class=\"passage\"><b>Reef Guardians SLU</b> is a youth conservation group in Gros Islet, St. Lucia. It has asked Island Pulse Media to promote a beach clean-up and turtle-nesting awareness day at Grande Anse beach. The audience is students aged 13 to 19 across the island (some speak mainly Kwéyòl at home) and local residents. The day falls during the busy Carnival season, so the campaign competes with many other events. Zara reviews the group's first draft materials:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>a poster with red text on a bright green background, six different fonts and a small turtle photo placed in the exact centre, surrounded by logos with almost no empty space;</li>\n<li>a 10-second animated turtle for social media that moves across the screen at exactly the same speed throughout, with keyframes only on the first and last frames, and a bouncing beach ball that never changes shape and bounces to the same height every time;</li>\n<li>a plan to post the poster once on Facebook, with no other channels and no way of measuring success.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The group has a budget of XCD 1,000 and a deadline of three weeks. Members want to film students on the beach, near the nesting area, for the social media videos.</p>","prompt":"<p>Answer ALL parts.</p><ol type=\"a\"><li><b>Critique</b> the draft poster using at least FOUR different elements or principles of design, and <b>propose</b> a redesign that specifies the layout grid, the visual hierarchy, the typefaces and an accessibility standard. <i>(6 marks)</i></li><li><b>Design</b> a colour palette for the campaign: name the harmony rule and the wheel positions, give hex codes, apply the 60-30-10 guide, explain your use of tints and shades, and justify the palette with colour psychology and accessibility. <i>(5 marks)</i></li><li><b>Plan</b> the improved animation at a stated frame rate: keyframes and breakdowns, timing and spacing (with frame numbers), squash and stretch, arcs and follow-through. Give suitable export settings for TikTok and Instagram Reels. <i>(6 marks)</i></li><li><b>Plan</b> a cross-platform campaign with a SMART objective and THREE strategies, giving a KPI with a formula and a target for each, and explain how consistency is kept across platforms and languages. <i>(6 marks)</i></li><li><b>Evaluate</b> ONE ethical or legal issue raised by the campaign and explain how the group should handle it. <i>(2 marks)</i></li></ol><p style=\"text-align:right\"><b>[25 marks]</b></p>","min":500,"max":900,"frame":"<p><b>(a)</b> Weakness 1 (contrast) ... Weakness 2 (typography/consistency) ... Weakness 3 (point of interest/rule of thirds) ... Weakness 4 (white space/proximity) ... Redesign: grid ..., hierarchy ..., typefaces ..., accessibility standard ...<br><b>(b)</b> Harmony rule ... wheel positions ... #...... (60%), #...... (30%), #...... (10%) ... tints ... shades ... psychology ... contrast ratio ...<br><b>(c)</b> At ... fps ... turtle keys at frames ... breakdowns ... easing ... flippers follow through ... ball keys at frames ... squash on ... stretch on ... Export: ...<br><b>(d)</b> Objective: by ..., ... Strategy 1 ... KPI = ... ÷ ... × 100, target ... Strategy 2 ... Strategy 3 ... Consistency ...<br><b>(e)</b> Issue ... handled by ...</p>","checklist":["I critiqued the poster with at least four different design terms, each with evidence, and specified a full redesign.","My palette names the harmony rule, its wheel positions, valid hex codes, a 60-30-10 split, tints and shades, and a contrast check.","My animation plan states the frame rate, gives frame numbers for keys and contacts, and covers keyframes, breakdowns, timing, spacing, squash and stretch, arcs and follow-through.","I gave export settings suitable for vertical social video.","My campaign has a SMART objective, three strategies on different channels and a KPI with a formula and target for each.","I explained consistency across platforms and in English and Kwéyòl.","I evaluated a real ethical or legal issue from the scenario and how to handle it."],"rubric":[{"criterion":"Poster critique and redesign","marks":6,"look":["contrast|legib|readab|red.{0,20}green|colou?r.?blind","font|typeface|consisten|repetition","hierarch","negative space|white space|clutter|proximity","rule of thirds|power point|point of interest|focal|golden","wcag|4\\.5|contrast ratio"],"min":5,"strong":"Four or more precise weaknesses and a fully specified redesign with an accessibility standard.","weak":"Use four different terms (contrast, consistency, hierarchy, negative space, point of interest), give evidence for each, then specify the grid, hierarchy, two typefaces and a contrast standard such as WCAG 4.5:1."},{"criterion":"Colour palette design","marks":5,"look":["analogous|complementary|triadic|split|tetradic","wheel|opposite|adjacent|next to","#[0-9a-f]{6}","60","tint|shade","calm|trust|nature|ocean|energ|urgen|psycholog"],"min":5,"strong":"A precise palette with hex codes and proportions, justified by psychology and checked for contrast.","weak":"Name the rule and where the colours sit on the wheel, give hex codes with a 60-30-10 split, say where tints and shades go and why the colours suit the audience."},{"criterion":"Animation plan and export","marks":6,"look":["fps|frames per second","frame [0-9]+|frames? [0-9]+","keyframe|key pose","breakdown|inbetween","ease|slow in|slow out|spacing","squash","stretch","arc|follow.?through|overlap","1080 ?(by|x|×) ?1920|9:16"],"min":7,"strong":"A precise, frame-numbered plan that applies every principle, with correct export settings.","weak":"State the frame rate, give frame numbers for keys and contacts, and apply keyframes, breakdowns, easing, squash and stretch, arcs and follow-through; export H.264 MP4 at 1080 × 1920."},{"criterion":"Campaign plan and KPIs","marks":6,"look":["by [0-9]|by the|three weeks|event day","tiktok|instagram|reel","whatsapp|email|school|form","guerr?illa|chalk|qr|pop.?up|influencer","÷|/|divid|× 100|x 100","target|%","hashtag|consisten|same logo|mascot","kwéyòl|kweyol|creole|bilingual|language"],"min":6,"strong":"A SMART objective with three well-chosen strategies, each with a formula-based KPI and target, and clear bilingual consistency.","weak":"Set a SMART objective, give three strategies on different channels, attach a KPI with a formula and a target to each, and explain how the hashtag, mascot and colours stay the same in English and Kwéyòl."},{"criterion":"Ethical or legal issue","marks":2,"look":["permission|permit|consent|release|parent|licen|nest|wildlife|disturb","because|so that|should|must"],"min":2,"strong":"A real issue from the scenario, evaluated with a practical way to handle it.","weak":"Choose an issue such as filming minors, using music, chalk on public paths or filming near turtle nests, and explain what the group must do about it."}],"model":"<p><b>(a)</b> <i>Contrast:</i> red text on bright green has similar value, so it vibrates on screen and is almost unreadable for viewers with red-green colour deficiency; the key message is lost. <i>Consistency and hierarchy:</i> six fonts break repetition and give no clear order, so nothing tells the viewer what to read first. <i>Point of interest:</i> the turtle is small and dead centre, competing with logos of similar size, so the poster fails the two-second test. <i>Negative space and proximity:</i> there is almost no empty space, and the logos are mixed in with the information, so related details are not grouped. <i>Redesign:</i> a rule-of-thirds grid on an A3 portrait poster, with a large turtle on the lower-right power point, swimming towards the headline to give lead room; hierarchy of headline (\"Guard the Nest\"), then date and place in one block, then the call to action with a QR code, and logos grouped in a strip at the bottom; two typefaces only, a bold rounded display face for the headline and a clean sans-serif for details; all text to meet the WCAG 4.5:1 contrast guideline, and no information carried by colour alone.</p>\n<p><b>(b)</b> An <b>analogous</b> base of blue-green and green, which sit next to each other on the RYB wheel, with a <b>complementary</b> accent of coral, a red-orange that sits opposite blue-green. <i>60%:</i> a pale turquoise tint #CFF1EF for backgrounds. <i>30%:</i> a deep teal shade #0B4F52 for headlines and the logo strip, and sea green #2E8B57 for shapes. <i>10%:</i> coral #FF6F59 for the date badge and the QR frame. The teal and green link to the sea and nature and feel calm and trustworthy, which suits a conservation message, while the warm coral accent creates urgency and draws students' eyes to the action. Tints keep large areas light so text stands out; shades give weight to the most important text. Teal text on the pale tint measures about 7.7:1, well above 4.5:1. Coral is used only as an accent shape, never as a background for small text, because white text on coral would fail the contrast check.</p>\n<p><b>(c)</b> At <b>24 fps</b>, the 10-second clip is 240 frames. <i>Turtle:</i> set key poses at the extremes of each flipper stroke (frames 1, 25, 49 and so on), with breakdowns halfway (frames 13, 37...) that decide how the flippers sweep along curved <b>arcs</b>. Ease in over frames 1 to 12 and ease out over frames 229 to 240, with closer spacing at the start and end and wider spacing mid-swim, so the turtle accelerates and glides. Add a gentle rise and fall of the shell, and let the flipper tips <b>follow through</b> a few frames behind the main stroke. <i>Ball:</i> keys at the top (frame 1), first contact (frame 12), lower top (frame 21), second contact (frame 28) and a final small bounce (contact frame 33), each bounce lower and quicker because the ball loses energy; close spacing near the tops and wide spacing near the sand. On contact frames the ball <b>squashes</b> (flatter and wider, same volume); on the frames just before and after it <b>stretches</b> along its path. <i>Export:</i> H.264 MP4, 1080 × 1920 (9:16), 24 fps, AAC audio at 48 kHz, with captions burned in and text kept clear of the bottom of the screen.</p>\n<p><b>(d)</b> <i>SMART objective:</i> 150 students aged 13 to 19 register for the clean-up by the day before the event, three weeks from launch. <i>Strategy 1, TikTok and Reels challenge:</i> two student nano-influencers (one English-speaking, one Kwéyòl-speaking) post 15-second \"litter pick\" clips with #ReefGuardiansSXM. KPI: engagement rate = engagements ÷ reach × 100, target 6%. <i>Strategy 2, school WhatsApp groups:</i> a short message with a sign-up link carrying a UTM tag, shared by student councils. KPI: conversion rate = registrations ÷ link clicks × 100, target 20%. <i>Strategy 3, guerrilla chalk turtle tracks</i> (with permission) leading from the Gros Islet bus stop to a QR poster. KPI: registrations from that QR code ÷ QR scans × 100, target 25%. <i>Consistency:</i> one brand sheet fixes the turtle mascot, the palette, the typefaces and the hashtag, and every asset is made from the same template in English and Kwéyòl. <i>Budget:</i> boosts XCD 400, influencer thank-you fees XCD 300, chalk and QR posters XCD 200, contingency XCD 100.</p>\n<p><b>(e)</b> Filming near the nesting area raises a <b>wildlife and safety</b> issue as well as a consent issue: crews, lights and crowds can disturb nests, and the students filmed are minors. The group should film only in areas approved by the turtle-monitoring volunteers, never at night near nests, and collect signed release forms from parents or guardians for every student who appears. This protects the turtles the campaign exists to save and protects the group legally.</p>","teacherTip":"<p>Challenge version (25 marks: 6 + 5 + 6 + 6 + 2). Check every hex code and contrast claim, and that the frame numbers in (c) are consistent with the stated frame rate (10 seconds at 24 fps is 240 frames). Grade One responses connect the parts: the palette from (b) appears in the redesign in (a), the animation in (c) serves the campaign in (d), and the ethics in (e) come from the scenario rather than a generic list.</p>"}},"preview":true});
(window.COURSE_MODULES = window.COURSE_MODULES || []).push({"id":"M2","code":"Module 2","title":"🔒 Web and Digital Publishing","locked":true,"overview":"<p>Module 2 teaches you to author, design, publish and manage digital content. You will learn the language of digital publishing (ePub, ISBN, metadata, CMS, Kindle Direct Publishing, Google Play Books, Kobo), trace the history of the Internet from ARPANET to Web 3.0, and write for blogs, podcasts, video scripts and social media with a Caribbean voice while respecting copyright and ethical standards. You will plan sites with concept maps, site maps and prototypes, write web-friendly and search-optimised content, produce photo, audio and video for the web, and build working web pages in <b>HTML and CSS</b>. Finally you will evaluate forms of digital publishing and online platforms, describe content management systems including headless CMS, and discuss the trends shaping web publishing. This module follows the ten sections of the DME2 course outline.</p>\n<p>In CAPE Unit 2, <b>Paper 01</b> has 45 multiple-choice items, 15 from each module, worth 20%. <b>Paper 02</b> has three compulsory 15-mark questions, one per module, worth 20% in total and sat without internet access; for Module 2 expect to plan a site structure, correct or write short HTML and CSS, and evaluate publishing platforms. The <b>School-Based Assessment (Paper 031)</b> is worth 60%: in groups of 2 to 3 you design and implement a product or service (preferably local, social or entrepreneurial) and submit a proposal (14 marks), evidence of progress and process (12) and the product (19), including a <b>30-second audio-visual ad</b> (5). The report is limited to 1,500 words. A website, blog or e-zine built in this module is often the core of a strong SBA product.</p>\n<p>Work through the units in order. Keep VS Code (or Notepad) and a browser open while you study the HTML and CSS units, and type every code sample yourself: copying by hand builds the accuracy Paper 02 demands. Use the easier version of an activity if you need a first step, complete each quick check-in honestly and write the short task at the end of each unit. Finish with the Module 2 Test (pass mark 70%) and the Paper 02 practice question.</p><div class=\"pv-lock\"><div class=\"pv-lock-ic\">🔒</div><h3>Unlock Module 2</h3><p>This module is part of the full course. Choose a plan to open every section, the games, past papers, the flipbook and the exam room.</p><p><a class=\"pv-lock-btn\" href=\"../../../index.html#plans\" target=\"_top\">See plans</a></p></div>","objectives":["By the end you will be able to explain digital publishing and related Internet terminologies (ePub, ISBN, metadata, CMS, MMOG) and describe the history of the Internet and the digital revolution.","By the end you will be able to generate original content ideas for digital publishing using blog, podcast, video and audio slideshow writing techniques, storyboards and scripts, with a Caribbean perspective and respect for copyright and ethical standards.","By the end you will be able to create low-fidelity and high-fidelity prototypes, concept maps, navigational charts, site maps and hierarchy maps that apply design principles to layout.","By the end you will be able to create web-friendly, audience-appropriate content guided by a creative brief, plain-language writing, licensing, SEO, machine indexability and accessible media.","By the end you will be able to apply appropriate cameras, shooting principles, and open-source, industry-standard and cloud-based tools to develop photo, audio, podcast and video content for the web.","By the end you will be able to create functional websites using HTML and CSS, including hyperlinks, multimedia, the box model, responsive layout and accessible, usable page design.","By the end you will be able to evaluate the various forms of digital publishing using criteria such as accuracy, authority, purpose, usability, accessibility, risk and security.","By the end you will be able to evaluate the usefulness of online publishing platforms, including free and paid hosted options, blogs, Wikipedia, YouTube and live streaming.","By the end you will be able to describe content management tools and techniques, including self-hosted and cloud CMS, open-source and proprietary systems, CMS types and content workflows.","By the end you will be able to discuss the factors that influence development and trends in web publishing, including emerging innovations and research tools."],"glossary":[],"units":[{"id":"M2U1-x","title":"🔒 Digital publishing and internet terminologies","locked":true,"objectives":["Define digital publishing and explain its purpose for creators, businesses and audiences in the Caribbean.","Describe digital publishing platforms and tools: Amazon Kindle Direct Publishing, Apple Books, Google Play Books, Kobo Writing Life, Barnes &amp; Noble Press (Nook), Wattpad, IngramSpark, Draft2Digital and content management systems.","Explain the eight key features of digital publishing platforms.","Explain interactive e-books, ePub (EPUB 3), PDF, reflowable and fixed layout, ISBN and metadata, and evaluate the advantages and disadvantages of e-books.","Describe the history of the Internet and the digital revolution: ARPANET, TCP/IP, the World Wide Web, Mosaic, Web 1.0, 2.0 and 3.0, broadband and mobile.","Explain accessibility and global adoption, and why massively multiplayer online games (MMOGs) count as a form of digital publishing."],"videos":[],"screens":[{"type":"learn","title":"Part of the full course","html":"<div class=\"pv-lock\"><div class=\"pv-lock-ic\">🔒</div><h3>Unlock this unit</h3><p>This unit is part of the full course. Choose a plan to open every section, the games, past papers, the flipbook and the exam room.</p><p><a class=\"pv-lock-btn\" href=\"../../../index.html#plans\" target=\"_top\">See plans</a></p></div>"}]},{"id":"M2U2-x","title":"🔒 Authoring digital content in a Caribbean context","locked":true,"objectives":["Apply digital media writing techniques for blogs, websites, podcasts, video scripts, audio slideshows and social media.","Generate original content ideas through conceptualisation and creative planning from a brief or real-world problem.","Use storyboards, scripts, mock-ups and wireframes to plan content before production.","Develop creative content that reflects authentic Caribbean stories, voices and cultural context.","Explain copyright law, the Berne Convention and Caribbean intellectual property offices, and apply ethical standards: plagiarism, attribution, fair use and fair dealing, and Creative Commons licences.","Explain cybercrime in relation to digital content and the legal consequences of copyright infringement.","Apply the key techniques: the definition, uses and three-part format of a podcast, the seven video script writing tips and on-camera presentation, and voice tone, pacing and sequencing for audio slideshows."],"videos":[],"screens":[{"type":"learn","title":"Part of the full course","html":"<div class=\"pv-lock\"><div class=\"pv-lock-ic\">🔒</div><h3>Unlock this unit</h3><p>This unit is part of the full course. Choose a plan to open every section, the games, past papers, the flipbook and the exam room.</p><p><a class=\"pv-lock-btn\" href=\"../../../index.html#plans\" target=\"_top\">See plans</a></p></div>"}]},{"id":"M2U3-x","title":"🔒 Prototypes using design principles","locked":true,"objectives":["Define a prototype as an incomplete version of a product taken into user testing, and distinguish low-, mid- and high-fidelity prototypes and wireframes, mock-ups and interactive prototypes.","Follow the design journey from wireframe to mockup to prototype, justify when to use each artifact and which tool (Balsamiq, Figma, Sketch, Adobe XD, Axure RP, Moqups), explain why teams prototype early (new perspective, fresh eyes, stakeholder buy-in) and identify the four types of prototype (feasibility, low-fidelity user, high-fidelity user, live-data).","Explain and create a concept map (\"brainstorming with a purpose\", with linking words), a navigational chart, a site map (HTML sitemap and XML sitemap) and a hierarchy map, and trace user pathways.","Distinguish a sitemap from navigation, and justify the five types of website navigation: horizontal bar, dropdown, hamburger, vertical sidebar and footer.","Apply design principles to layouts: rule of thirds, composition, balance, alignment, hierarchy, contrast, spacing and visual flow.","Compare cloud-based prototyping and authoring tools such as Figma, Canva, Adobe Express, Adobe XD, Balsamiq and Marvel, and choose cloud-based tools for each map (Lucidchart, Miro, Canva whiteboards, FigJam, draw.io, Coggle, MindMeister, Octopus.do, Slickplan, XML-Sitemaps.com).","Build a simple clickable wireframe in Figma using frames, auto layout, components and prototype links."],"videos":[],"screens":[{"type":"learn","title":"Part of the full course","html":"<div class=\"pv-lock\"><div class=\"pv-lock-ic\">🔒</div><h3>Unlock this unit</h3><p>This unit is part of the full course. Choose a plan to open every section, the games, past papers, the flipbook and the exam room.</p><p><a class=\"pv-lock-btn\" href=\"../../../index.html#plans\" target=\"_top\">See plans</a></p></div>"}]},{"id":"M2U4-x","title":"🔒 Content development for online publishing","locked":true,"objectives":["Explain what makes digital content effective (useful, usable and purposeful) and describe the seven-stage content cycle from planning to measuring and maintaining.","Define and research content scope (topic, purpose, audience, depth, format, channels and constraints) using audience research, keyword research, competitor review and a content audit, with accurate, relevant and credible sources.","Prepare a creative brief that includes the target group, a persona, a content checklist and tone of voice.","Apply criteria for web-friendly content: writing for scanners, the inverted pyramid, front-loading, short paragraphs, scannable headings, direct address and plain language, avoiding needless repetition and jargon.","Explain content licensing (proprietary, royalty-free, Creative Commons, public domain, open access) and TASL attribution, and optimise content for search: crawling, indexing, ranking, keywords, metadata and machine indexability.","Prepare images and multimedia for the web (format, size, alt text) and apply UX and accessibility considerations, including WCAG's POUR principles, before reviewing content against a checklist."],"videos":[],"screens":[{"type":"learn","title":"Part of the full course","html":"<div class=\"pv-lock\"><div class=\"pv-lock-ic\">🔒</div><h3>Unlock this unit</h3><p>This unit is part of the full course. Choose a plan to open every section, the games, past papers, the flipbook and the exam room.</p><p><a class=\"pv-lock-btn\" href=\"../../../index.html#plans\" target=\"_top\">See plans</a></p></div>"}]},{"id":"M2U5-x","title":"🔒 Tools and techniques for digital content development","locked":true,"objectives":["Distinguish tools (the hardware and software you use to create) from technique (the methods you apply to capture, edit and tell the story), and make every deliverable sized, formatted, accessible and fit for its platform.","Select cameras and capture devices (mobile phones, handheld, tablet and mounted devices, plus microphones) for web content.","Apply shooting principles for the web: rule of thirds, composition, framing, depth of field, camera angles and lighting.","Compare open-source (GIMP, Inkscape, Audacity, Kdenlive, OBS, Blender), industry-standard (Adobe suite, Final Cut Pro, DaVinci Resolve) and cloud-based tools (Canva, CapCut, Figma, WeVideo, Google Workspace), and plan for disruption by editing locally and backing up to the cloud.","Apply best practice in photo editing (protect, crop and correct, prepare and export; adding text, changing backgrounds, colour correction) and audio editing (clean recording, multiple tracks, noise reduction, mastering), including creating a podcast step by step.","Apply the production techniques: the four stages of launching a podcast (concept, equipment, record and edit, publish and promote) and producing an audio slideshow with voice-first or storyboard-first workflows.","Edit video for the web (hook, purposeful cuts, J-cuts and L-cuts), prepare it with captions and accessibility features, apply navigation such as breadcrumbs and YouTube chapters, and export in suitable web formats."],"videos":[],"screens":[{"type":"learn","title":"Part of the full course","html":"<div class=\"pv-lock\"><div class=\"pv-lock-ic\">🔒</div><h3>Unlock this unit</h3><p>This unit is part of the full course. Choose a plan to open every section, the games, past papers, the flipbook and the exam room.</p><p><a class=\"pv-lock-btn\" href=\"../../../index.html#plans\" target=\"_top\">See plans</a></p></div>"}]},{"id":"M2U6-x","title":"🔒 Website building with HTML and CSS","locked":true,"objectives":["Distinguish the roles of HTML (structure), CSS (presentation) and JavaScript (behaviour), then write a valid, tidily nested HTML document using doctype, head and body, headings, paragraphs, lists, tables, images with alt text and semantic tags (header, nav, main, section, footer).","Create internal, external and anchor hyperlinks, and open external links safely in a new tab with target=\"_blank\" and rel=\"noopener\".","Style pages with CSS selectors, properties and values, applying the box model, typography, colour (hex and rgb), backgrounds, borders, spacing and layout with flexbox and a simple grid; distinguish external, internal and inline CSS.","Embed images, video, audio and external widgets such as YouTube videos and Google Maps, and explain print versus screen resolution (dpi, px, rem) and responsive media with media queries.","Plan a multi-page site architecture from wireframes and mock-ups, and apply page design principles (contrast, grid systems, legibility, link styling) with UI and UX, responsiveness, WCAG accessibility and usability.","Build and test a mobile-first, one-page business site for Front Street, Philipsburg, with semantic HTML, an external style sheet, a Flexbox row and a media query, checked in browser developer tools (F12) at phone, tablet and desktop widths."],"videos":[],"screens":[{"type":"learn","title":"Part of the full course","html":"<div class=\"pv-lock\"><div class=\"pv-lock-ic\">🔒</div><h3>Unlock this unit</h3><p>This unit is part of the full course. Choose a plan to open every section, the games, past papers, the flipbook and the exam room.</p><p><a class=\"pv-lock-btn\" href=\"../../../index.html#plans\" target=\"_top\">See plans</a></p></div>"}]},{"id":"M2U7-x","title":"🔒 Website infrastructure, data handling and security","locked":true,"objectives":["Explain how domain names, URLs and DNS work, including Caribbean country-code top-level domains such as .sx, .tt and .jm.","Compare hosting types (shared, VPS, cloud and free static hosts such as GitHub Pages and Netlify) and explain the roles of back-end systems, databases and content management tools.","Explain how websites collect and store data through forms, and apply privacy, consent, data storage and permission principles.","Describe basic website security threats (phishing, malware, weak passwords, outdated plugins) and protections such as strong passwords, 2FA, HTTPS/SSL, updates and backups.","Repeat a hand-coded build in a website builder (Weebly, Google Sites, Wix) and evaluate the two approaches."],"videos":[],"screens":[{"type":"learn","title":"Part of the full course","html":"<div class=\"pv-lock\"><div class=\"pv-lock-ic\">🔒</div><h3>Unlock this unit</h3><p>This unit is part of the full course. Choose a plan to open every section, the games, past papers, the flipbook and the exam room.</p><p><a class=\"pv-lock-btn\" href=\"../../../index.html#plans\" target=\"_top\">See plans</a></p></div>"}]},{"id":"M2U8-x","title":"🔒 Forms of digital publishing","locked":true,"objectives":["Describe the main forms of digital publishing: websites, blogs, social networking platforms, games, apps, podcasts, videos, e-books, newsletters and digital magazines.","Classify forms of digital publishing by purpose, audience, platform, content type and level of interactivity.","Define websites, blogs, social networking, games, apps, podcasts, videos and e-books, and evaluate them using accuracy, authority, usability, accessibility, satisfaction, purpose, and risk and security concerns, plus audience engagement and privacy.","Compare the Caribbean content available in each form of digital publishing.","Choose and justify the right form of digital publishing for a given audience and purpose."],"videos":[],"screens":[{"type":"learn","title":"Part of the full course","html":"<div class=\"pv-lock\"><div class=\"pv-lock-ic\">🔒</div><h3>Unlock this unit</h3><p>This unit is part of the full course. Choose a plan to open every section, the games, past papers, the flipbook and the exam room.</p><p><a class=\"pv-lock-btn\" href=\"../../../index.html#plans\" target=\"_top\">See plans</a></p></div>"}]},{"id":"M2U9-x","title":"🔒 Online publishing platforms","locked":true,"objectives":["Distinguish between hosted free and paid online publishing platforms, and between hosted and self-hosted options, using WordPress.com, Blogger, Medium, self-hosted WordPress, Squarespace and Wix.","Use online platforms to publish content: blogs (frequency, SEO, community), Wikipedia (neutrality, verifiability, notability, collaborative authorship), YouTube (upload settings, thumbnails, metadata, monetisation) and live streaming.","Explain the setup and best practice for live streaming on Facebook Live, Instagram Live, Twitch and TikTok Live.","Evaluate the benefits and limitations of online platforms: cost, ease of use, reach, ownership, accessibility, analytics, monetisation, platform rules, privacy, security, copyright and content control.","Describe how content on online platforms is maintained over time."],"videos":[],"screens":[{"type":"learn","title":"Part of the full course","html":"<div class=\"pv-lock\"><div class=\"pv-lock-ic\">🔒</div><h3>Unlock this unit</h3><p>This unit is part of the full course. Choose a plan to open every section, the games, past papers, the flipbook and the exam room.</p><p><a class=\"pv-lock-btn\" href=\"../../../index.html#plans\" target=\"_top\">See plans</a></p></div>"}]},{"id":"M2U10-x","title":"🔒 Content management and trends in web publishing","locked":true,"objectives":["Define content management (CM), explain why content is stored once in a repository for reuse, and explain the two core parts of a CMS: the Content Management Application (CMA) and the Content Delivery Application (CDA).","Distinguish among Enterprise Content Management (ECM), Document Management Software (DMS), Web Content Management (WCM), Mobile Content Management (MCM), headless CMS and Digital Asset Management (DAM), including the five stages of ECM.","Explain how a headless CMS decouples content from presentation and the role of an Application Programming Interface (API) in multi-channel content delivery.","Compare open source, proprietary and SaaS content management systems, partial and fully cloud CMS, and self-hosted and third-party hosted CMS.","Identify common CMS features and describe content management techniques: creating, editing, organising, tagging, scheduling, approving, publishing, archiving and updating.","Discuss the factors influencing trends in web publishing (commoditisation, pervasiveness of technology, affordability, accessibility, adoption, politics, empowerment) and their impact on creation, design, distribution, user experience and engagement.","Discuss current trends and developments in digital publishing and use research tools to discover and track them."],"videos":[],"screens":[{"type":"learn","title":"Part of the full course","html":"<div class=\"pv-lock\"><div class=\"pv-lock-ic\">🔒</div><h3>Unlock this unit</h3><p>This unit is part of the full course. Choose a plan to open every section, the games, past papers, the flipbook and the exam room.</p><p><a class=\"pv-lock-btn\" href=\"../../../index.html#plans\" target=\"_top\">See plans</a></p></div>"}]}],"test":{"title":"Module 2 test","pass":70,"items":[]},"paper2":{"type":"learn","title":"Paper 02 practice","html":"<div class=\"pv-lock\"><div class=\"pv-lock-ic\">🔒</div><h3>Paper 02 practice</h3><p>Paper 02 practice is part of the full course. Choose a plan to open every section, the games, past papers, the flipbook and the exam room.</p><p><a class=\"pv-lock-btn\" href=\"../../../index.html#plans\" target=\"_top\">See plans</a></p></div>"}});
(window.COURSE_MODULES = window.COURSE_MODULES || []).push({"id":"M3","code":"Module 3","title":"🔒 Mobile App Development","locked":true,"overview":"<p>Module 3 takes you into the mobile world that most Caribbean people now carry in their pockets. You will map the mobile ecosystem (content, infrastructure, devices, users, carriers, developers and the enabling environment), distinguish Android, iOS and other platforms, smart devices and the Internet of Things, and contrast the Caribbean mobile ecosystem with the global picture in statistics, infrastructure and social impact. You will examine why local apps matter, analyse the factors that shape the mobile user experience, and apply real tools and techniques, from conceptualisation, wireframing and prototyping in Figma to building with MIT App Inventor, Thunkable, Android Studio or Xcode, and testing. You will finish by planning how to distribute and market an app, choosing a revenue model, and discussing the trends shaping mobile technology. This module follows the nine sections of the DME2 course outline.</p>\n<p>In CAPE Unit 2, <b>Paper 01</b> has 45 multiple-choice items, 15 from each module, worth 20%. <b>Paper 02</b> has three compulsory 15-mark questions, one per module, worth 20% in total and sat without internet access; for Module 3 expect to develop an app concept, explain user experience factors, sketch a wireframe, and recommend revenue and marketing strategies. The <b>School-Based Assessment (Paper 031)</b> is worth 60%: in groups of 2 to 3 you design and implement a product or service (preferably for a local social or entrepreneurial need), submitting a proposal (14 marks), progress and process evidence (12) and the product (19), including a <b>30-second audio-visual short ad</b> (5). The report is limited to 1,500 words. A mobile app prototype that solves a real Caribbean problem makes an excellent SBA product.</p>\n<p>Work through the units in order. Keep a notebook for wireframe sketches and install a free tool such as Figma, MIT App Inventor or Thunkable so you can try each technique yourself. Use the easier version of an activity if you need a first step, complete each quick check-in honestly and write the short task at the end of each unit. Finish with the Module 3 Test (pass mark 70%) and the Paper 02 practice question.</p><div class=\"pv-lock\"><div class=\"pv-lock-ic\">🔒</div><h3>Unlock Module 3</h3><p>This module is part of the full course. Choose a plan to open every section, the games, past papers, the flipbook and the exam room.</p><p><a class=\"pv-lock-btn\" href=\"../../../index.html#plans\" target=\"_top\">See plans</a></p></div>","objectives":["By the end you will be able to discuss the relationships and interdependencies among content, infrastructure, devices, users, carriers, developers and the enabling environment within the mobile ecosystem.","By the end you will be able to distinguish among mobile operating systems (Android, iOS, HarmonyOS), platforms, smart and wearable devices, the Internet of Things and cross-platform development tools.","By the end you will be able to contrast the Caribbean mobile ecosystem with the global ecosystem across statistics, social and economic impact, applications, content production, infrastructure and policy.","By the end you will be able to examine the benefits of developing mobile applications that address local and regional Caribbean needs.","By the end you will be able to describe the factors that impact the mobile user experience and explain design strategies to address them.","By the end you will be able to apply tools and techniques to conceptualise, wireframe, prototype, build and test a mobile application.","By the end you will be able to describe approaches to distributing and marketing mobile applications, including app store optimisation and the user journey.","By the end you will be able to discuss commercial, open-source and civic revenue models for mobile applications and evaluate which suits a given app and audience.","By the end you will be able to discuss the factors that influence development and trends in the mobile ecosystem, with attention to Caribbean implications."],"glossary":[],"units":[{"id":"M3U1-x","title":"🔒 The mobile ecosystem","locked":true,"objectives":["Identify and explain the four core components of the mobile ecosystem: content, infrastructure, devices and users.","Explain the role of carriers and telcos such as TelEm, Digicel, Flow (Liberty Latin America) and UTS (Chippie) in data delivery and connectivity.","Describe mobile consumer behaviour, expectations and usage patterns in the Caribbean.","Explain the enabling environment: policy frameworks, regulators (BTP, ECTEL, OUR, TATT) and developer communities.","Discuss the relationships and interdependence among ecosystem players, using Caribbean examples.","Evaluate connectivity challenges and local opportunities in the Caribbean mobile ecosystem."],"videos":[],"screens":[{"type":"learn","title":"Part of the full course","html":"<div class=\"pv-lock\"><div class=\"pv-lock-ic\">🔒</div><h3>Unlock this unit</h3><p>This unit is part of the full course. Choose a plan to open every section, the games, past papers, the flipbook and the exam room.</p><p><a class=\"pv-lock-btn\" href=\"../../../index.html#plans\" target=\"_top\">See plans</a></p></div>"}]},{"id":"M3U2-x","title":"🔒 Mobile operating systems, platforms and devices","locked":true,"objectives":["Distinguish among mobile operating systems: Android (open source, Google ecosystem, fragmentation), iOS (closed ecosystem, security, design philosophy) and HarmonyOS.","Compare Android and iOS development environments, app stores and revenue models.","Describe smart devices and wearables: smartphones, tablets, smartwatches and smart speakers.","Explain the Internet of Things (IoT), sensors and embedded systems, with Caribbean examples.","Explain cross-platform development tools such as Flutter, React Native and .NET MAUI.","Justify the choice of platform for an app using target audience, budget and time-to-market."],"videos":[],"screens":[{"type":"learn","title":"Part of the full course","html":"<div class=\"pv-lock\"><div class=\"pv-lock-ic\">🔒</div><h3>Unlock this unit</h3><p>This unit is part of the full course. Choose a plan to open every section, the games, past papers, the flipbook and the exam room.</p><p><a class=\"pv-lock-btn\" href=\"../../../index.html#plans\" target=\"_top\">See plans</a></p></div>"}]},{"id":"M3U3-x","title":"🔒 Caribbean context vs the international mobile ecosystem","locked":true,"objectives":["Contrast regional and international statistics on smartphone penetration, mobile Internet use, connectivity and app downloads, quoting sources and years.","Explain the social and economic impact of mobile technology: mobile banking and mobile money, e-government and digital health.","Describe applications of mobile technology in agriculture, education, tourism and disaster management, including Hurricane Irma recovery.","Analyse the imbalance between content consumption and content production in the Caribbean, and the role of digital content creation.","Contrast mobile and broadband infrastructure: submarine cables, 4G and 5G rollout and connectivity gaps.","Discuss policy, security and ethical considerations, and how mobile connects the Caribbean diaspora."],"videos":[],"screens":[{"type":"learn","title":"Part of the full course","html":"<div class=\"pv-lock\"><div class=\"pv-lock-ic\">🔒</div><h3>Unlock this unit</h3><p>This unit is part of the full course. Choose a plan to open every section, the games, past papers, the flipbook and the exam room.</p><p><a class=\"pv-lock-btn\" href=\"../../../index.html#plans\" target=\"_top\">See plans</a></p></div>"}]},{"id":"M3U4-x","title":"🔒 Benefits of developing local mobile applications","locked":true,"objectives":["Identify local and regional needs that mobile apps can address, including school services, transport, health, agriculture, tourism, emergency services, heritage, culture and small businesses.","Explain how universal app types (bus schedules, restaurant reviews, hotel rankings, popular destinations) become more useful when they are built with local data and local knowledge.","Explain the social and cultural linkages that local apps create through entertainment, news, history and cultural preservation.","Discuss the economic benefits of local app development: job creation, regional entrepreneurship and export potential.","Examine real Caribbean-developed apps and social impact apps for disaster preparedness, community health and charity fundraising."],"videos":[],"screens":[{"type":"learn","title":"Part of the full course","html":"<div class=\"pv-lock\"><div class=\"pv-lock-ic\">🔒</div><h3>Unlock this unit</h3><p>This unit is part of the full course. Choose a plan to open every section, the games, past papers, the flipbook and the exam room.</p><p><a class=\"pv-lock-btn\" href=\"../../../index.html#plans\" target=\"_top\">See plans</a></p></div>"}]},{"id":"M3U5-x","title":"🔒 Factors that impact the mobile user experience","locked":true,"objectives":["Describe how screen size, resolution and the touch interface affect the mobile user experience, including touch targets and readability.","Describe how bandwidth, connectivity and data usage affect users, and explain offline-first and low-data design strategies.","Describe how portability and context of use, and device performance (processing power, storage and battery life), affect app design.","Explain accessibility and inclusive design, including screen readers (TalkBack, VoiceOver), voice control, contrast and captions.","Describe how the physical environment (lighting, noise, movement, location) and the user profile (age, ability, digital skills, language, culture, needs) shape design decisions.","Recommend and justify a design strategy for each factor."],"videos":[],"screens":[{"type":"learn","title":"Part of the full course","html":"<div class=\"pv-lock\"><div class=\"pv-lock-ic\">🔒</div><h3>Unlock this unit</h3><p>This unit is part of the full course. Choose a plan to open every section, the games, past papers, the flipbook and the exam room.</p><p><a class=\"pv-lock-btn\" href=\"../../../index.html#plans\" target=\"_top\">See plans</a></p></div>"}]},{"id":"M3U6-x","title":"🔒 Tools and techniques to create a mobile application","locked":true,"objectives":["Conceptualise a mobile app by defining its purpose, target users, core features and minimum viable product (MVP).","Create low- and high-fidelity wireframes on paper and in Figma or Balsamiq, map user flows and build a clickable prototype.","Distinguish between native development tools (Android Studio with Kotlin or Java and the Android SDK; Xcode with Swift and the iOS SDK), cross-platform tools, and no-code or low-code builders (MIT App Inventor, Thunkable, Glide, Adalo).","Build a simple working app step by step in MIT App Inventor using the Designer, the Blocks editor and the AI Companion.","Use online development resources and communities (Stack Overflow, GitHub, official documentation) responsibly.","Plan basic testing, debugging, feedback and refinement, including unit testing, user acceptance testing and beta releases (TestFlight, Google Play testing tracks)."],"videos":[],"screens":[{"type":"learn","title":"Part of the full course","html":"<div class=\"pv-lock\"><div class=\"pv-lock-ic\">🔒</div><h3>Unlock this unit</h3><p>This unit is part of the full course. Choose a plan to open every section, the games, past papers, the flipbook and the exam room.</p><p><a class=\"pv-lock-btn\" href=\"../../../index.html#plans\" target=\"_top\">See plans</a></p></div>"}]},{"id":"M3U7-x","title":"🔒 Marketing and distributing mobile applications","locked":true,"objectives":["Describe app distribution models: app stores (Google Play, Apple App Store), direct download and sideloading, enterprise distribution and web-based access.","Explain the stages of the user journey: discover, test, review, purchase, download, update and retain.","Apply App Store Optimisation (ASO): title, description, keywords, icon, screenshots, ratings and reviews.","Describe marketing approaches: social media, websites, email, influencers, search, paid advertising, public relations, press releases, review sites and community outreach.","Plan beta testing and pre-launch community building for a Caribbean app."],"videos":[],"screens":[{"type":"learn","title":"Part of the full course","html":"<div class=\"pv-lock\"><div class=\"pv-lock-ic\">🔒</div><h3>Unlock this unit</h3><p>This unit is part of the full course. Choose a plan to open every section, the games, past papers, the flipbook and the exam room.</p><p><a class=\"pv-lock-btn\" href=\"../../../index.html#plans\" target=\"_top\">See plans</a></p></div>"}]},{"id":"M3U8-x","title":"🔒 Revenue models for mobile applications","locked":true,"objectives":["Discuss commercial revenue models: paid apps, freemium, in-app purchases, in-app advertising (banner, interstitial, rewarded video) and subscriptions.","Explain developer fees, app licensing and white-label apps as sources of income for developers.","Describe open-source and community-supported models: crowdfunding, grants, donations and sponsorship, with examples such as Wikipedia and Signal.","Discuss civic, charity and social good apps for disaster preparedness, health, safety, fundraising, education and public services.","Evaluate and justify a revenue model for a Caribbean app using user value, privacy, accessibility, ethics and long-term sustainability."],"videos":[],"screens":[{"type":"learn","title":"Part of the full course","html":"<div class=\"pv-lock\"><div class=\"pv-lock-ic\">🔒</div><h3>Unlock this unit</h3><p>This unit is part of the full course. Choose a plan to open every section, the games, past papers, the flipbook and the exam room.</p><p><a class=\"pv-lock-btn\" href=\"../../../index.html#plans\" target=\"_top\">See plans</a></p></div>"}]},{"id":"M3U9-x","title":"🔒 Factors influencing trends in the mobile ecosystem","locked":true,"objectives":["Explain the factors that drive mobile trends: commoditisation, technology access (pervasiveness), affordability, accessibility, adoption, user behaviour and empowerment.","Describe emerging trends: AI integration, mobile payments, 5G and edge computing, IoT, AR and VR, wearables, foldables, PWAs, low-code/no-code, digital identity, super apps and app-based services.","Discuss the impact of cybersecurity, privacy, data regulation (GDPR and regional data protection laws) and mobile accessibility on app development.","Discuss Caribbean trends, including mobile money, digital identity and e-government services, and their implications for the region.","Use research tools and methods (Statista, data.ai, Sensor Tower, Google Trends, GSMA Intelligence) to discover, monitor and evaluate mobile trends."],"videos":[],"screens":[{"type":"learn","title":"Part of the full course","html":"<div class=\"pv-lock\"><div class=\"pv-lock-ic\">🔒</div><h3>Unlock this unit</h3><p>This unit is part of the full course. Choose a plan to open every section, the games, past papers, the flipbook and the exam room.</p><p><a class=\"pv-lock-btn\" href=\"../../../index.html#plans\" target=\"_top\">See plans</a></p></div>"}]}],"test":{"title":"Module 3 test","pass":70,"items":[]},"paper2":{"type":"learn","title":"Paper 02 practice","html":"<div class=\"pv-lock\"><div class=\"pv-lock-ic\">🔒</div><h3>Paper 02 practice</h3><p>Paper 02 practice is part of the full course. Choose a plan to open every section, the games, past papers, the flipbook and the exam room.</p><p><a class=\"pv-lock-btn\" href=\"../../../index.html#plans\" target=\"_top\">See plans</a></p></div>"}});